hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"5401","5766 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me that Suhayl ibn Abi Salih informed him, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell gold for gold, nor silver (wariq) for silver, except weight for weight, like for like, equal for equal."
"5402","5767 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dawud, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dirham for dirham, no increase; dinar for dinar; do not give preponderance of some over some, nor sell what is absent of them for what is present."
"5403","5768 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Men of knowledge informed me, among them Malik ibn Anas, that Nafi', freedman of Ibn Umar, narrated to them, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5404","5769 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abd al-Majid ibn Suhayl, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri and from Abu Hurayrah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed a man over Khaybar, and he brought him choice dates (janib). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Is all the dates of Khaybar like this? He said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we take a sa' of this for two sa's, and two sa's for three. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not do it; sell the mixed dates (jam') for dirhams, then buy janib with the dirhams."
"5405","5770 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur al-Razi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Hunayn: A man of Iraq said to Abdullah ibn Umar: Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said, while he was governor over us: Whoever gives a dirham for a hundred dirhams, let him take it. Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gold for gold, weight for weight, like for like; whoever gives more, it is riba. And Ibn Umar said: If you are in doubt, ask Abu Sa'id al-Khudri about that. So he asked him, and he informed him he heard that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). What Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said was conveyed to Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), so he sought his Lord's forgiveness and said: It is only an opinion of mine."
"5406","5771 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from al-Taymi, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id: A man brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dates he disapproved of, and said: How did you get this? He said: I bought it for two sa's of dates. He said: You doubled, you committed riba - or: you committed riba, you doubled."
"5407","5772 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a sa' of fresh juicy dates (rayyan), while the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dates were dry unirrigated (ba'l). He said: How did you get this? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we sold two sa's of dates for a sa' of this. He said: Do not do it; rather sell your dates, and buy of this."
"5408","5773 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dinar for dinar, dirham for dirham, a sa' of dates for a sa' of dates, a sa' of wheat for a sa' of wheat, a sa' of barley for a sa' of barley, with no disparity in any of that."
"5409","5774 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, he said: Uqbah ibn Abd al-Ghafir narrated to me, he said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri narrated to me, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No sa' of dates for two sa's, no wheat for two sa's, no dirham for two dirhams."
"5410","5775 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar informed us, he said: Isra'il informed me, from Abu Ishaq, from Masruq, from Bilal, he said: I had dates belonging to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I found better than it, a sa' for two sa's, so I bought it and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Where did you get this, O Bilal? I said: I bought it, a sa' for two sa's. He said: Return it, and return our dates to us."
"5411","5776 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed me, from Amir ibn Yahya and Khalid ibn Abi Imran, from Hanash ibn Abdullah al-Saba'i, from Fudalah ibn Ubayd, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Khaybar, trading with the Jews, an ounce of gold for two or three dinars. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell gold for gold except weight for weight."
"5412","5777 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Ubadah and Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, i.e. from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to sell silver for silver and gold for gold except like for like, and ordered us to sell gold for silver and silver for gold as we wish."
"5413","5778 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Nafi' ibn Yazid informed us, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Sulayman, freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Hassan al-Najibi, informed us that he heard Hanash al-San'ani narrating from Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit in the expedition of people toward the West, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the expedition of Khaybar: It reached me that you trade a mithqal for a half and two-thirds, and it is only valid mithqal for mithqal, weight for weight."
"5414","5779 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: I heard Malik say: Musa ibn Abi Tamim narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Bashshar, from Abu Hurayrah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dinar for dinar, with no disparity between them; dirham for dirham, with no disparity between them."
"5415","5780 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi Tamim, and he mentioned with its chain the same. Abu Ja'far said: So it is established by these successive reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade selling silver for silver and gold for gold with disparity, and likewise all measured things mentioned in these reports we narrated. Acting upon them is worthier for us than acting upon the hadith of Usamah, whose interpretation may be as we mentioned in this chapter. Then his Companions after him also held in that what was successively reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5416","5781 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb informed us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Jabalah ibn Suhaym, he said: I heard Ibn Umar say: Umar addressed and said: Let none of you buy a dinar for two dinars, nor a dirham for two dirhams, nor a qafiz for two qafizs; I fear riba (al-rama') for you, and I will not be brought one who does it but I will pain him with punishment in his person and his wealth."
"5417","5782 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from al-Ash'ath, from his father, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar said: Let none of you take a dirham for two dirhams, for I fear riba (al-rama') for you."
"5418","5783 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb informed us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Nafi' say: Ibn Umar narrated to me, he said: Umar addressed and said: Do not sell gold for gold, nor silver (wariq) for silver, except like for like; do not give preponderance of some over some; I fear riba (al-rama') for you."
"5419","5784 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), the same. Abu Ja'far said: So this is Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) addressing with this upon the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the presence of his Companions (may Allah's pleasure be upon them), with none of them objecting to him; so that proves their agreement with him upon it. Then it was also narrated in that from Abu Bakr and Ali and others among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what agrees with that as well."
"5420","5785 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, from Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth, from Musa ibn Ulayy, he narrated to him from his father, from Abu Qays, freedman of Amr ibn al-As, he said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq wrote to the commanders of the armies when he came to Syria: As for after, you have descended upon the land of riba, so do not trade gold for gold except weight for weight, nor silver (wariq) for silver except weight for weight, nor food for food except measure for measure. Abu Qays said: I read his letter."
"5421","5786 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from al-Mughirah ibn Miqsam, from his father, from Abu Salih al-Samman, he said: I was sitting with Ali ibn Abi Talib when a man came to him and said: I have dirhams which are not spent for my need, so I buy with them dirhams that pass for me, adding something therein. Ali said: Buy gold with your dirhams, then buy silver (wariq) with your gold, then spend it as you wish."
"5422","5787 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hammad, from Abu Salih, from Shurayh, from Umar, he said: Dirham for dirham; the excess between them is riba. Abu Nu'aym said: One of our companions said, from Sufyan: Dirham for dirham. Husayn said: Ahmad ibn Salih said to me: Abu Salih, imam of Hammad's mosque."
"5423","5788 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Salim ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: Umar and Abdullah ibn Umar used to forbid selling two dirhams for a dirham, hand to hand, and they would say: Dirham for dirham, and dinar for dinar."
"5424","5789 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb recited to me: Musa ibn Ulayy narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Mansur, from Abu Rafi', he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab passed by me with silver (wariq), and said: Make for us anklets (awdah) for a boy of ours. I said: O Commander of the Believers, I have ready-made anklets; if you wish, I take the silver and you take the anklets. Umar said: Like for like? I said: Yes. So he placed the silver in one pan of the scale and the anklets in the other pan; when the scale balanced, he took with one hand and gave with the other."
"5425","5790 - Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri' narrated to us, from Qabath ibn Razin, he said: Ulayy ibn Rabah, i.e. al-Lakhmi, narrated to me, he said: We were in an expedition with Fudalah ibn Ubayd, and I asked him about selling gold for gold. He said: Like for like, with no disparity between them."
"5426","5791 - And among what was narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding his withdrawal on exchange is what Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu al-Sahba': Ibn Abbas withdrew from exchange (sarf). So this is Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) - who narrated from Usamah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Riba is only in deferment - interpreting it as permitting silver for silver and gold for gold, two for one and more, having retracted that view. Either his retraction was because he knew what Usamah (may Allah be pleased with him) had narrated was the riba of the Qur'an, and he knew the riba of deferment is other than that, or there reached him what contradicts Usamah's hadith (may Allah be pleased with him) - which Usamah's hadith lacked - from the multitude who transmitted to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until proof was established upon him, and that was not in Usamah's hadith (may Allah be pleased with him) because it is a lone report, so he returned to what the group brought, by whose transmission proof is established, and left what the lone narrator brought, who may be subject to error, mistake and heedlessness. And what we explained on exchange is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah's mercy be upon them all)."
"5427","Chapter 2: A necklace sold for gold, containing beads and gold. 5792 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Layth ibn Sa'd, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran, from Hanash al-San'ani, from Fudalah ibn Ubaydullah, he said: I acquired on the day of Khaybar a necklace containing gold and beads, and I wanted to sell it, so I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. He said: Separate part of it from part, then sell it as you wish."
"5428","5793 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: Abu Shuja' Sa'id ibn Yazid al-Himyari narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran, from Fudalah ibn Ubayd, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I bought on the day of Khaybar a necklace containing gold and beads for twelve dinars; I separated it and the gold was more than twelve dinars. I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: It is not sold until you separate it."
"5429","5794 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Yazid, he said: I heard Khalid ibn Abi Imran narrating from Hanash, from Fudalah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought on the day of Khaybar a necklace containing beads strung with gold; a man had bought it for seven or nine. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he said: No, until you distinguish between them. He said: I only meant the stones. He said: No, until you distinguish between them; so he rejected it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group held that a necklace as we described may not be sold for gold, because that price, being gold, is divided over the value of the beads and over the gold, so each becomes sold for its share of the price, like two commodities sold for gold, each sold for its share of that gold's value. They said: Since what falls to the necklace's gold is determined with the beads by conjecture, and gold may only be sold for gold like for like, the sale is invalid unless it is known that the price of the necklace's gold equals its weight in the gold it was bought with. That is not known by dividing the price; it is only known by it being separate after ascertaining its weight, which cannot be ascertained until separated from the necklace. They said: So this necklace may not be sold for gold until its gold is separated from it, for what we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and our reasoning. Others opposed them and said: If the amount of its gold is unknown - whether it equals the whole price's weight, less or more - except by separating the necklace and weighing that gold to know its weight, it may not be sold for gold until its gold is separated and known to be less than that price. But if knowledge encompasses the weight of its gold, and it is known to be less than the gold it was sold for - or its weight is not encompassed by knowledge, yet it is truly known to be less than the price the necklace was sold for, being gold - the sale is permitted. For its gold is then for its weight's like in the price gold, and its beads for the price's remainder, with no need for it as in goods sold for one price. Proof is that we see gold may not be sold for gold like for like (with disparity), yet they agree two dinars, one superior in quality to the other, sold in one transaction for two equal-quality dinars, or for good unminted gold, is a valid sale. Had that been referred to value as non-gold non-silver goods sold for one price are, the sale would be void, for the inferior dinar would take less than its weight since its value is less than the other dinar's. Since that sale's validity is agreed upon, and the Sunnah is established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that gold, its dust and its coins, are alike, it is established that gold's ruling in sale for gold is not upon division over values, but is specific with a ruling apart from other goods sold in one transaction; what falls to it of the price is its weight, not its value's share. This is what reasoning testifies for this view. And the hadith of Fudalah we mentioned became confused for us: some narrated it as we mentioned at this chapter's start, others narrated it otherwise."
"5430","5795 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani' narrated to me that he heard Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi say: I heard Fudalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while at Khaybar, was brought a necklace containing gold and beads, from the spoils being sold. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the gold in the necklace to be removed alone, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gold for gold, weight for weight."
"5431","5796 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Humayd ibn Hani' narrated to us, from Fudalah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he did not say ""at Khaybar."""
"5432","5797 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri' narrated to us, he said: Haywah narrated to us from Abu Hani', and he mentioned with its chain the same. In this hadith is other than in the first hadith: here the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) removed the gold and placed it separately, then said: Gold for gold, weight for weight, so people know how the ruling of gold for gold is. It may be the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) separated the gold because the Muslims' welfare lay in that, doing what benefited them - not because selling gold before removal, with other than it, in one transaction, is impermissible. This contradicts what those narrated who narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not sold until separated. And others narrated it contrarily as well."
"5433","5798 - So Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Hanash ibn Abdullah al-San'ani narrated to me that he was at sea with Fudalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari. Hanash said: I bought a necklace containing gold dust (tibr), ruby and peridot, and I came to Fudalah ibn Ubayd and mentioned that to him. He said: Do not take gold dust for gold dust except like for like, for I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Khaybar, and I bought a necklace for seven dinars containing gold dust and jewels, and I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not take gold dust for gold except like for like. In this hadith is other than the preceding hadiths: what Fudalah narrated therein from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is gold dust for gold, like for like, without mentioning invalidity of the sale of the necklace sold for that though it contained gold and other than it. This contradicts the first hadiths."
"5434","5799 - And others narrated it otherwise as well: Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Qurrah ibn Abd al-Rahman and Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Amir ibn Yahya al-Ma'afiri informed them both, from Hanash, that he said: We were with Fudalah ibn Ubayd in an expedition, and a necklace containing gold, silver and jewels fell to me and my companions, and I wanted to buy it, so I asked Fudalah. He said: Remove its gold, place it in the pan, place gold in the other pan, then take only like for like, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him take only like for like. This contradicts the preceding hadiths, for it contains Fudalah's order to remove the gold and sell it alone, without attributing that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); what he attributed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his prohibition of selling gold for gold except weight for weight. This is undisputed, while the order to separate is from Fudalah's (may Allah be pleased with him) statement. He may have ordered that holding the sale therein for gold impermissible until separated. Or he may have ordered that because he knew that necklace's gold amount and measure cannot be known until separated from it. So this hadith became confused, and what was intended by it could not be ascertained. No one may argue with one of the meanings narrated upon it but his opponent argues against him with the other meaning. We already presented in this chapter how reasoning bears upon it: upon what those held who made the ruling of gold sold with other than it for gold not upon dividing the price over values, but that gold is sold for its weight in the price gold, and the remainder for the price's remainder. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah's mercy be upon them all)."
"5435","5800 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed me, from Abdullah ibn Hubayrah al-Saba'i, from Abu Tamim al-Jayshani, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan bought a necklace containing gold dust, peridot, pearls and ruby for six hundred dinars. Ubadah ibn al-Samit stood when Mu'awiyah ascended the pulpit, or when he prayed Zuhr, and said: Lo, Mu'awiyah bought riba and consumed it; lo, he is in the Fire up to his throat. It may be that necklace contained more gold than what it was bought with, and Ubadah's reaction was for that. Or it may have been sold on credit, for it was narrated of Mu'awiyah that he saw no harm in that. And there was narrated in that, and in the cause for which Ubadah (may Allah be pleased with him) objected to Mu'awiyah as he did, what (follows)."
"5436","5801 - What Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid informed us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ash'ath, he said: We were in an expedition under Mu'awiyah, and we acquired gold and silver, so Mu'awiyah ordered a man to sell it to people against their stipends. People disputed over it, so Ubadah stood and forbade them, and they returned it; the man came to Mu'awiyah complaining to him. Mu'awiyah stood addressing and said: What is with men narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hadiths, lying about him therein, which we never heard? Ubadah stood and said: By Allah, we shall narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) though Mu'awiyah dislikes it: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell gold for gold, nor silver for silver, nor wheat for wheat, nor barley for barley, nor dates for dates, nor salt for salt, except equal for equal, hand to hand, identical for identical."
"5437","5802 - Isma'il ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani, that he said: People came in Mu'awiyah's governorship selling gold and silver vessels against the stipend. Ubadah ibn al-Samit stood and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling gilded (vessels) for gold, and silver for silver, wheat for wheat, dates for dates, barley for barley, and salt for salt, except like for like, equal for equal; whoever gives more or takes more has committed riba. Abu Ja'far said: So that proves Ubadah's (may Allah be pleased with him) objection to Mu'awiyah was selling gold for gold to a term, nothing else. As for the necklace containing gold sold for gold, or the necklace containing silver sold for silver, what we narrated from him gives no proof of its ruling when sold for more than its gold's or silver's weight, in gold or silver."
"5438","5803 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abd al-A'la, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Buy the sword adorned with silver. So this is Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) permitting sale of a sword whose ornament is silver for silver. And regarding the like was also narrated from a group of the Successors, disagreement."
"5439","5804 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Haywah and Ibn Lahi'ah informed me, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran, that he asked al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and Salim ibn Abdullah about buying a garment woven with gold, for gold, and they said: Buying it for gold is not proper."
"5440","5805 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Mujahid, that he saw no harm in buying gold for gold, or silver for silver and gold."
"5441","5806 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Mubarak, from al-Hasan, that he saw no harm in a silver-plated sword being sold for dirhams, for more than what is in it: the silver for the silver, and the sword for the excess."
"5442","5807 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from his father, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Yusuf, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, that he said on selling an adorned sword: When the silver in it is less than the price, there is no harm in that."
"5443","5808 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from his father, from Muhammad, from Abu Yusuf, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Amir al-Sha'bi, he said: There is no harm in selling an adorned sword for dirhams, for it contains its straps, sheath and blade."
"5444","Book 18: Gifts and charity. Chapter 1: Revoking a gift. 5809 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah and Hisham narrated to us from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Ibn Abbas: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who returns in his gift is like the one who returns in his vomit. Abu Ja'far said: So a group held the giver may not revoke what he gave, arguing with this hadith. They said: Since the Messenger of Allah made returning in a gift like returning in vomit, and a man's returning in his vomit is forbidden to him, so is his returning in his gift. Others opposed them and said: The giver may revoke his gift when it stands as it was, unconsumed and ungrown in itself, provided the recipient is not an unmarriageable kin of the giver and provided he did not compensate him, i.e. give him a reward for it. If he compensated him and he accepted that compensation, or the recipient is an unmarriageable kin of the giver, the giver may not revoke it. If the giver is not an unmarriageable kin of the recipient, but she is a wife who gave to her husband or a husband who gave to his wife, they are in that like unmarriageable kin, and neither may revoke what he gave to his spouse. Among their proof is that the Messenger of Allah likened the returner in his gift to the returner in vomit, without clarifying who the returner in vomit is. It may mean the man returning in his vomit, making the returner in his gift like one returning in what is forbidden to him, establishing the first view. Or it may mean the dog returning in its vomit, and the dog is not bound by prohibition or permission, so the returner is one returning in filth like the filth the dog returns in, which does not bar the giver from revoking. So we considered whether we find in the reports what shows us the intent in the first hadith."
"5445","5810 - So Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: We have not the evil example; the one returning in his gift is like the dog returning in its vomit."
"5446","5811 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us from Abdullah ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The one returning in his gift is like the dog that vomits then returns in its vomit. So this hadith proves the Messenger of Allah by what we mentioned in the first hadith meant only to keep his community from the examples of dogs, not that he nullified their revoking their gifts. And these words we narrated from Ibn Abbas were also narrated from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with them) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5447","5812 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Hawwalah."
"5448","5813 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us from Khilas ibn Amr, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The example of one returning in his gift is like the dog: it ate until full, vomited, then returned in its vomit and ate it. And the like of these words was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a meaning other than this meaning."
"5449","5814 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn Abdullah informed me that Abdullah ibn Umar used to narrate that Umar gave a horse in charity in Allah's cause, then found it being sold afterwards and wanted to buy it, so he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking his counsel in that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do not return in your charity. So Ibn Umar would not buy property he had made charity."
"5450","5815 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: I gave a horse in Allah's cause, and the one holding it wasted it, so I wanted to buy it from him, thinking he would sell it cheap, the opposite of expensive. I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said: Do not buy it even if he gives it to you for one dirham; do not return in your charity, for the one returning in his charity is like the dog returning in its vomit."
"5451","5816 - Isma'il ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, that he saw a horse sold in the market, having given it in charity, so he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Shall I buy it? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not buy it nor anything of its offspring, i.e. what it produces of young. So the Messenger of Allah barred Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) from buying what he had given in charity or anything of its offspring, making him if he did like the dog returning in its vomit. That did not make buying charity forbidden upon its giver, but leaving it is better for him. Likewise what we mentioned before of what was mentioned from the Messenger of Allah on revoking a gift is not upon prohibition, but because leaving it is better. And (it was narrated):"
"5452","5817 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Dhuray' narrated to us, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Tawus, from Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a giver to return in his gift except the father for his child. An objector said: This proves prohibition of revoking a gift to other than one's child. It was said: That proves nothing of what you mentioned, for the Prophet may have described that return as unlawful to stress it, out of dislike that any of his community have the evil example. The Messenger of Allah said: Charity is not lawful for one strong and able-bodied, yet that was not meaning it is forbidden to the rich, but meaning it is not lawful to him as it is lawful to others among the needy and disabled. Likewise the saying: It is not lawful for a giver to return in his gift, is only that it is not lawful to him as lawful things Allah permitted to His servants are lawful. And he did not make for its doer an example like the example the Messenger of Allah made for the returner in his gift. In that entered returning therein by revoking and by buying and other than it, then he excepted what a father gives his child. That, in our view and Allah knows best, is upon permitting the father to take what he gave his son at his need and poverty to it; for what is due to the child of that is not by an act he does making it a return whose example is like the dog returning in its vomit. Rather it is something Allah obligated to him for his poverty, so He did not straiten it upon him, as was also narrated from the Messenger of Allah in other than this hadith."
"5453","5818 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I gave my mother a garden, and she died leaving no heir but me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your charity became due and your garden returned to you. Abu Ja'far said: Do you not see the Messenger of Allah permitted the giver his charity when it returned to him by inheritance, while barring Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) from buying his charity. So by these two hadiths is established permissibility of charity returning to the giver by Allah's act, and dislike of charity returning to him by his own act. Likewise the father's maintenance from the son's wealth for his need and poverty became due to him by Allah Almighty's obligating it to him. So the Prophet thereby permitted him to take back his gift and spend it upon himself, making it like what returned to him by inheritance, not like what returned by buying and taking back. If one says: The Prophet specified the giving father apart from other givers, is the child's ruling in what he gave his father other than the father's ruling in what he gave his child? It is said: Rather their ruling is alike, for the Messenger of Allah's mentioning one of them upon the meaning we mentioned suffices for mentioning both and others whose ruling is like theirs. Allah said: Forbidden to you are your mothers, your daughters, your sisters, your paternal aunts, your maternal aunts, your brother's daughters and your sister's daughters. So He forbade all these by lineage. Then He said: And your mothers who nursed you, and your sisters through nursing, mentioning in nursing-prohibition only these two. So His mentioning them proves the rest forbidden by lineage share one ruling in nursing, and mentioning these two sufficed for mentioning others, since He had joined them all in lineage-prohibition, making their ruling one. So His forbidding some of them also through nursing proves their ruling therein is one. Likewise when the Messenger of Allah said: It is not lawful for anyone to return in his gift, he generalized all people. Then he said: Except the father for his child, upon the meaning we mentioned, proving those besides the father among givers, in gifts returning to them by Allah restoring them, are likewise, and mentioning some sufficed for the rest. So in none of these reports is what proves the giver may revoke his gift by annulling it to take it from the recipient and return it to his prior ownership. So we considered whether we find in what was narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah anything in that."
"5454","5819 - So Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hanzalah narrated to us from Salim, he said: I heard Ibn Umar say: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: Whoever gives a gift is more entitled to it until he is compensated for it with what pleases him."
"5455","5820 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Ghatfan ibn Tarif al-Murri, from Marwan ibn al-Hakam: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever gives a gift for kinship or as charity does not return in it; and whoever gives a gift thinking reward is sought by it remains upon his gift, revoking it if not pleased therefrom. So this is Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) distinguishing between gifts and charities: charities are not revoked, while gifts are of two kinds, one kind kinship, which he returned to charity's ruling, barring the giver from revoking; and another kind other than that, in which he gave the giver revoking while not pleased therefrom."
"5456","5821 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Umar, he said: Whoever gives a gift to a kin, it is valid; and whoever gives a gift to other than an unmarriageable kin of his is more entitled to it while not compensated for it."
"5457","5822 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, he said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman narrating from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from Ali, he said: The giver is more entitled while not compensated therefrom. So this is Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) giving the giver revoking his gift while not compensated therefrom. That, in our view, is upon the giver for whom revoking was given, as mentioned in the hadith we narrated from him before this, so that their two statements (may Allah be pleased with them both) do not contradict."
"5458","5823 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us from Jabir, from al-Qasim, and he mentioned with its chain the same as we narrated from Sulayman. And the like of this was narrated from Fudalah ibn Ubayd."
"5459","5824 - Abu Zur'ah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Rabi'ah ibn Yazid, from Abdullah ibn Amir al-Yahsabi, he said: I was with Fudalah ibn Ubayd when two men disputing came to him. One said: I gave this one a falcon upon his compensating me, and he did not. The other said: He gave me, mentioning nothing. Fudalah said to him: Return his gift to him, for only women and the base of men return in gifts."
"5460","5825 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Rabi'ah ibn Yazid, from Abdullah ibn Amir al-Yahsabi, that he said: I was with Fudalah ibn Ubayd when two men disputing over a falcon came to him. One said: I gave him a falcon hoping he compensates me for it. The other said: Yes, he gave me a falcon; I neither asked him nor sought it. Fudalah said to him: Return his gift to him, for only women and the worst of people return in gifts."
"5461","5826 - And there was also narrated from Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) in that what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, from Abu al-Darda', he said: Gifts are three: a man who gave without being asked, so it is as charity's path, and he may not return in his charity. And a man who was asked then gave, so he has reward; if he accepts a reward upon his gift, he has only that, and he may revoke his gift while not compensated. And a man who gave stipulating reward, so it is a debt upon its holder in his life and after his death. So this is Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) making gifts whose course is charity's course under charity's ruling, barring the giver from revoking, as the giver of charity is barred from revoking his charity. And he made what of them is otherwise, with no reward stipulated, revocable while the giver is not compensated for it; and he made what has compensation stipulated under sale's ruling, making the compensation due from the recipient in his life and after his death. This is gifts' ruling in our view. As for what we mentioned of the giver's revoking ending by the recipient's death or by his consuming the gift, it is for what was narrated from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) also in that."
"5462","5827 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from Umar, the same, i.e. like his hadith we mentioned in the section before this section, adding: and he consumes it, or one of them dies. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) made consuming the gift bar its giver from revoking it, and made one's death cut the giver's revoking therein as well; so we say likewise. And the like of what was narrated from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was narrated from Shurayh on gifts."
"5463","5828 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, he said: I heard Muhammad narrating that Shurayh said: Whoever gives for kinship, beneficence or ties, his giving is valid; and the side sought near is compensated from his gift or it is returned to him."
"5464","5829 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, the same."
"5465","5830 - Abu Ja'far said: As for each spouse's gift to the other, Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad: A woman gave her husband a gift then revoked it, and they disputed to Shurayh, who said to the husband: Your two witnesses that they saw her give to you without coercion or humiliation, otherwise her oath that she gave to you under coercion and humiliation. So this is Shurayh asking the husband for proof that she gave him without coercion after her taking back the gift. That proves had the Sunnah been established with him upon that, he would have returned the gift to her and not allowed her revoking it. And it was his view that the giver may revoke his gift except from an unmarriageable kin, so he made the wife in this like an unmarriageable kin; so we say likewise."
"5466","5831 - As for the husband's gift to his wife, Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah informed us, from Abu Mansur, he said: Ibrahim said: When a woman gives to her husband or a man gives to his wife, the gift is valid, and neither may revoke his gift."
"5467","5832 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from his father, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that he said: Husband and wife are like an unmarriageable kin: when one gives to his spouse he may not revoke. So the spouses in these reports were made like an unmarriageable kin, barring each from revoking what he gave his spouse; so we say likewise. We described herein what I hold on gifts, and what we mentioned of these reports, since we know of no one like those we narrated them from opposing them. So we left reasoning for them and followed them. Reasoning, had we been left with it, would be otherwise: that the giver not revoke a gift to other than an unmarriageable kin; for his ownership left it by giving it and it became the recipient's apart from him, so he may not annul what became owned against him except with its owner's pleasure. But following the reports and imitating the imams of knowledge is worthier, so we followed and imitated them. And all we explained in this chapter is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah's mercy be upon them all)."
"5468","Chapter 2: A man grants some of his children to the exclusion of others. 5833 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man and Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, they both informed him that they heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir say: My father granted me a slave boy, and my mother ordered me to go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to witness that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you give all your children? He said: No. He said: Then return it."
"5469","5834 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and from Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, they both narrated to him from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: His father brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I granted this son of mine a slave boy of mine. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you grant all your children the like of this? He said: No. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then take it back. Abu Ja'far said: So a group held that when a man grants some of his children to the exclusion of others it is void, arguing with this hadith. They said: Al-Nu'man at the time his father granted him was small, so his father was taking possession for him due to his minority on his behalf. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Return it, after it was in the ruling of what was possessed, this proves a father's grant to some children to the exclusion of others is not owned by the grantee and no gift is concluded for him upon it. Others opposed them and said: A man should equalize among his children in giving so they equalize in dutifulness, and not prefer some over others, causing estrangement in the hearts of the unpreferred. If he grants some something to the exclusion of others and the grantee takes possession for himself if adult, or his father takes possession for him from himself if small, by informing him and witnessing it, it is valid. Among their proof is that al-Nu'man's hadith we mentioned was narrated from him as they mentioned, with no proof he was then small; perhaps he was adult and had not taken possession."
"5470","5835 - And it was also narrated upon other than this meaning in the first hadith. So Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Amir al-Sha'bi, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: My father took me to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), having granted me a grant, to witness him upon that. He said: Did you grant all your children the like of this? He said: No. He said: Would it please you that they be to you in dutifulness all equal? He said: Yes. He said: Then call another to witness upon this. So what in this hadith of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to Bashir about what he granted al-Nu'man: Call another to witness upon this, proves ownership is established; for had it not been established, his saying would be invalid. This differs from the first hadith; for this saying does not prove invalidity of the contract al-Nu'man had made; for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may decline to witness wealth and past matters. And likewise those after him; for testimony is a matter the witness undertakes for the witnessed, so he may not undertake it. It may also bear otherwise: his saying: Call another to witness upon this, i.e. I am the imam, and the imam does not witness but judges. And in his saying: Call another to witness upon this, is proof of the contract's validity."
"5471","5836 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Warqa' narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I heard al-Nu'man upon this pulpit of ours say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Equalize among your children in giving as you love them to equalize among you in dutifulness. Abu Ja'far said: So the intent in this hadith was commanding equalization among them in giving so they all equalize in dutifulness. And in it is nothing mentioning invalidity of a contract concluded upon preferring."
"5472","5837 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir say: My father gave me a gift, and my mother Amrah bint Rawahah said: I am not pleased until you call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the witnesses to witness it. So he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I gave my son from Amrah a gift, and I call you to witness. He said: Did you give all your children the like of this? He said: No. He said: Then fear Allah and be just among your children. So in this hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not order him to return the thing; in it is only commanding equalization."
"5473","5838 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Murajja narrated to us, he said: Dawud narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: My father set out with me carrying me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, witness that I granted al-Nu'man of my wealth such-and-such. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Did you grant all your children? He said: No. He said: Would it not please you that they be to you in dutifulness equal? He said: Yes. He said: Then no. So Dawud's hadith wording differed in what Murajja narrated from him here and what Wuhayb narrated from him earlier in this chapter; and so al-Sha'bi narrated it from al-Nu'man, and Abu al-Duha also narrated it from al-Nu'man."
"5474","5839 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Fitr (hawwalah). 5840 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Fitr narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Duha narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir say: My father took me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to witness him upon something he gave me. He said: Have you other children besides him? He said: Yes. So he said with his hand: Did you not equalize among them? In this hadith he did not report that he ordered him to return it. He only said: Did you not equalize among them, by way of counsel, and that had he done it it would be better. And there was narrated from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this story of al-Nu'man other than all we narrated from al-Nu'man."
"5475","5841 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Nufayl narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, he said: Bashir's wife said to Bashir: Grant my son your slave boy, and call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to witness it for me. He said: So he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, So-and-so's daughter asked me to grant her son my slave boy, and said: Call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to witness. He said: Does he have brothers? He said: Yes. He said: Did you give all of them? He said: No. He said: Then this is not proper, and I witness only upon what is right. So in this hadith the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordering Bashir to return was before Bashir executed the charity, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advised him with what we mentioned. This differs from all narrated from al-Nu'man; for in those hadiths he had granted him before bringing him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), telling the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I granted this son of mine such, reporting he had done it. While in this hadith of Jabir is his informing the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his wife's asking him, so the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) speech to him was by way of counsel, and upon how the thing should be done if he chooses to do it. And Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri in agreement with this meaning."
"5476","5842 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman and Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to me that they heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir say: My father granted me a slave boy, then walked with me until he brought me to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: O Messenger of Allah, I granted my son a slave boy; if you permit me to execute it for him I shall execute it. Then he mentioned the hadith. So what we mentioned proves the grant had not been completed in him from when he granted him until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to return it. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he divided something among his household, equalized among them all, giving the slave among them as he gives the free."
"5477","5843 - Yunus narrated that to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me, from al-Qasim ibn Abbas, from Abdullah ibn Niyar, from Urwah, from A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought fine beads (tibah kharaz) and divided them between the free woman and the slave-girl. A'ishah said: And likewise my father used to divide for the free and the slave. So this was of what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would do: encompassing with his gifts all his household, their free and their slaves, not that it is obligatory but it is better than otherwise. Likewise his counsel on children to equalize among them in giving is not obligatory, nor that if one does otherwise it is not established. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah's mercy be upon them all). And some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with them) preferred some of their children over others in gifts."
"5478","5844 - So Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from A'ishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq granted her the harvest of twenty wasqs of his property at al-Ghabah. When death came to him he said: By Allah, O my daughter, none among people is dearer to me in wealth than you, nor any people harder upon me in poverty after me than you; I had granted you the harvest of twenty wasqs, and had you harvested and secured it it would be yours, but today it is the heir's property; they are only your brother and your two sisters, so divide it upon Allah Almighty's Book. A'ishah said: By Allah, O father, had it been such-and-such I would have left it; she is only Asma', so who is the other? He said: The one in the womb of Bint Kharijah; I think she is a girl."
"5479","5845 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, he said: Masruq narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq had given A'ishah a grant, then when he fell ill he said to her: Put it into the inheritance; and they mentioned possession, gift and charity."
"5480","5846 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, he said: Salih ibn Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf informed me that Abd al-Rahman preferred the sons of Umm Kulthum with a grant he divided among his children. So this is Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) giving A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) to the exclusion of his other children, deeming it permitted, and she deemed it likewise, with none of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with them) objecting to them. And this is Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) also preferring some of his children in what he gave them over others, with no objector objecting to him. So how may anyone carry these men's acts upon contradicting the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying. Rather the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying in what we mentioned of that was in our view upon recommendation, like his recommending equalization among his household in giving. And leaving preferring their free over their slaves is not that other than it is impermissible, but upon his recommending that, while other than it in ruling is permitted like its permissibility. And our companions differed on a child's gift in which the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) order to Bashir is followed, how it is? Abu Yusuf (may Allah's mercy be upon him) said: The female therein is equalized with the male. And Muhammad ibn al-Hasan (may Allah's mercy be upon him) said: Rather he makes it among them according to inheritances, for the male like the share of two females. Abu Ja'far said: In the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Equalize among them in giving as you love them to equalize to you in dutifulness, is proof he meant equalization between females and males; for nothing is wanted of dutifulness from a daughter but the like wanted of a son. Since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted from a father for his child what he wants from a child for him, and what he wants of dutifulness from the female is like what he wants from the male, what he wanted from him for them in giving is for the female like what he wanted for the male. And in Abu al-Duha's hadith, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you other children besides him? He said: Yes. He said: Did you not equalize among them? And he did not say: Have you other male or female children? And that is only with the female's ruling therein like the male's; had it not been so, he would only mention equalization after knowing they are all males. Since he refrained from investigating that, their ruling's equality with him is established. So this is better in our view than what Muhammad (may Allah's mercy be upon him) said. And there was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what also proves that."
"5481","5847 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, he said: A man was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then a son of his came, so he kissed him and seated him upon his thigh, then a daughter of his came and he seated her beside him. He said: Why did you not do justice between them? Do you not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted from him justice between the daughter and the son, preferring neither over the other; so that proves what we mentioned on giving as well."
"5482","Chapter 3: Umra (life-grant). 5848 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Zayd, from al-Walid ibn Rabah, from Abu Hurayrah: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Muslims are bound by their conditions. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to permitting umra, making it return to the giver after the grantee's death, arguing with this hadith. Others opposed them, saying: The Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying falls only upon conditions the Book permitted stipulating, the Sunnah brought, and Muslims agreed upon. As for what the Book or the Sunnah forbade, it is not included. Do you not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in Barirah's hadith: Every condition not in Allah's Book is void, even if a hundred conditions. And what is in Allah Almighty's Book is what is textually therein or what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said; for accepting it is only obligatory by Allah Almighty's Book, since He says therein: Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it, and whatever he forbids you, refrain. And not every condition Muslims stipulate enters the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Muslims are bound by their conditions; for had it been so, the two conditions in a sale the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade would be permitted, and this hadith would contradict that and his saying: Every condition not in Allah's Book is void even if a hundred conditions. Since he did not make it upon this meaning, but upon specific conditions, which we ascertained and knew, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed us by his saying: Muslims are bound by their conditions, that they are bound by those conditions he permitted them to stipulate, so those owed to them need not annul them. And there was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what also proved that."
"5483","5849 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi' al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn Abdullah al-Muzani narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Muslims are bound by their conditions, except a condition permitting the forbidden or forbidding the lawful. So this proves the conditions Muslims are bound by are other than these excepted conditions. And the umra conditions, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apprised us of their invalidity in reports coming from him successively. Among them is what (follows):"
"5484","5850 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Sulayman ibn Yasar: A governor over Madinah, called Tariq, judged umra for the heir, upon Jabir's saying from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5485","5851 - Yunus informed us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus, from Hajar, from Zayd ibn Thabit: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged umra for the heir. So the Messenger of Allah in this made umra for the heir, thereby annulling the umra condition. The first party said: The Messenger of Allah did not clarify in this hadith whose heir he is; it may mean the giver's heir. It was said: This is impossible in our view; for the mention was upon something given to the grantee for his life upon returning after death to the giver, so the Messenger of Allah made that for the heir, i.e. made for the grantee's heir what the giver had stipulated not be inheritance."
"5486","5852 - Proof of that is that Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi informed us, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, from Zayd ibn Thabit: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is granted something for his life, it is his and his heir's. So this saying proves the heir judged for in the hadith we mentioned in the preceding section is the grantee's heir. And (it was narrated):"
"5487","5853 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus: Hujr ibn Qays informed him that Zayd ibn Thabit informed him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Umra is inheritance."
"5488","5854 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal informed us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Hujr al-Madari, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The path of umra is the path of inheritance. Abu Ja'far said: So this too means like what preceded. And (it was narrated):"
"5489","5855 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Mu'awiyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Umra is valid for its people. The first party said: Its people are those who granted it. So among the proof against them in that (is what follows)."
"5490","5856 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ubayd ibn Ya'ish narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, he said: Mu'awiyah said to me: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever grants an umra, it is his; his descendants who inherit him inherit it. So this hadith proves its people for whom it is valid are the grantees, not the granters."
"5491","5857 - And Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Umra belongs to whom it was given."
"5492","5858 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah, from Yahya, and he mentioned with its chain the same."
"5493","5859 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5494","5860 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep your properties to yourselves; do not grant umra; whoever grants anyone something, it is his."
"5495","5861 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad informed us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Kathir informed us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No umra; whoever is granted something, it is his. The first party said: We do not deny umra belongs to whom it was granted; we only said it returns to the granter after the grantee's death. So among our proof against them is that the Messenger of Allah forbade umra in the reports we mentioned. It is impossible he forbade it while it runs as contracted; rather he forbade it because it runs otherwise. He said: Whoever is granted something, it is his, unrestricted, without saying: his while alive. So that proves it is his like his other property in his life and after his death. This is the meaning of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah that he made it valid, i.e. valid for the grantee therein forever after."
"5496","5862 - And among what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he made it valid is what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan informed us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Umra is valid."
"5497","5863 - Proof of that also is that Ibn Abi Dawud and Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Hisham said: What do you say on umra? I said to him: al-Nadr ibn Anas narrated to me, from Bashir ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayrah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Umra is valid. Al-Zuhri said: It is not umra until made for him and his descendants. 5864 - He said to Ata ibn Abi Rabah: What do you say? He said: Jabir ibn Abdullah narrated to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Umra is inheritance. So Ata and Qatadah both made it valid for the grantee, inherited from him, and al-Zuhri did not object to them; he only said: It is not umra whose ruling is this until made for the grantee and his descendants, so it is like his property, inherited from him as his other properties are inherited from him, though those inheriting it from him include others than his descendants, as Abu Salamah narrated to him; and we shall mention that in its place in this chapter, Allah willing."
"5498","5865 - And among what also proves what we mentioned is that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not grant umra nor ruqba; whoever is granted something or given ruqba, it belongs to the heir when he dies."
"5499","5866 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep your properties to yourselves; do not waste them; for whoever is granted an umra, it is his, alive and dead, and his descendants'."
"5500","5867 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is granted an umra for his life, it is his in his life and his heirs' after his death."
"5501","5868 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from his father, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Humayd, from Jabir, he said: A man of us granted his mother a grant for her life; when she died he said: I am more entitled to my grant. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged it inheritance. Ibn Abi Shaybah said: This Humayd is a man of Kindah. Abu Ja'far said: So these reports uncovered for us the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intent in the preceding reports, upon the interpretation we described; and there were also narrated on umra reports with other than this wording. Among them is what (follows):"
"5502","5869 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man granted an umra for him and his descendants, it belongs to whom it was given; for he was given a gift in which inheritances fall."
"5503","5870 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Layth narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab (hawwalah). 5871 - And Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Layth narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever grants a man an umra for him and his descendants, his saying cut his right therein, and it belongs to whom it was granted and his descendants."
"5504","5872 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed: Whoever is granted an umra, it is his and his descendants', decisively; no condition or exception by the giver is permitted therein. Abu Ja'far said: So in these reports, whoever is granted an umra for him and his descendants, it belongs to the grantee, not returning to the giver by condition or exception; for he gave a gift in which inheritances fall. Those permitting stipulation in umra said: By this we say: when umra falls upon this it never returns to the giver, but when it lacks mention of descendants it returns to the giver after the grantee's passing. They said: This is weightier than what Ata and Abu al-Zubayr narrated from Jabir ibn Abdullah; for Abu Salamah added upon them his saying: and his descendants, and he is not lesser than them, and addition is weightier. So among our proof for the others in that: had no hadith been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on umra but this hadith of Abu Salamah, it would contain the strongest proof for those saying umra never returns to the granter, nor is his condition permitted. For umra is of one of two cases: either included in the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Muslims are bound by their conditions, so the granter's condition executes as he stipulated, none void, as the endower's conditions execute in what he endowed; or it is out of the granter's ownership into the grantee's ownership, becoming among his other property, voiding what he stipulated upon it. We considered: when umra is made for the grantee and his descendants, and he dies leaving descendants and a wife, or bequests, or debt, those execute therein as in his property, unbarred by the granter's condition making it for him and his descendants, though his wife is not of his descendants, nor his creditors nor his legatees. Likewise had the grantee died childless, nothing of it returns to the granter. Since what we described is so, it is so ever: it is valid as the granter made it, while his stipulated condition is void, none of it executing, little or much, and it exits the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Muslims are bound by their conditions, so its conditions are not of those the Prophet meant thereby. And this view we upheld is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (may Allah's mercy be upon them)."
"5505","5873 - And the like was also narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both). Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: I heard Ibn Umar, a man asking him about a man to whom a man gave a she-camel for his life, and it gave birth (i.e. delivered), and he said: It is his and its young; then I asked him after that, and he said: It is his, alive and dead, and Allah knows best."
"5506","Chapter: Endowed charities (waqf). 5874 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim and Sa'id ibn Sufyan al-Jahdari narrated to us, they said: Ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Nafi' informed me from Ibn Umar: Umar acquired land at Khaybar and came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking his counsel. He said: I acquired land, never acquiring better property; how do you order me? He said: If you wish, immobilize its root, neither sold nor gifted; Abu Asim said: I think he said: nor inherited. He said: So give it in charity among the poor, kin, slaves, in Allah's cause, the wayfarer and the weak; no sin upon its administrator to eat from it without amassing wealth. He said: I mentioned that to Muhammad, and he said: Without amassing capital."
"5507","5875 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle narrated to me, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to me from Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Muttalib, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Nafi', freedman of Ibn Umar, from Ibn Umar: Umar consulted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on giving his property at Thamgh in charity. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give it in charity: divide its fruit and immobilize its root, neither sold nor gifted. Abu Ja'far said: So a group held that when a man endows his house upon his children and grandchildren, then after them in Allah's cause, it is valid, and it thereby left his ownership to Allah (mighty and majestic), with no way after that to sell it, arguing with these reports. Among those holding that: Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan (may Allah's mercy be upon them both), and it is the view of Madinah's and Basrah's people. Others opposed them, among them Abu Hanifah and Zufar ibn al-Hudhayl (may Allah's mercy be upon them both), saying: All this is inheritance, not leaving its endower's ownership for this reason. Among their proof is that when Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) consulted him, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Immobilize its root and make its fruit flow. It may be what he ordered thereby removes it from his ownership, or it may not remove it from his ownership, but it runs upon what he ran it while he leaves it, and he may annul that whenever he wishes. Like a man binding himself to Allah to give his palms' fruit in charity while he lives, told: Execute that, but not compelled upon it, nor taken by it whether he wills or refuses. But if he executes it, it is good, and if he withholds it he is not compelled upon it. Likewise his heirs after him: if they execute it upon what their father ran it, it is good, and if they withhold it that is theirs. And Umar's (may Allah be pleased with him) endowment remaining to our time proves nothing that none of his household could annul it. What would prove they may not annul it is had they disputed therein after his death and been barred from that. Had that been permitted, umra would contain what proves endowments are not sold. Rather what reached us is their leaving Umar's (may Allah be pleased with him) endowment running upon what Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) ran it in his life, with no report that any of them objected therein with anything. And there was narrated from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) what proves he could annul it."
"5508","5876 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ziyad ibn Sa'd, from Ibn Shihab: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Had I not mentioned my charity to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or the like of this, I would have taken it back. When Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said this, it proves the mere immobilizing of the land did not bar him from returning in it, and only what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him therein barred him from returning, and he parted with him upon fulfilling it, so he disliked returning from that, as Abdullah ibn Umar disliked returning after the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) death from the fasting he had parted with him upon doing, though he might not fast. Then this Shurayh, judge of Umar, Uthman and Ali, the rightly-guided rightly-guiding caliphs (may Allah's pleasure be upon them all), there was also narrated from him in that what (follows):"
"5509","5877 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Yusuf, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, he said: I asked Shurayh about a man making his house confined (habs) upon the last then the last of his children. He said: I only judge, I do not give fatwa. He said: So I adjured him, and he said: No confinement against Allah's ordained shares. And this judges cannot be ignorant of, nor may imams imitate one ignorant of its like; then no objector objected to him among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor among their Successors (may Allah's mercy be upon them). Then there was also narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that what (follows):"
"5510","5878 - Al-Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: My brother Isa narrated to me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), after Surah al-Nisa' was revealed with the ordained shares therein, forbidding confinement (habs)."
"5511","5879 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdillah ibn Bukayr and 'Amr ibn Khalid informed us, they both said: 'Abdullah ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof."
"5512","5880 - 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to me, and he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof."
"5513","5881 - Rawh and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, they said: Ahmad ibn Salih said to us: 'This hadith is authentic, and I hold this view.' Rawh said: Ahmad ibn Salih said to me - and al-Dimashqi, meaning 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf, had narrated it to me from Ibn Lahi'ah - that Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed that endowments (al-ahbas) are prohibited and impermissible, and that they had existed before the revelation of the inheritance ordinances, contrary to what they became after the revelation of the inheritance ordinances. This is the exposition of this chapter through the transmitted reports. As for its exposition through rational examination: Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, Zufar and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them, along with all those who differ with them and those who agree with them, have agreed that when a man endows his house in his (death-)illness for the poor and the needy and then dies in that illness, it is valid from his third (of the estate), and that it is not inherited from him. So we examined that, (asking) whether it points to one of the two sayings. (We found that) when a man designates something of his wealth, of dinars or dirhams, as charity but does not carry it out before he dies, it is inheritance, whether he designated it in his illness or in his health - unless he makes it a bequest (to take effect) after his death, in which case it is executed after his death from one-third of his wealth, just as bequests are executed. But if he designates it in his illness and does not deliver it to the poor by paying it to them, it is like what he designates in his health; and whatever of his wealth he disposes of in his health is executed from his entire wealth, and he retains no ownership over it thereafter - such as manumissions, gifts and charities. That which is executed when he does it in his illness is from one-third of his wealth. And the endower, when he endows his house or his land in his illness and dedicates its ultimate benefit for the sake of Allah, that is valid by their agreement from one-third of his wealth after his death, with no recourse for his heir against it. And that is not included in the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): 'There is no habs (withholding) against the ordinances of Allah.' So rational examination accordingly requires that likewise be its course when he endows in health: it is executed from his entire wealth, and he has no recourse against it thereafter, by analogy and examination based on what we have mentioned. To this I incline, and this I hold, through rational examination, not through the reports, for the reports on that I have already described, explained their meanings and uncovered their aspects. If one says: does the land leave its owner's possession through endowment, (passing) to no owner's possession? It is said to him: what do you object to in this, when you and your opponent have agreed that land which its owner makes a mosque for the Muslims, leaving it free for them, has thereby left his possession to no owner's possession, but rather to Allah Almighty? So what binds your opponent in what you argued against him with what we have described binds you likewise in this. If one says: what is the meaning of the Messenger of Allah's prohibition of habs which you narrated from him in the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both? It is said to him: the people have said two things about that. One of them is the saying we mentioned when we narrated it. The other is that by it was meant what the people of the pre-Islamic era used to do with the bahirah, the sa'ibah, the wasilah and the hami: they would withhold whatever they designated as such, and would not let anyone inherit it. Then when the chapter of the inheritance ordinances was revealed and Allah Almighty explained therein the shares of inheritance and distributed wealth over them, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'There is no habs.' Then those who permitted endowed charities spoke about them, after affirming them as we mentioned, and some of them said: they are valid whether taken possession of from the donor or not. Among those who held that is Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him. And some of them said: they are not executed until he removes them from his possession and another takes possession of them from him. Among those who held this saying are Ibn Abi Layla, Malik ibn Anas and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them. So we needed to examine that in order to extract a correct saying from the two sayings. We saw that the acts of servants are of (different) kinds. Among them is manumission, which is executed by mere word, because the slave leaves his master's ownership only to Allah Almighty. And among them are gifts and charities, which are not executed by mere word until accompanied by the taking of possession by the one to whom ownership was given. So we wanted to see the ruling of endowments: to which of them it most resembles, so as to attach it to it. We saw that when a man endows his land or his house, the one for whom it is endowed owns only its usufruct and owns nothing of its corpus; rather he (the endower) removed it from his own ownership to Allah Almighty. So it is established that this is the counterpart of what he removes from his ownership to Allah Almighty. Just as that needs no taking of possession along with the word, so likewise endowments need no taking of possession along with the word. Another proof: if we were to require taking of possession, the taker would only take possession of what he does not own through the endowment, so his taking possession of it and his not taking possession of it are the same. So there is established by what we mentioned that which Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, held."
"5514","Book of Pledges (Kitab al-Rahn). Chapter 1: Riding the pledge, using it, and drinking its milk. 5882 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah informed us, from al-Sha'bi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: 'The back (of the animal) is ridden for its expenditure when it is pledged, and the milk of the milch-animal is drunk for its expenditure when it is pledged.' Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to (the view) that the pledgor may ride the pledge in return for his spending on it, and may also drink its milk in return for his spending on it, and they adduced this hadith for that. Others differed with them on that and said: the pledgor may not ride the pledge nor drink its milk while it is a pledge with him, and he may not benefit from it in anything. And among the proof for them against the people of the first saying was that this hadith which they adduced is an equivocal (mujmal) hadith: it is not explained therein who is the one who rides and drinks the milk. So whence was it permissible for them to make him the pledgor rather than the pledgee? This cannot be for anyone except with a proof pointing him to that, whether from the Book, the Sunnah, or consensus. And besides that, Hushaym narrated this hadith and explained therein what Yazid ibn Harun did not explain."
"5515","5883 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Salim al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Zakariyya, from al-Sha'bi, from Abu Hurayrah, mentioning that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'When the riding-animal is pledged, the pledgee must feed it; and the milk of the milch-animal is drunk, and the one who drinks bears its expenditure, and he rides.' So this hadith showed that the one meant by the riding and the drinking of milk in the first hadith is the pledgee, not the pledgor. So that was assigned to him, and the expenditure was placed upon him as recompense for what he obtains in return of what we mentioned. And this was, in our view - and Allah knows best - at the time when usury was permissible, when the loan that brings benefit had not yet been prohibited, nor the taking of a thing for a thing even if the two were unequal. Then usury was prohibited after that, and every loan that brings benefit was prohibited, and the people of knowledge agreed that the expenditure on the pledge is upon the pledgor, not upon the pledgee, and that the pledgee may not use the pledge. And among what was narrated on the abrogation of usury is that which..."
"5516","5884 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur and al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from 'A'ishah, she said: When the verses at the end of Surah al-Baqarah were revealed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and recited them to the people, then he prohibited trading in the sale of wine."
"5517","5885 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, he said: Mansur narrated to me, from Muslim, from Masruq, from 'A'ishah, the like thereof. So when usury was prohibited, all its forms were prohibited, and things taken were returned to their equal countervalues, and the sale of milk in the udders was prohibited. Included in that is the prohibition of the expenditure by which the spender comes to own milk in the udders - and that expenditure is not fixed to any amount, and the milk is likewise. So through the abrogation of usury there was lifted (the ruling) that expenditure be obligatory upon the pledgee in return for the benefits due to him as recompense thereof, and for the milk which he milks and drinks. And it is said to the one who assigns that to the pledgor, giving him the use of the pledge: is it permissible for the pledgor to pledge to a man a riding-animal while he is riding it? He cannot but say: No. Then it is said to him: since the pledge is not valid unless it is left free between him (the pledgee) and it so that he takes possession of it and it comes into his hand to the exclusion of the pledgor's hand - as Allah Almighty described the pledge in His saying: 'pledges taken in possession' - he says: Yes. Then it is said to him: since it is not permissible to initiate the pledge over what the pledgor is riding, it is likewise not permissible for it to remain in his hand thereafter as a pledge for his right except for that (same reason), because the continuance of possession is indispensable in the pledge, since the pledge is only the pledgee's detention of the pledged thing for the debt. And in that too is what prevents the pledgee from employing the pledged slave-girl, for thereby she returns to a state over which initiating the pledge is not permissible. And another proof: they have agreed that the pledged slave-girl may not be approached sexually by the pledgor, and the pledgee may prevent him from that. So just as the pledgee prevents the pledgor, by right of the pledge, from approaching her, he likewise may prevent him, by right of the pledge, from employing her. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them. And there has..."
"5518","5886 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, he said: 'No benefit is derived from the pledge in anything.' So here is al-Sha'bi saying this, while there has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that which we mentioned. So it is possible that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that, then he (al-Sha'bi) himself says contrary to it, while the abrogation was not established with him? If that is indeed so, then he has become suspect in his opinion; and if he is suspect in his opinion, he is suspect in his narration. And if uprightness is established for him in his narration, uprightness is established for him in abandoning contradicting it; and if the fall of one of the two matters is granted, the fall of the other is granted. And the one who argues against us with this hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, says: whoever narrates a hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knows best its interpretation. So it would follow upon his own principle, and bind him in his saying, to say that what al-Sha'bi said, of what we mentioned contradicting what was narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was a proof of its abrogation."
"5519","Chapter 2: The pledge that perishes in the pledgee's hand - what is its ruling? 5887 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that he heard Malik, Yunus and Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrating from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'The pledge is not forfeited (la yaghlaqu al-rahn).' 5888 - Yunus ibn Yazid said: Ibn Shihab said: And Ibn al-Musayyib used to say: 'The pledge belongs to its owner: its gain is his and its loss is upon him.'"
"5520","5889 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata' and Sulayman ibn Musa: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'The pledge is not forfeited.' Abu Ja'far said: Then one said: since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'The pledge is not forfeited; to its owner belong its gain and upon him its loss,' it is established thereby that the pledge is not lost against the debt, and that to its owner belongs its gain - which is its safety - and upon him its loss - which is the liability for the debt after the loss of the pledge. And this is an interpretation which all the people of knowledge of the language have rejected, claiming it has no basis with them. And what led us to bring this hadith, even though it is disconnected (munqati'), is the argumentation against us of the one who holds to the connected (musnad) reports with it, and his claim that we contradicted it. And it would have bound him upon his own principle, had he been fair to his opponent, not to argue with the like of this since it is disconnected, while the disconnected does not establish proof with him. If he says: I only accepted it, though disconnected, because it is from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, and the disconnected (report) of Sa'id stands in place of the connected. It is said to him: who granted you to single out this Sa'id, while withholding from the like of him among the people of Madinah - such as Abu Salamah, al-Qasim, Salim, 'Urwah and Sulayman ibn Yasar, may Allah's mercy be upon them, and their likes among the people of Madinah; and al-Sha'bi and Ibrahim al-Nakha'i and their likes, may Allah's mercy be upon them, among the people of Kufah; and al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin and their likes, may Allah's mercy be upon them, among the people of Basrah; and likewise whoever was in the era of those we mentioned among the rest of the jurists of the cities, may Allah's mercy be upon them, and whoever was above them of the first generation of the Successors, such as 'Alqamah, al-Aswad, 'Amr ibn Shurahbil, 'Abidah and Shurayh, may Allah's mercy be upon them? If this is unconditionally yours concerning Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, then it is unconditionally another's concerning those we mentioned. And if another is barred from that, then you are barred from its like, for this is arbitrary judgment, and no one may judge in Allah's religion by arbitrariness. And the people of knowledge have said, on the interpretation of the Messenger of Allah's saying, other than what I mentioned."
"5521","5890 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, as far as I know - and if not, then it has entered into what he had licensed to me. He said: Abu 'Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who gave a pledge to a man and took dirhams from him, saying: 'If I come to you with your due by such-and-such (time), (well and good); otherwise the pledge is yours for your due.' So Ibrahim said: 'The pledge is not forfeited.' Abu 'Ubayd said: So did he make it an answer to his question? And there has been narrated from Tawus something like this; that reached me from Ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Amr, from Tawus. 5891 - Abu 'Ubayd said: And 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi informed me, from Malik ibn Anas and Sufyan ibn Sa'id, that they both used to explain it upon this explanation."
"5522","5892 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, from Malik ibn Anas, with that also."
"5523","5893 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'The pledge is not forfeited.' So thereby the owner of the pledge is prevented from buying it from the one with whom he pledged it until it is sold to another than him. So al-Zuhri also went, concerning that forfeiture (ghulq), to (the view) that it is about the sale, not about the loss. So these early ones say what we mentioned. And there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this also that which..."
"5524","5894 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Mus'ab ibn Thabit narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, that a man took a horse as a pledge, and the horse died in the pledgee's hand. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Your right has gone.' So this, from the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), proved the nullification of the debt through the loss of the pledge. If one says: this is disconnected, it is said to him: that which you interpreted is also disconnected; so if the disconnected is a proof for you against us, then the disconnected is also a proof for us against you. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from another direction that which agrees with that also."
"5525","5895 - Abu al-'Awwam Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Nizar al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to me, from his father, he said: Those of our jurists whose saying is taken as final whom I met - among them Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, Kharijah ibn Zayd and 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdillah, among a group of their peers, people of jurisprudence, righteousness and merit - he mentioned all that he gathered of their sayings in his book in this manner, that they said: 'The pledge is against what is in it (i.e. it perishes with the debt) when it perishes and its value is unknown; and the trustworthy among them raises that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him).' So these are the imams of Madinah and its jurists, saying that the pledge perishes with what is in it (against the debt), and the trustworthy among them raises it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So whichever of them narrated it, it is a proof, for he is a jurist imam; then their saying it all together, and their consensus upon it. So there is established thereby the soundness of that also, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib - and it is from him that the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), 'The pledge is not forfeited,' is taken."
"5526","5896 - And this opponent of ours has claimed that whoever narrates a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knows best its interpretation - to the point that he said, concerning the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, which Sayf narrated to us from Qays ibn Sa'd, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged by the oath along with the witness - 'Amr said: concerning properties - so he made the saying of 'Amr on this a proof and an evidence for him that that ruling is concerning properties to the exclusion of other things. So if the saying of 'Amr ibn Dinar - this interpretation of his - necessitates a proof thereby, then the saying of Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib which we mentioned, and his interpretation of what he narrated, is more worthy of being a proof. And this opponent of ours has claimed that he holds to following (al-ittiba'); so from whom did he take this saying of his, and who is his imam in it? And we have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to it, and from a Successor of his Companions contrary to it also. And there has been narrated from the imams of his Companions contrary to that also."
"5527","5897 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Abu al-'Awwam, from Matar, from 'Ata', from 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said concerning the man who takes a pledge and it is lost. He said: If it was for less, they return (the excess) to him; and if it was for more, then he is a trustee concerning the excess."
"5528","5898 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Isra'il, from 'Abd al-A'la al-Tha'labi, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, that 'Ali said: 'When a man pledges a pledge to a man, and the receiver says to him: I will not accept it except for more than what I give you - then if it is lost, he returns the excess to him. But if he pledges it to him, while it is more than what was given, with the willing consent of the pledgor, and it is lost, then it is against what is in it (i.e. it perishes with the debt).'"
"5529","5899 - Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Khatib narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Khilas - he is Ibn 'Amr - that 'Ali said: 'When there is excess in the pledge and a calamity befalls it, then it is against what is in it; but if no calamity befalls it and he (the pledgee) is suspected, then he returns the excess.'"
"5530","5900 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Humam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan and Khilas ibn 'Amr, that 'Ali said concerning the pledge: 'They return to each other the increase and the decrease together; but if a calamity befalls it, he is absolved.' So here 'Umar and 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, have agreed that the pledge whose value equals the debt is lost with the debt; their disagreement is only concerning what exceeds the pledge's value over the debt's amount. So 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: it is a trust. And 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said what we have narrated from him in the hadith of Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ahmad ibn Dawud. And there has been narrated also from al-Hasan and Shurayh on that that which..."
"5531","5901 - Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, that al-Hasan and Shurayh said: 'The pledge is against what is in it.'"
"5532","5902 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Hasin, he said: I heard Shurayh saying: 'The pledges have gone with what was in them.'"
"5533","5903 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from 'Isa ibn Jaban, he said: I pledged jewelry, and it was worth more than what was in it (the debt); then it was lost, so we disputed to Shurayh, and he said: 'The pledge is against what is in it.' So here al-Hasan and Shurayh held that the pledge's loss nullifies it against the debt; and that has been narrated also from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i."
"5534","5904 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from his father Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that he said concerning the pledge: 'It perishes in the pledgee's hands: if its value and the debt are equal, it is lost with the debt; if its value is less than the debt, he returns the excess (of the debt) to him; and if its value is more than the debt, then he is a trustee concerning the excess.' And there has been narrated on that from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah that which..."
"5535","5905 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', concerning a man who pledged a slave-girl to a man, then she perished. He said: 'She is against the pledgee's right.' So here 'Ata' says this, while we have narrated from him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: 'The pledge is not forfeited.' So this too is a proof against our opponent, since it is of his principle that whoever narrates a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his interpretation thereof is a proof. So he has contradicted all this in this chapter, and contradicted what we have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and from 'Umar and 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, and from those of the Successors we mentioned, may Allah's mercy be upon them. So who is his imam in this? Or whom has he followed? Then rational examination in this also refutes what he said and held, since making the pledge a trust that is lost for nothing. And they have agreed that trusts: their owner may take them, and it is forbidden for the pledgee to withhold them from him. But the pledge is contrary to that, since the pledgee may detain it and withhold its owner from it until he recovers his debt; thereby its ruling departs from the ruling of trusts. And we saw usurped things: it is forbidden for the usurpers to detain them, and lawful for those usurped from to take them; but the pledge is not so, for the pledgee may lawfully detain the pledge and withhold the pledgor from it until he recovers his debt from it. And we saw loans for use: the borrower may benefit from them, and the lender may take them from him whenever he wishes. But the pledge is not so, for the pledgee is forbidden to use the pledge, and the pledgor may not take it from him until he pays him his debt. So the ruling of the pledge became distinct from the ruling of deposits, usurpations and loans for use, and it is established that its ruling is contrary to the ruling of all that. And they have agreed that the pledgee may detain it until he recovers the debt, and it is lawful for the pledgor to take it when he is quit of the debt. So since the detention of the pledge is guaranteed by the detention of the debt, and the lifting of its detention is guaranteed by the lifting of the detention of the debt, so likewise the establishment of the debt is guaranteed by the establishment of the pledge: so long as the pledge stands, the debt stands; and whenever the pledge does not stand, the debt does not stand. And likewise we saw the sold item - in our saying and in the saying of this opponent of ours - the seller may detain it for the price, and whenever it is lost in his hand it is lost with the price. So examination, upon what we and he agreed on of this, requires the pledge to be likewise, and that its loss nullifies the debt just as the loss of the sold item nullifies the price. So this is the examination in this chapter - except that Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them, held concerning the pledge what we have narrated in this chapter from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, may Allah's mercy be upon him. And they argued for that with what they agreed upon concerning usurpation, saying: we saw usurped things, whose loss from their usurpation does not necessitate more than the guarantee of their value, though their usurpation is forbidden. They said: so pledged things, of which it is established that they are guaranteed, are more worthy that their guarantee not necessitate upon the one who guaranteed them more than the amount of their value. And they used to explain the saying of Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, 'To him its gain and upon him its loss,' as being about the sale: meaning, when the pledge is sold for a price short of the debt, the pledgee bears that shortfall - and that is the loss mentioned in the hadith; and when it is sold for an excess over the debt, the pledgor takes that excess - and that is the gain mentioned in the hadith."
"5536","Book of Sharecropping and Irrigation Partnerships (Kitab al-Muzara'ah wa al-Musaqah). Chapter 1: Sharecropping. 5906 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Ibn 'Umar saying: I heard Rafi' ibn Khadij saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited sharecropping (al-muzara'ah)."
"5537","5907 - Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Ibn 'Umar saying: We used to practise mukhabarah, and we saw no harm in that, until Rafi' ibn Khadij claimed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited mukhabarah, so we abandoned it."
"5538","5908 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: 'Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn 'Abdillah ibn 'Umar informed me that his father - meaning 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar - used to let out his land until it reached him that Rafi' ibn Khadij al-Ansari used to forbid the letting of land. So he met him and said: O Ibn Khadij, what do you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the letting of land? He said: I heard my two uncles - and they had witnessed Badr - narrating to the people of the house that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited the letting of land. 'Abdullah said: I certainly knew that land used to be let in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then 'Abdullah feared that the Messenger of Allah had introduced something new in that which he had not known, so he abandoned the letting of land."
"5539","5909 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited al-haql. Shu'bah said: So I said to al-Hakam: What is al-haql? He said: That land be let. Abu Ja'far said: I think he said: for a third and a fourth."
"5540","5910 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Mujahid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited us from a matter that was beneficial to us, though the command of Allah's Prophet is more beneficial to us. He said: 'Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or have it cultivated.'"
"5541","5911 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Zubaydi narrated to us, he said: I heard Mujahid saying: Usayd, the nephew of Rafi' ibn Khadij, narrated to me, he said: Rafi' ibn Khadij said, and he mentioned the like thereof, except that he said: 'Let him cultivate it; and if he is unable, let him have his brother cultivate it.'"
"5542","5912 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Mujahid, he said: I took Tawus by the hand until I brought him to Ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, and he narrated to him from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he prohibited the letting of land. But Tawus refused, saying: I heard Ibn 'Abbas, that he saw no harm in that."
"5543","5913 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Tariq ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited muzabanah and muhaqalah. And he said: 'Only three cultivate: a man who has land, so he cultivates it; a man to whom his brother granted land, so he cultivates what was granted to him of it; and a man who hires for gold or silver.'"
"5544","5914 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym and al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof."
"5545","5915 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, from Ya'la ibn Hakim, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or have his brother cultivate it, and let him not let it for a third, nor for a fourth, nor for specified food.'"
"5546","5916 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Bukayr ibn 'Amir narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Nu'm, he said: Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to me that he cultivated land, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was watering it, and asked him: To whom belongs the crop, and to whom belongs the land? He said: My crop, with my seed and my labour - to me half, and to the sons of so-and-so half. So he said: 'You have dealt in usury; return the land to its people and take your expenditure.'"
"5547","5917 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Bukayr narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Rafi', the like thereof."
"5548","5918 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Najashi, the freedman of Rafi' ibn Khadij, narrated to me, he said: I said to Rafi': I have land which I let out - then Rafi' forbade me, and I think he said to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited the letting of land. He said: 'When one of you has land, let him cultivate it or have his brother cultivate it; and if he does not do so, let him leave it and not let it for anything.' So I said: What if I leave it, neither cultivating it nor letting it for anything, then some people cultivate it and give me something of its produce as a gift - may I take it? He said: No."
"5549","5919 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us. (Ha') 5920 - And Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, they both said: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Shaybani narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to me, he said: I asked 'Abdullah ibn Ma'qil about sharecropping, and he said: Thabit ibn al-Dahhak informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited sharecropping."
"5550","5921 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Mihr narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib informed us, and he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof."
"5551","5922 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah narrated to me, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, he said: Some men among us had surplus lands in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they used to let them for a half, a third and a fourth. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or grant it to his brother; and if he refuses, let him hold it.'"
"5552","5923 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Ata' narrated to us, from Jabir, the like thereof."
"5553","5924 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: It was said to 'Ata': Did Jabir ibn 'Abdillah narrate to you that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or have his brother cultivate it, and let him not let it?' So 'Ata' said: Yes."
"5554","5925 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa asked 'Ata' while I was present, then he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof."
"5555","5926 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khattab ibn 'Uthman al-Fawzi narrated to us, he said: Damrah narrated to us, from Ibn Shawdhab, from Matar, from 'Ata', from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, then he mentioned the like thereof."
"5556","5927 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us; Ibn Khuthaym said: he narrated to me from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: 'Whoever does not abandon mukhabarah, let him be informed of war from Allah Almighty.'"
"5557","5928 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulaym al-Ta'ifi informed us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Uthman ibn Khuthaym, and he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof, adding: 'from Allah and His Messenger.'"
"5558","5929 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Salim ibn Hayyan, from Sa'id ibn Mina', from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever has surplus water or surplus land, let him cultivate it or have it cultivated, and do not sell them.' Sulaym said: So I said to him: Meaning the letting? He said: Yes. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to these reports and disliked thereby the hiring of land for a portion of what comes out of it; and these reports have come with different meanings. As for Thabit ibn al-Dahhak, may Allah be pleased with him, he narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prohibited sharecropping, but did not explain which sharecropping. If it is sharecropping for a known portion of what the land produces, this is that over which these arguers with these reports and their opponents differ. But if that sharecropping which he prohibited is sharecropping for a third, a fourth, and something else - such as what comes out of what is sown in a specified spot of the land - this is of what both groups agree upon the invalidity of sharecropping over. And there is nothing in this hadith of Thabit to negate that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant one particular of these two meanings to the exclusion of the other. As for the hadith of Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, he said therein: Some men among us had surplus lands, and they used to let them for a half, a third and a fourth. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever has land, let him cultivate it and grant it to his brother; and if he refuses, let him hold it.' So in this hadith, he permitted them nothing but to cultivate them themselves or to grant them to whom they loved; he did not permit them in this hadith anything else. So it may be that that prohibition was to the effect that it not be let for a third, nor a fourth, nor for dirhams, nor for dinars, nor for anything else - so that what is intended by that prohibition is the hiring of land. And a group has held to the dislike of hiring land for gold and silver."
"5559","5930 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Dinar informed us, he said: Tawus used to dislike the letting of land for gold and silver. So here Tawus dislikes the letting of land for gold and silver, but sees no harm in giving it for some of what comes out; and that will come hereafter, Allah Almighty willing. So if the prohibition in the hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, fell upon letting fundamentally - for something of what comes out and for other than that - this is a meaning which both groups contradict. And it may be that the prohibition occurred for another meaning. So we looked: did anyone narrate from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, on that anything pointing to the meaning for which the prohibition was?"
"5560","5931 - And behold Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' al-Madani narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sa'd, from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, that it reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that men let their farms for half of what comes out of them, a third of it, and for the madhayanat. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning that: 'Whoever has land, let him cultivate it; and if he does not cultivate it, let him grant it to his brother; and if he does not do so, let him hold it.'"
"5561","5932 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Hisham ibn Sa'd informed me that Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki narrated to him, he said: I heard Jabir ibn 'Abdillah saying: In the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we used to take land for a third or a fourth with the madhayanat, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited that."
"5562","5933 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: We used to practise mukhabarah in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), obtaining such-and-such. So he said: 'Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or grant it to his brother; otherwise let him cultivate it.' So Abu al-Zubayr informed in this, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, of the meaning for which the prohibition occurred: that it was only for something they used to obtain in the hiring, so the prohibition came on account of that. And it may be that the meaning of the hadith of Thabit ibn al-Dahhak, may Allah be pleased with him, which we mentioned is likewise. As for the hadith of Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, it has come with different wordings on account of which it is confused. As for the hadith of Ibn 'Umar from him, it is like the hadith of Thabit ibn al-Dahhak, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited sharecropping. So it admits what we described of the meanings of the hadith of Thabit, as we mentioned and explained. And as for whoever narrated it like what Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated, it also admits what we described of what the hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, admits. Then we looked thereafter: do we find from Rafi' a meaning pointing us to the aspect of the prohibition of that, why it was?"
"5563","5934 - And behold Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us that Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari informed them, from Hanzalah ibn Qays al-Zuraqi, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, he said: We - Banu Harithah - were the people of Madinah with most fields, and we used to let land on (condition) that what the madhayanat and the rabi' watered would be ours, and what the channels watered would be theirs. So sometimes this would be safe and this perish, and sometimes this would perish and this be safe; and we had at that time neither gold nor silver with which to know that. So we asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he prohibited us."
"5564","5935 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Hanzalah ibn Qays al-Zuraqi narrated to us that he heard Rafi' ibn Khadij saying: We were the people of Madinah with most fields, and we used to say to the one with whom we sharecropped: To you this piece, and to us this piece - cultivate it for us. So sometimes this piece would produce and this would produce nothing, and sometimes this would produce and this would produce nothing. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited us that; but as for (hiring) for silver coin (wariq), he did not prohibit us."
"5565","5936 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from Ya'la ibn Hakim, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, he said: We used to practise muhaqalah in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and muhaqalah is that a man lets his land for a third, a fourth, or specified food. While I was one day (thus), one of my uncles came to me and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited us from a matter that was beneficial to us, though obedience to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more beneficial. He said: 'Whoever has land, let him grant it to his brother, and let him not let it for a third, nor a fourth, nor specified food.' So Rafi' explained in this hadith how they used to sharecrop, and the meaning of his hadith returned to the meaning of the hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him. And it is established that the prohibition in both hadiths was only because each group of the landowners and the cultivators would allot a portion of the land, so that to him would belong what comes out of it of crop - if safe, to him; if ruined, upon him. And this is of what is agreed upon its invalidity. So this has expounded the meaning of the hadith of Rafi': that the prohibition mentioned therein was for the meaning we described, not for hiring land for a portion of what comes out of it. And others denied Rafi' what he narrated of that, informing that he did not retain the beginning of the hadith."
"5566","5937 - So 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Abu 'Ubaydah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ammar, from al-Walid ibn Abi al-Walid, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he said: May Allah forgive Rafi' ibn Khadij; by Allah, I knew the hadith better than him. Two men of the Ansar came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) having fought each other, and he said: 'If this is your state, then do not let farms.' So he heard his saying: 'Do not let farms.' So here Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, informs that the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), 'Do not let farms' - the prohibition which Rafi' heard - was not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by way of prohibition; it was only out of dislike for evil occurring between them. And there has been narrated from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, also something of that."
"5567","5938 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan, Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, he said: I said to him: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, would that you abandon mukhabarah, for they claim the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited it. So he said: The most knowledgeable of them informed me - meaning Ibn 'Abbas - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not prohibit it, but said: 'That one of you grant his brother his land is better for him than that he take upon it a known rent.'"
"5568","5939 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr, and he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof. So Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, explained that what was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that was not for prohibition; rather he intended gentleness with them. And it may also be that he disliked for them the taking of rent because of what occurred between the two men in the hadith of Zayd, saying: 'That one of you grant his brother his land is better for him than that he take upon it a known rent' - because what had occurred between those two men of evil was only concerning the rent due to one of them upon his fellow. So he saw that the grant, which occasions nothing of that between them, is better for them than the sharecropping which occasions between them the like of that. And some have brought the hadith of Rafi' upon the wording of this hadith of Ibn 'Abbas."
"5569","5940 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, he said: I heard Mujahid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited us from a matter that was beneficial to us, and commanded us with better than it, saying: 'Whoever has land, let him cultivate it or grant it.' He said: So I mentioned that to Tawus, and he said: Ibn 'Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only said: 'His granting it to his brother is better for him' or 'his granting it is better.' So it may be that the aspect of this hadith is upon that also: his saying, 'He prohibited us from a matter that was beneficial to us,' means what Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned that Rafi' heard; and 'he commanded us with such,' what Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated. So in all that was heard, in reality, there was no prohibition of letting land for a third and a fourth. And there has been narrated from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas and Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, also on the prohibition of that, that it was only for some of the meanings whose mention preceded."
"5570","5941 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib informed us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Ikrimah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Ibn Lubaybah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, he said: The people used to let farms for what would be upon the water-channel, and for what would be watered by the water around the well. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited that, saying: 'Let them for gold and silver.'"
"5571","5942 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ghalib narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Nafi', that Rafi' ibn Khadij informed 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar - while he was leaning on my body - that his uncles came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then returned saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited the letting of farms. So Ibn 'Umar said: We knew that he was a farm-owner letting it in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on (condition) that to him belongs what is in the rabi' of the water-channel from which the water gushes, and a portion of the straw - I do not know what the straw is. So Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, explained in this hadith why the Prophet's prohibition was, and that it was only because they used to stipulate what is upon the rabi' of the water-channel - and that is invalid in the saying of all people. And Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, took the prohibition as possibly being upon that meaning also. And the hadith of Sa'd added over the rest of these hadiths the Prophet's permission of hiring land for gold and silver. So it has become clear why the Prophet's prohibition of sharecropping in the preceding reports was, and what of that he prohibited. And there is established in none of them the prohibition of hiring land for some of what comes out, when it is a third, a fourth or the like. And a group has argued for the people of the first saying with that which..."
"5572","5943 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, from his father, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from Ibn Hurmuz, from Asad ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij, he hearing him mention that they were prevented from muhaqalah - which is that one let land for some of what is in it."
"5573","5944 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard 'Amr ibn Dinar saying: I heard Ibn 'Umar saying: We used to practise mukhabarah and saw no harm in that, until Rafi' ibn Khadij claimed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited it, so we abandoned it on account of his saying."
"5574","5945 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi informed us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me, he said: 'Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited mukhabarah, muzabanah and muhaqalah. And mukhabarah is: for a third, a fourth and a half of the white (unsown) land. And muzabanah is: the sale of fresh dates on the palm-tops for dried dates, and the sale of grapes on the vine for raisins. And muhaqalah is: the sale of standing grain upon its stalks for food."
"5575","5946 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Salim ibn Hayyan, from Sa'id ibn Mina', from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited muhaqalah, muzabanah and mukhabarah."
"5576","5947 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata' and Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"5577","5948 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from his uncle Wasi' ibn Habban, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited muhaqalah and muzabanah."
"5578","5949 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq informed us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from Zayd ibn Thabit, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"5579","5950 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Yunus ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn 'Abdillah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"5580","5951 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Hafs al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Sa'd ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, he said: 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof. He said: And muhaqalah is partnership in the crop, and muzabanah is dates for dates upon the palm-tops. They said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited muhaqalah, which is the hiring of land for a third and a fourth, and also prohibited mukhabarah, which is likewise. It is said to them: As for what you mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his prohibition of muhaqalah, you have spoken truly, and we agree with you upon the soundness of its coming. But as for your interpreting it as sharecropping for a third and a fourth, this is an interpretation from you, and you have no proof from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that pointing that its interpretation is as you interpreted. And it may be with us what you mentioned, and it may be as your opponent said: that it is the sale of wheat by measure for the wheat of this field whose measure is unknown. That, with us and with you, is invalid; and this is more akin to that, for it is conjoined with muzabanah - and muzabanah is the sale of measured dates for what is on the palm-tops of dates. So this hadith admits what both groups interpreted it upon, and there is no proof in it for either group against the other. And there have come reports other than these reports, in them the permission of sharecropping for a third and a fourth."
"5581","5952 - And among them is what Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from al-Hakam, from Abu al-Qasim - and he is Miqsam - from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Khaybar for a half, then he sent Ibn Rawahah, and he divided with them."
"5582","5953 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dealt with the people of Khaybar for half of what comes out of the crop."
"5583","5954 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The farms used to be let in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on (condition) that to the landowner belongs what is upon the rabi' of the water-channel of the crop, and a portion of the straw - I do not know how much it is. 5955 - Nafi' said: Then Rafi' ibn Khadij came, while I was with him, and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Khaybar to the Jews on (condition) that they work it and cultivate it for half of what comes out of dates or crop."
"5584","5956 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awn al-Ziyadi - and he is Ibn Muhammad ibn 'Awn - narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, he said: Allah granted Khaybar as booty, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left them as they were, and made it between him and them; then he sent Ibn Rawahah, and he assessed it upon them."
"5585","5957 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq informed us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, the like thereof. So in these reports the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Khaybar for half of its dates and its crop. So thereby is established the permissibility of sharecropping and irrigation-partnership, and that does not contradict what preceded of our mention of the hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, and Rafi' and Thabit, may Allah be pleased with them both, given what we mentioned of their realities. Then an arguer argued on that, saying: These reports have also been opposed by what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the prohibition of selling fruits before they exist - of what we described in the chapter of selling fruits before their soundness appears. He said: Since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited purchase with fruits before they exist, included in that is hiring with them before they exist. So just as sale with them before their existence was void, hiring with them before their existence is likewise. Do you not see that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited the sale of what you do not have, so hiring with that was impermissible since purchase with it was impermissible? So likewise, since purchase with what did not exist was impermissible, hiring with it is also impermissible. It is said to him: Had there not been narrated in these reports which we mentioned, on hiring sharecroppers for a third and a fourth, it would have been as you mentioned. But since there was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) their permission, and the Muslims acted upon them after him, it may be that hiring with what did not exist is not included in purchase with what did not exist, and is excepted from that, even if not explained in the hadith - just as salam was permitted, and the prohibition of selling what you do not have did not prohibit it; rather the prohibition in that fell upon the sale of what you do not have other than salam. So likewise it may be that the prohibition of selling fruits before they exist is upon what is besides sharecropping with them and irrigation-partnership over them. And the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after him acted with sharecropping and irrigation-partnership."
"5586","5958 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, he said: I heard my father mentioning from Musa ibn Talhah, he said: 'Uthman granted to a group of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam, Sa'd ibn Malik and Usamah. My neighbour among them, Sa'd ibn Malik and Ibn Mas'ud, used to give their lands for a third and a fourth."
"5587","5959 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, he said: I asked Musa ibn Talhah about sharecropping, and he said: 'Uthman granted 'Abdullah land, granted Sa'd land, granted Khabbab land and granted Suhayb land; and both my neighbours used to sharecrop for a third and a fourth."
"5588","5960 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us that Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari informed them, from Isma'il ibn Abi Hakim, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, sent Ya'la ibn Munabbih to Yemen, commanding him to give them the white (unsown) land on (condition) that if the cattle, the seed and the iron are from 'Umar, then to him two-thirds and to them a third; and if the cattle, the seed and the iron are from them, then to 'Umar a half and to them a half. And he commanded him to give them the palms and the vines on (condition) that to 'Umar two-thirds and to them a third."
"5589","5961 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad informed us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to us, from Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali, that he said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, used to give land for a half."
"5590","5962 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us that al-Hajjaj informed them, from 'Uthman ibn 'Abdillah ibn Mawhab, from Musa ibn Talhah, that he said: Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, may Allah be pleased with him, used to let land for a third and a fourth."
"5591","5963 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, that Mu'adh, may Allah be pleased with him, came to Yemen while they practised mukhabarah, and he confirmed them upon that."
"5592","5964 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, that Mu'adh, may Allah be pleased with him, when he came to Yemen, used to let land or farms for a third or a fourth. And he said: He came to Yemen while they did it, and he carried it out for them."
"5593","5965 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad al-Kufi narrated to me, from Kulayb ibn Wa'il, he said: I said to Ibn 'Umar: A man with land and water but neither seed nor cattle came to me; I took his land for a half and cultivated it with my seed and my cattle, sharing half with him? He said: Good."
"5594","5966 - Then the Successors after them differed on that. So Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Hammad, that he said: I asked Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, Sa'id ibn Jubayr, Salim ibn 'Abdillah and Mujahid about letting land for a third and a fourth, and they disliked it."
"5595","5967 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud informed us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Hammad, that he said: I asked Mujahid and Salim about letting land for a third and a fourth, and they disliked it. And I asked Tawus about that, and he saw no harm in it. He said: So I mentioned that to Mujahid - and he used to honour and revere him - and he said: He (Tawus) cultivates."
"5596","5968 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah informed us, from Mansur, he said: Ibrahim used to dislike letting land for a third and a fourth."
"5597","5969 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar informed us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, the like thereof."
"5598","5970 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah informed us, from Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, the like thereof."
"5599","5971 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad informed us, from Qays ibn Sa'd, they informed them from 'Ata', the like thereof."
"5600","5972 - Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil and Yunus ibn 'Ubayd, from al-Hasan, that he used to dislike a man letting land to his brother for a third and a fourth. As for the aspect of this chapter through rational examination: it is as the people of the first saying said, that that is not permissible in sharecropping, contracting and irrigation-partnership except for dirhams, dinars and goods. That is because those who permitted irrigation-partnership in that claimed they likened it to mudarabah - which is wealth a man gives to a man to work with for a half, a third or a fourth; all have agreed upon its permissibility, and it stands in place of hiring for known wealth. They said: So likewise irrigation-partnership: the palms given stand in place of the capital in mudarabah, and what arises from them of dates is like what arises from wealth of profit. So our proof against them in that was that in mudarabah, profit is established only after the safety of the capital and its reaching the owner's hands - and sharecropping and irrigation-partnership were not seen to do that therein. Do you not see that irrigation-partnership, in the saying of whoever permits it, if the palms bore fruit and the fruit was picked from them, then the palms burned while the fruit was safe, that fruit would be between the palm-owner and the irrigator according to what they stipulated therein - and the absence of the given palms does not prevent that, just as the absence of the capital in mudarabah prevents profit. And irrigation-partnership and sharecropping, when contracted without a known term, are invalid; they are permissible only to a known term. But mudarabah is permissible without a known term; and the mudarib, after taking the wealth as mudarabah, may refrain from working with it whenever he wishes, and is not compelled to that; and the wealth-owner too may take the wealth from his hand whenever he wishes, whether that mudarib wills or refuses. But irrigation-partnership and sharecropping are not so, for we saw that the irrigator, when he refuses to work after the irrigation-partnership contract occurs, is compelled to that; and if the palm-owner wants to take them from him and annul the irrigation-partnership, that is not his until the term they contracted upon expires. So the mudarabah contract is a contract that does not oblige either the wealth-owner or the mudarib; rather the mudarib works with that wealth so long as he and the wealth-owner agree upon that. But irrigation-partnership compels each of the palm-owner and the irrigator to fulfil what its contract necessitates. And mudarabah resembles partnership in what we mentioned, while irrigation-partnership resembles hiring in what we described. Then we returned to the ruling of hiring, how is it, to know thereby how is the ruling of irrigation-partnership which resembled it in the respect we described. We saw hirings occur upon different aspects. Among them are hirings for reaching a known distance for a known wage - these are permissible, and this is an aspect of hirings. And among them is what occurs for a known work, such as sewing this shirt and the like, for a known wage - that too is permissible. And among them is what occurs for a known term, such as a man hiring a man to serve him a month for a known wage - that too is permissible. So in all hirings there was need to be aware of what the contract occurred upon thereof; it was permissible in all that only over something known - whether a known distance, a known work, or known days. And these known things in themselves: their countervalues may not be unknown; rather the ruling of their countervalues was made as their ruling. So there was need for them to be known, just as that which is the countervalue thereof needs to be known. But mudarabah occurred over work with wealth - unknown, and not to a known term; so the work therein was unknown, and the countervalue thereof unknown. So it is established in these things we described, of hirings and mudarabahs, that the ruling of each is the ruling of its countervalue: what its countervalue is known, it may only be in itself known; and what in itself is unknown, its countervalue may be unknown. Then we saw irrigation-partnership, sharecropping and contracting: none of them is permissible except to a known term in a known thing. So examination accordingly requires the countervalue thereof to be permissible only if known, and its ruling to be as the ruling of its countervalue - just as the ruling of the things we mentioned, of hirings and mudarabahs, was the ruling of their countervalues. So it is established by sound examination that irrigation-partnership and sharecropping are permissible only for dirhams, dinars and the like of goods. And all this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, may Allah be pleased with him, in this chapter. As for Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them both, they held to the permissibility of them both together, abandoning examination in that, and following what we narrated in this chapter of the reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and from his Companions after him, imitating them in that."
"5601","Chapter 2: One who cultivates in people's land without their permission - what is their ruling on that? And what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that. 5973 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Ata', from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever sows a crop in people's land without their permission has nothing of the crop, and his expenditure on that shall be returned to him.' Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to (the view) that whoever sows a crop in people's land without their command, that crop belongs to the landowners, and they bear for the cultivator what he spent on it - adducing this hadith for that. Others differed with them on that, saying: The landowners have the choice: if they wish, they leave the cultivator to take that crop of his, making him liable for any diminution of their land if that crop of his diminished the land in anything; and if they wish, they prevent the cultivator from that, bearing for him the value of that crop of his uprooted. And they had of the proof in that that this hadith has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon other than what they mentioned on that..."
"5602","5974 - And it is as Ahmad ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Ata', from Rafi' ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever sows in people's land without their permission, to him his expenditure, and he has nothing of the crop.' And Yahya ibn Adam also narrated this hadith from Sharik and Qays, both from Abu Ishaq, mentioning it from them in the Book of Kharaj, as Ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us also - not as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us. So the meaning of this hadith with us is other than the meaning of what al-Himmani narrated, for what al-Himmani narrated is his saying: 'He has nothing of the crop, and his expenditure on that shall be returned to him.' The aspect of that is that another gives him the expenditure he spent on that, so that the crop belongs to him (the other) - not through what is given of that. And this is impossible with us, for the expenditure expended on that crop is not subsisting, nor does it have a subsisting substitute - since it was only paid in wages of workers and other than that of what the cultivator did for himself. So it is impossible that that be due to him upon the landowner except with a recompense which the landowner obtains from him in that. But the basis of the hadith with us - and Allah knows best - is only upon what Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, not upon what al-Himmani narrated on that. And its aspect with us is that the cultivator has nothing in the crop to take for himself and own as he owns the crop he sows in his own land or in another's land of one who permitted him to sow therein - but he takes his expenditure and his seed, and gives the rest in charity. Such is the aspect of this hadith with us on that, and Allah knows best. And Yahya ibn Adam mentioned that from Hafs ibn Ghiyath also."
"5603","5975 - And among the proof of the soundness of that also is what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Abu Yusuf, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yahya ibn 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever revives a dead land, it is his; and the unjust root has no right.' 5976 - 'Urwah said: This man who narrated to me this hadith narrated to me that he saw palms being cut at their roots with axes."
"5604","5977 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yahya ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from a man of Banu Bayadah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the like of that also. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the cutting of palms planted without right after they had grown in the land, and did not make them for the landowners, obliging them the liability of what was spent on them? So that proved that the crop sown in the land is more worthy of being likewise: that it be uprooted and given to the crop-owner, like the palms we mentioned - unless the landowner wishes to prevent that and bear the value of the crop and the palms uprooted and pulled out, in which case it is his..."
"5605","5978 - And what we mentioned of that is also proved by what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Wasil ibn Abi Jamil, from Mujahid, he said: Four men partnered in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). One of them said: Upon me the seed; another said: Upon me the labour; another said: Upon me the land; and another said: Upon me the plough-oxen. So they sowed, then harvested. Then they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he assigned the crop to the seed-owner, assigned to the labour-owner a wage, assigned to the plough-owner a dirham for every day, and nullified the land in that. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he invalidated this sharecropping, did not assign the crop to the landowner; rather he assigned it to the seed-owner. And that is also proved by what the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their Successors after them judged concerning one who builds in people's land without their command."
"5606","5979 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us that 'Amir al-Ahwal informed them, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said concerning a man who builds in a house, then its people come and claim it: If he built by their command, to him his expenditure; but if he built without their permission, to him its demolition. And there has..."
"5607","5980 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, the like thereof."
"5608","5981 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah informed us, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Shurayh, the like of that exactly. And there has..."
"5609","5982 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, he said: And Hammad ibn Salamah said, from Humayd al-Tawil, that he informed them that 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, may Allah have mercy on him, wrote with the like of that concerning one who builds in people's house, and concerning one who plants in people's land, with the like of that also exactly. Do you not see that they all assigned the demolition to the builder, and did not assign it to the landowner? So the crop, by examination, is likewise. And what the meaning of the hadith of Rafi' ibn Khadij which we narrated in this chapter carried us to is more worthy than what he who differed with us carried it to, so that that agrees with what the Bayadi man narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also, and they do not contradict in that. And we have narrated from Rafi' ibn Khadij, in the chapter of sharecropping before this chapter, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man cultivating for him, asking him about it. He said: It is my crop, the land belongs to the family of so-and-so, and the seed from me for half of what comes out. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'You have dealt in usury; take your expenditure.' That was not upon the meaning: take your expenditure from the landowner, for the landowner did not command him to spend for himself. Rather its meaning is: take your expenditure from what has come out of the crop of this crop, and give the rest in charity. So what we narrated from Rafi', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning one who sows in another's land - his assigning to him his expenditure - is likewise. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan in this chapter, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"5610","Book 21: Book of Pre-emption (Kitab al-Shuf'ah). Chapter 1: Pre-emption by neighbourhood. 5983 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me that Abu al-Zubayr informed him that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdillah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Pre-emption is in every partnership in land, quarter or walled-garden. It is not lawful that he sell until he offers to his partner, who takes or leaves.' Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to (the view) that pre-emption is only through partnership in land, walled-garden or quarter, and is not due through neighbourhood - adducing this hadith for that. Others differed with them on that, saying: Pre-emption, in what you described, is due to the partner who has not divided; then after him it is due to the partner who divided, through the road in which partnership remains for him; then after him it is due to the adjoining neighbour. And among the proof for them in that was that this report only contains that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Pre-emption is in every partnership in land, quarter or walled-garden' - and he did not say: pre-emption is only in every partnership. So that is no negation that pre-emption be due through other than partnership. Rather he only informed in this hadith that it is due in every partnership, and did not negate that it be due in other than it. And there has come from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that which added over the meaning of this hadith."
"5611","5984 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'The neighbour has most right to his neighbour's pre-emption; and if he is absent, he is awaited when their road is one.'"
"5612","5985 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik informed us, he said: 'Ata' narrated to us, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned the like thereof."
"5613","5986 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik informed us, from 'Ata', from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof. So in this hadith is the imposition of pre-emption in a sale in which there is no partnership, through partnership in the road. So neither of these two hadiths is made to contradict the other; rather both are established and acted upon. So the hadith of Abu al-Zubayr contains information of the ruling of pre-emption for the partner in that of which part was sold. And the hadith of 'Ata' on that contains information of the ruling of pre-emption in the sold property in which no one has partnership, through the road. And the people of the first saying said: There has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that which negates what you claimed. So they mentioned on that that which..."
"5614","5987 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged with pre-emption in what was undivided; then when the boundaries fall, there is no pre-emption."
"5615","5988 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, the like thereof."
"5616","5989 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Qutaylah al-Madani narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, the like thereof."
"5617","5990 - Sa'd ibn 'Abdillah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abdillah ibn Abi Salamah al-Majishun narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof. They said: So this hadith negated that pre-emption be due when the boundaries are delimited. So among the proof against them was that this hadith - upon the principle of the arguer with it against us - does not necessitate proof, for the most reliable of Malik's companions, may Allah's mercy be upon them, only narrated it from Malik disconnected, not raising it to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5618","5991 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir and al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, they said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged with pre-emption in what was undivided; then when the boundaries fall, there is no pre-emption."
"5619","5992 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah, the like thereof. So this hadith was severed (maqtu'), and the severed - with them - establishes no proof. Then even if this hadith were established and its chain connected, there would be nothing in it - with us - contradicting the hadith we mentioned from 'Ata', from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him. For what is in this hadith is only the saying of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged with pre-emption in what was undivided. Thereby he was informing of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged. Then he said thereafter: 'Then when the boundaries fall, there is no pre-emption' - and that was a saying from his own opinion, which he did not narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This hadith would only be a proof against whoever holds to the obligatoriness of pre-emption by neighbourhood if the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had said: 'Pre-emption is in what was undivided; then when the boundaries fall, there is no pre-emption.' Then that would be a negation from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that in what was divided there be pre-emption. But Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, only informed therein from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he knew of his judgment; then he negated pre-emption by his own opinion in what he did not know therein a ruling from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while another knew it. Then Ma'mar narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri, differing with Malik in its text and in its chain."
"5620","5993 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyadah narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged with pre-emption in everything undivided; then when the boundaries fall and the roads are diverted, there is no pre-emption."
"5621","5994 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, and he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof. So in this hadith is the negation of pre-emption after the falling of boundaries and the diversion of roads - and that is a proof of its establishment before the diversion of roads, even if the boundaries were delimited. So this hadith agreed with the hadith of 'Abd al-Malik from 'Ata', adding over what Malik narrated; so it is more worthy than it. And the hadith of Malik may also admit that by the falling of boundaries by whose falling pre-emption is negated, he meant in houses and roads. Then the sold property would have neither partnership in it for anyone nor in its road. Then the meaning of this hadith would be like the meaning of the hadith of Ma'mar - and that is most worthy of what it is taken upon, so that it and the hadith of Ma'mar do not contradict. And Ibn Jurayj narrated from al-Zuhri that which agrees with what Ma'mar narrated."
"5622","5995 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'When the roads are delimited, there is no pre-emption.' If one says: It is established by what you mentioned that pre-emption is due through partnership in houses and lands, and through partnership in the road to that - so whence did you impose pre-emption through neighbourhood? It is said to him: I imposed it through that which..."
"5623","5996 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Bahr al-Qattan and Ahmad ibn Janab narrated to us, they said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'The neighbour of the house has most right to the house.'"
"5624","5997 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ali and Ahmad narrated to us, they said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'The neighbour of the house has most right to the pre-emption of the house.'"
"5625","5998 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof."
"5626","5999 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof."
"5627","6000 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Humayd and Qatadah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof - and he did not mention Samurah."
"5628","6001 - Ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Janab narrated to us. (Ha') 6002 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Bahr and Ahmad ibn Janab narrated to us, they said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"5629","6003 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan - he is al-Thawri - narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from one who heard 'Ali and 'Abdullah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged by neighbourhood."
"5630","6004 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir informed us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Hayyan, from his father, from 'Amr ibn Hurayth, the like thereof. So in these reports is the obligatoriness of pre-emption through neighbourhood. If one says: It may be that this neighbour is a partner, for the partner may be called a neighbour. It is said to him: There is nothing in the hadith pointing to anything of what you mentioned; rather there has been narrated from Abu Rafi' that which proved that that neighbour is the one who has no partnership."
"5631","6005 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from 'Amr ibn al-Sharid, he said: al-Miswar ibn Makhramah came to me, placing his hand upon one of my shoulders, saying: Come with us to Sa'd. So we came to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas in his house, and Abu Rafi' came saying to al-Miswar: Will you not command this one - meaning Sa'd - to buy from me two rooms in my house? So Sa'd said: By Allah, I will not add for you over four hundred dinars in instalments or deferred. So he said: Glory be to Allah, I was offered for it five hundred dinars cash; and were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: 'The neighbour has most right to his saqab,' I would not sell to you. So what we mentioned proved that that neighbour whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant is the neighbour whom the common people know. And who granted you that the partner is called a neighbour? And where did you find this in the languages of the Arabs? If he says: Because I saw the wife called her husband's neighbour. It is said to him: You spoke truly; the wife was called her husband's neighbour - not because her flesh mixes with his flesh, nor her blood with his blood, but because of her closeness to him. So likewise the neighbour was called a neighbour because of his closeness to his neighbour, not because of his mixing with him in what he neighbours him in. And you claimed that the reports are upon their outward (sense) - so how did you abandon the outward in this, along with the proofs, attaching to other than it with which there is no proof? Then there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also, of his imposing pre-emption through neighbourhood and his explaining that neighbourhood, that which..."
"5632","6006 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from 'Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father al-Sharid ibn Suwayd, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, a land in which no one has a portion nor a partner except neighbourhood was sold. He said: 'The neighbour has most right to his saqab.' So the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), 'The neighbour has most right to his saqab,' was an answer to al-Sharid's question to him about a separate land in which no one has a right nor a road. So what we mentioned proved that the adjoining neighbour: pre-emption is due to him by right of his neighbourhood. So it is established by what we narrated of the reports in this chapter that pre-emption is due through each of three meanings: through partnership in the sale - sold thereof what was sold; through partnership in the road to it; and through adjacency to it. So none of them ought to be abandoned, nor some of them taken to contradict; since they have come out in agreement from the aspects we mentioned, as we explained and clarified in this chapter. If one says: You have made these three pre-emptors through the causes you mentioned - so why did you impose pre-emption for some of them to the exclusion of others when they are present and claim it, preferring the right of some of them therein over the right of others, rather than making it for them all together since they are all pre-emptors? It is said to him: Because the partner in the sold thing is a co-owner in it and in the road to it; so with him of the right in the road is like that which is with the partner in the road. And with him is the intermixture of his ownership with the sold thing, which is not with the partner in the road - so he is more worthy than him and than the adjoining neighbour. And with the partner in the road is partnership in the road and adjacency to the sold thing; so with him of the causes of pre-emption is like that which is with the adjoining neighbour, and with him also is what is not with the adjoining neighbour, of the intermixture of his ownership-right in the road with his ownership therein. So for that he was - with us - more worthy of pre-emption than him. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all. And that has been narrated from Shurayh."
"5633","6007 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Hisham, from Muhammad, from Shurayh; and Ash'ath - I think - from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, he said: The co-owner (khalit) has most right than the pre-emptor, and the pre-emptor has most right than any besides him."
"5634","6008 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Salim informed me, he said: Hushaym informed us, from Yunus; and Hisham from Muhammad. (Ha') 6009 - And Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, from Hisham, from Muhammad, from Shurayh, the like thereof."
"5635","6010 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Jabir, from 'Amir, from Shurayh, he said: Pre-emption is two pre-emptions: pre-emption for the neighbour, and pre-emption for the partner. If one says: There has been narrated from 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, contrary to this - then he mentioned that which..."
"5636","6011 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Manzur ibn Tha'labah, from Aban ibn 'Uthman, he said: 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: There is no mukabalah: when the boundaries fall, there is no pre-emption. It is said to him: This has been narrated from 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, as you mentioned; but there is nothing in it - with us - as proof for you, for it may be that by that he meant: when the boundaries are delimited of all the rights, and the road is included in that. Then that would agree with what we narrated from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, in this chapter: 'When the boundaries fall and the roads are diverted, there is no pre-emption.' And had it been upon what you interpreted it upon, Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Abu Rafi' would have contradicted him in that, in what we narrated from them in what preceded of this chapter. And there has been narrated from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, also on that that which..."
"5637","6012 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Khalid ibn Mawhab narrated to us, he said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from 'Awn ibn 'Ubaydillah ibn Abi Rafi', from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdillah ibn 'Umar, he said: 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When the boundaries fall and the people know their rights, there is no pre-emption. So this agreed with what we narrated from 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, admitting what the hadith of 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, admitted. And there has been narrated from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, contrary to that also."
"5638","6013 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs, that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Shurayh to judge with pre-emption for the adjoining neighbour. And there has been narrated also from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that which points that pre-emption is due through partnership in the road."
"5639","6014 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Musa narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah al-Sukkari, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rufay', from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'The partner is a pre-emptor, and pre-emption is in everything.'"
"5640","6015 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged with pre-emption in everything. So since the partner in the road is called a partner, he is included in that. If one says: There is no holding to this hadith, for it imposes pre-emption in everything of animals and other than them, while you do not impose pre-emption in animals. It is said to him: This is not upon what you mentioned; rather the meaning of 'pre-emption in everything' is: in houses, estates and lands. And the proof of that is what was narrated from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"5641","6016 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: Ma'n ibn 'Isa narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: There is no pre-emption in animals."
"5642","Book 22: Book of Hirings. Chapter 1: Hiring for teaching the Qur'an - is that permissible or not? And what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that. 6017 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi al-Safar, from 'Amir al-Sha'bi, from Kharijah ibn al-Salt, from his uncle, that he said: We came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), passing by a clan of the Arab clans. They said to us: You have come from this learned man with good; so do you have medicine, a charm, or anything? For with us is an insane man in fetters. He said: So we said: Yes. They brought him, and I began reciting over him the Opening of the Book (al-Fatihah) for three days, morning and evening, gathering my saliva then spitting. Then it was as if he was untied from a bond. So they gave me a reward, but I said: No, until I ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I asked him, and he said: 'Eat; for by my life, whoever eats through a false charm, you have eaten a true one.'"
"5643","And there has: 6018 - Abu al-'Awwam Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Abu al-Mutawakkil al-Naji, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were in a raid, passing by a clan of the Arab clans. They said: Is there among you one who charms? For the chief of the clan was stung or something befell him. He said: So a man charmed him with the Opening of the Book, and he recovered; then he was given a flock of sheep but refused to accept it. So he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said to him: With what did you charm him? He said: With the Opening of the Book. He said: And what makes you know that it is a charm? He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Take them, and strike for me with you therein a share.' So a group argued with these reports, saying: There is no harm in reward for teaching the Qur'an. Others differed with them on that, disliking reward for teaching the Qur'an just as reward for teaching prayer is disliked. And among the proof for them against the people of the first saying in that was that the first reports on that: the reward mentioned therein was not for teaching the Qur'an; rather it was for charms over whose hiring the Qur'an was not intended. And likewise we too say: There is no harm in hiring for charms and all treatments, even though we know that the one hired for that may include in what he charms with some of the Qur'an - because it is not upon people that some of them charm others. So when they are hired therein to do what they are not obliged to do, that is permissible. But teaching the Qur'an: it is obligatory upon people that some of them teach others, for in that is conveyance from Allah Almighty - except that whoever of them knows it, that suffices for the rest of them, like prayer over funerals: it is only an obligation upon all people, except that whoever of them does that suffices for the rest. And had a man hired a man to pray over a deceased relative of his, that would not be permissible, because he only hired him to do what he is obliged to do. So likewise people's teaching one another the Qur'an is an obligation upon them - except that whoever of them does it, his doing it suffices for the rest. So when some of them hire others for teaching that, that hiring of theirs and his hiring him is void, because he only hired him to discharge an obligation due upon him to Allah Almighty, and in what he does for himself - for the obligation falls from him only by his doing it. And hirings are only permissible, and countervalues owned through them, in what the hired do for those who hire them. If one says: Has anything been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pointing to what you mentioned on preventing hiring for teaching the Qur'an? It is said to him: Yes, there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that that he said: 'Do not eat through the Qur'an.' And from 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: I used to teach some people of the People of the Bench the Qur'an, then a man of them gave me a bow to accept it for the sake of Allah Almighty. So I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said to me: 'If you wish that Allah collar you with a bow of fire, then accept it.' And we have mentioned all that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with its chains in what preceded of this book of ours, in the chapter of marriage for a chapter of the Qur'an, in the Book of Marriage. Then there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that also that which..."
"5644","6019 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Mas'ud Sa'id ibn Iyas al-Jurayri, from Abu al-'Ala' Yazid ibn 'Abdillah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from his brother Mutarrif ibn al-Shikhkhir, from 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Take a caller (to prayer) who does not take a wage for his call.' So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked the call with a wage. And there has been narrated on that also from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that which..."
"5645","6020 - Ahmad ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad ibn Hafs ibn 'Umar al-Taymi informed us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Yahya al-Bakka', that a man said to Ibn 'Umar: I love you for Allah. So Ibn 'Umar said to him: But I hate you for Allah, for you seek a wage in your call, taking a wage for the call. So it is established by what we mentioned that hiring for the call is disliked; so taking reward for teaching the Qur'an is likewise, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded with conveyance from Allah, even a verse of Allah's Book, and Allah obliged His Prophet with conveyance from Him, saying: 'O Messenger, convey what was sent down to you from your Lord; and if you do not, then you have not conveyed His message; and Allah protects you from the people.' And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the like of that also in that which..."
"5646","6021 - Abu Bakrah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq together narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Hassan ibn 'Atiyyah, from Abu Kabshah al-Saluli, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Convey from me even a verse of Allah's Book; and narrate from the Children of Israel, with no harm in that; and whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.' So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obliged his community in this hadith with conveyance from him. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distinguished between conveyance from him and narration from others, saying: 'And narrate from the Children of Israel, with no harm' - meaning: with no harm upon you in not narrating from them on that. So taking reward for that is taking reward for an obligation; and whoever takes reward for a work he does in what Allah obliged its doing upon him, that is forbidden for him, for he only does it for himself, to discharge thereby an obligation upon him. But whoever takes reward for a work he does for another - of charming or other than it, even if with Qur'an, treatment or the like - that is permissible, and taking reward for it is lawful. So there are sound, by what we mentioned, the meanings of what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter, of prohibition and of permission, and they do not contradict so as to conflict. And all this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them."
"5647","Chapter 2: Reward for cupping - is it wholesome for the cupper or not? 6022 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Isma'il al-Kharraz narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn 'Abdillah ibn Qariz, that al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid narrated to them that Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'The earning of the cupper is filthy.'"
"5648","6023 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Ibrahim ibn 'Abdillah ibn Qariz narrated to me, he said: al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid narrated to me, he said: I heard Rafi' ibn Khadij narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof."
"5649","6024 - And Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq together narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Rabah ibn Abi Ma'ruf narrated to us, from 'Ata', from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Among ill-gotten gain is the earning of the cupper.'"
"5650","6025 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Shihab narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Layla, from 'Ata', from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"5651","6026 - And 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Bayan al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Ziyad narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited the earning of the cupper."
"5652","6027 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, he said: 'Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah narrated to us, that he said: My father bought a cupper and broke his cupping instruments. So I said to him: O father, why did you break them? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited the price of blood. Abu Ja'far said: There is no proof in this of the prohibition of the cupper's earning; rather we brought it only lest one imagine we neglected it. Rather in this hadith is only the dislike of Abu Juhayfah for that. As for what is therein from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his prohibition of the price of blood, it is what blood is sold for - nothing else. So a group went to the dislike of the cupper's earning, adducing these reports for that. Others differed with them on that, saying: The cupper's earning is the earning of one with filth; so it is disliked for a man to soil and lower himself with that. But that it be in itself forbidden - no."
"5653","6028 - And they argued for that with what Yunus and al-Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Tawus, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn al-'Abbas, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped and gave the cupper his wage for that."
"5654","6029 - And al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibari narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us. (Ha') 6030 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, they both said: Wuhayb narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain of narration the like thereof."
"5655","6031 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, that he said: I heard al-Sha'bi narrating from Ibn 'Abbas that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to a cupper boy; he came and cupped him, and he gave him a wage of a mudd or half a mudd. Had it been forbidden, he would not have given him that."
"5656","6032 - Al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi informed us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from 'Amir al-Sha'bi, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped and gave the cupper his wage, and had it been unlawful he would not have given it to him."
"5657","6033 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Talib, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, that a cupper called Abu Taybah the cupper cupped the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he gave him his wage and reduced for him a portion of his yield, or his family reduced for him a portion of his yield. So Ibn 'Abbas said: Had it been unlawful the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have given it to him."
"5658","6034 - 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Kathir ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped, so he ordered a sa' of food for the cupper, and ordered his masters to lighten something of the levy upon him."
"5659","6035 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sulayman ibn Qays, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Abu Taybah the cupper and he cupped him, so he asked him: How much is your tax? He said: Three sa's, so he reduced a sa' from it for him."
"5660","6036 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sulayman ibn Qays, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith likewise exactly the same."
"5661","6037 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Warqa' ibn 'Umar narrated to us, from 'Abd al-A'la, from Abu Jamilah, from 'Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped and gave the cupper his wage."
"5662","6038 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding the earnings of the cupper: It is fodder for the working camel, or he said: Feed that to your working camel."
"5663","6039-6040 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Awn narrated to us. And Abu Umayyah Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, both said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from Yunus ibn 'Ubayd, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped and gave the cupper his wage."
"5664","6041 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Malik narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Anas, that Abu Taybah cupped the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was fasting, and he gave him his wage. He said: Had it been unlawful he would not have given it to him."
"5665","6042 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us that he said: Anas was asked about the earnings of the cupper, so he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped; Abu Taybah the cupper cupped him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered two sa's of food for him, and spoke to his masters to lighten something of his yield, and they did so."
"5666","6043 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed me that Humayd had narrated to them from Anas from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5667","6044 - Yunus narrated to us also, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed me, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith likewise exactly the same."
"5668","6045 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So in these reports is the permissibility of the cupper's earnings, so it is possible that that came after the prohibition we mentioned, or before it."
"5669","6046-6047 - Yunus narrated to us in examination of that, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us. And Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth informed us, both said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu 'Ufayr al-Ansari, from Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, from Muhayyisah ibn Mas'ud al-Ansari, that he had a slave cupper called Nafi', and Abu Taybah, so he went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about his levy, so he said: Do not go near it, so he repeated that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Feed with it the working camel; put it in its stomach."
"5670","6048 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar narrated to us, he said: Tariq ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us that Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' or Rafi' ibn Rifa'ah - the doubt is from them in that - came to a gathering of the Ansar and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the earnings of the cupper, and ordered us to feed it to our working camel."
"5671","6049 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih the scribe narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Haram ibn Sa'd ibn Muhayyisah, from al-Muhayyisah, a man of Banu Harithah, that he had a cupper, and the man's name was al-Muhayyisah; he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that and he forbade him from eating his earnings, then he returned and he forbade him, then he returned and he forbade him, then he returned and he forbade him; he kept returning to him until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Feed your working camel with his earnings, and feed your slave from it."
"5672","6050 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Haram ibn Sa'd ibn Muhayyisah, that Muhayyisah asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"5673","6051 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Fudayk al-Madani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Haram ibn Sa'd ibn Muhayyisah al-Harithi, from his father, that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"5674","6052 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"5675","6053 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, from Haram ibn Muhayyisah, one of Banu Harithah, from his father, and he mentioned the like of it. So what we mentioned indicates that what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that of permission in this was only after his forbidding it with a general absolute prohibition as in the first reports. And in the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting that he feed the slave or the working camel from it is proof that it is not unlawful. Do you not see that unlawful wealth whose eating is not lawful, it is not lawful for him to feed his slave from it nor his working camel; because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding slaves: Feed them from what you eat. So when the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permission to Muhayyisah to fodder his working camel with that and feed his slave from his cupper's earnings was established, that indicated the abrogation of what preceded of his forbidding that, and its lawfulness was established for him and for others. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them. And this is the reasoning in our view also; because we have seen a man hire a man to bleed a vein for him or to lance an abscess for him, and that is permissible, and hiring upon that is permissible, so cupping is likewise."
"5676","6054 - And there has also been narrated in that from those after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn 'Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi informed me, from his father, he said: I was with 'Abdullah ibn al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, when a woman came to him and said to him: I have a slave cupper, and the people of Iraq claim that I eat the price of blood. So 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas said to her: They have lied; you only eat the levy of your slave."
"5677","6055 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ra'y narrated to me that the cuppers had a market in the time of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5678","6056 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us that he said - and Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari had informed me: The Muslims never ceased affirming the wage of cupping, and not denying it."
"5679","6057 - Chapter of found property and strays. Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu al-'Ala' Yazid ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Abu Muslim al-Judhami, from al-Jarud, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The stray of the Muslim is the burning of the Fire."
"5680","6058 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Yazid the brother of Mutarrif, from Abu Muslim al-Judhami, from al-Jarud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The stray of the Muslim or the believer is the burning of the Fire."
"5681","6059 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to me, he said: al-Hasan narrated to us, from Mutarrif ibn al-Shikhkhir, from his father, that he said: We had come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among a group of Banu 'Amir. He said to us: Shall I not carry you? I said: We find stray camels on the road; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The stray of the Muslim is the burning of the Fire. So a group went to the view that strays are unlawful to take in every case, for announcement and otherwise, and they argued with these reports. Others opposed them in that and said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not intend by what we mentioned in these reports the prohibition of taking the stray for announcement, but he intended taking it for other than that."
"5682","6060 - And what they held has been clarified by what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Yazid ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Abu Muslim al-Judhami, from al-Jarud, that he said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while we were upon lean camels, so we said: O Messenger of Allah, we pass by al-Jurf and find camels so we ride them; so he said: The stray of the Muslim is the burning of the Fire. So their asking the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was about taking them to ride them, not to announce them, so he answered them by saying: The stray of the Muslim is the burning of the Fire, i.e.: the ruling of the Muslim's stray is that it be safeguarded for its owner until it is delivered to its owner, not to be benefited from by riding, nor for other than that. So by that the meaning of this hadith became clear, and that it is upon what we mentioned."
"5683","6061 - And among what those who forbade taking the stray argued with is what 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Abu Hayyan al-Taymi narrated to us, from al-Dahhak ibn al-Mundhir, from al-Mundhir, that he said: I was at al-Bawazij - a place - when the cattle returned and Jarir saw among them a cow he did not recognize, so he said to the herdsman: What is this cow? He said: A cow that joined the cattle; I do not know whose it is? So Jarir ordered it driven away until it disappeared. Then he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: None shelters the stray except one who is astray. They said: So this hadith also forbids taking the stray. So among the proof against them for the others in that is that it may mean that sheltering without announcement with it."
"5684","6062 - For that has also been clarified by what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me that Bakr ibn Sawadah had informed them, from Abu Salim al-Jayshani, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever shelters a stray is astray so long as he does not announce it."
"5685","6063 - Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, then he mentioned this hadith with its chain from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise exactly the same. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified in this hadith who it is that becomes astray by sheltering the stray, and that he is the one who does not announce it. So the meaning of this hadith returns to the meaning of the hadith of al-Jarud and 'Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir in that also."
"5686","6064 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn al-Mahdi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah informed me, from Wa'il ibn Dawud, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Suraqah, from his father Suraqah ibn Malik, that he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said to him: O Messenger of Allah, what do you think of a stray that comes to my camels' trough; is there reward for me if I water it? He said: In every moist liver there is reward."
"5687","6065 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Malik ibn Ju'shum, from his father, that his brother Suraqah ibn Malik said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, then he mentioned this hadith likewise exactly the same. And he in his watering it is sheltering it, yet the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not forbid him that sheltering when he intends by it the benefit of its owner, keeping it for its master, and reward in it. So by that it is established that the disliked sheltering in the hadith of Jarir is only the sheltering intended by other than keeping it for its owner, and seeking reward in it."
"5688","6066 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-Sadafi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb ibn Muslim al-Qurashi informed me, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith, Malik ibn Anas, and Sufyan ibn Sa'id al-Thawri, all together, informed me that Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ra'y narrated to them all, from Yazid the freedman of al-Munba'ith, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while I was with the Messenger of Allah, and asked him about found property; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Know its container and its string, then announce it for a year; if its owner comes, otherwise it is yours to deal with. He said: What about the stray sheep, O Messenger of Allah? He said: It is yours or your brother's or the wolf's. He said: What about the stray camel, O Messenger of Allah? He said: It has its water-skin and its sandals; it comes to water and eats trees until its master finds it."
"5689","6067 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed me, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id and Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, both together, narrated to me, from Yazid the freedman of al-Munba'ith, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about found property of gold, silver, and wariq, so he said: Know its string and its container, then announce it for a year; if it is not recognized, benefit from it, and let it be a trust with you; if a seeker comes for it one day, deliver it to him. Then we mentioned in the hadith regarding camels and sheep the like of what is in the hadith of Yunus exactly the same."
"5690","6068 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Maslamah ibn Qa'nab narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, from Yazid the freedman of al-Munba'ith, that he heard Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani say, then he mentioned this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise exactly the same."
"5691","6069 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ra'i, from Yazid the freedman of al-Munba'ith, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of that hadith likewise, except that he did not say in it: And let it be a trust with you."
"5692","6070 - Fahd ibn Sulayman and 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, both said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan narrated to me, he said: al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim narrated to me, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he was asked about the stray sheep; so he said: It is yours or your brother's or the wolf's. And he was asked about the stray camel; so he said: What have you to do with it? With it are its water-skin and its sandals; leave it until its master finds it. They said: So in this hadith he forbade him from taking the stray camel and ordered him to leave it, so that is also proof of the prohibition of taking strays. It was said to them: In that there is no proof of what you mentioned, but in that is the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordering him to leave the stray camel; because it is its nature to seek water until able to do so, and no loss is feared for it for that, because it comes to water and eats trees until its master meets it, so leaving it is better than taking it, and whoever takes it to safeguard it for its owner is not sinful by that. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked in this hadith about the stray sheep; so he said: It is yours or your brother's or the wolf's. I.e.: you may take it for yourself so it is in your hands for your brother, or leave it so the wolf takes it and eats it, or its master finds it and takes it. So in that is permission to take it."
"5693","6071 - And there has been narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that also what Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith and Hisham ibn Sa'd, both together, informed me, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As, that a man of Muzaynah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him; he said to him: O Prophet of Allah, what do you think about the stray sheep? So he said: Eaten food, for you or your brother or the wolf; keep your brother's stray for him. So he said to him: O Prophet of Allah, and what do you think about the stray camel? So he said: What have you to do with it? With it are its water-skin and its sandals, and the wolf is not feared for it; it eats herbage and comes to water; leave it until its seeker comes. So in this hadith also is permission to take the strays whose loss is feared, and to keep them for him. So that indicates that the meaning of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The stray of the Muslim or the believer is the burning of the Fire, and the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: None shelters the stray except one who is astray, he only intended by that the sheltering without announcement with that, and the taking without announcement with that also, which are contrary to keeping for the owner of the strays, so that the meaning of this hadith of ours and the meaning of those two hadiths agree, and this hadith and those two hadiths do not contradict. And in what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified regarding camels by his saying: What have you to do with it? With it are its water-skin and its sandals, and the wolf is not feared for it, is proof that he did not permit him to take it due to absence of fear for it. And in his permitting taking the sheep due to fear of the wolf for it is proof that the she-camel is likewise also when feared for from other than the wolf, and that taking it for its owner and safeguarding it for its master is more deserving than leaving it and its loss. And there has come from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that the ruling of the stray is like the ruling of found property in that, and it is what has."
"5694","6048b - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Khalid, from Abu al-'Ala', from Mutarrif, from 'Iyad ibn Himar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the stray; so he said: Announce it; if you find its owner, otherwise it is Allah's wealth."
"5695","6072 - So in this hadith announcing it is obligatory, and its announcer in his announcing it is holding it and sheltering it for its owner, and he was not ordered to leave that. So this indicates that the holding forbidden from that in other than this hadith is only the holding which the holder did for himself, not for the master of the stray in that. So this is what is in strays of rulings from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding found property that he ordered witnessing upon it and leaving its concealment, from what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that."
"5696","6073 - What Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Yazid ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Mutarrif ibn al-Shikhkhir, from 'Iyad ibn Himar al-Mujashi'i, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever picks up found property, let him have just persons witness upon it, and not conceal it, nor alter it; if its master comes, otherwise it is Allah's wealth which He gives to whom He wills. So when taking found property upon this manner was permissible, likewise also is taking the stray in that, and taking them both together is only disliked when intended from them is contrary to that. And Ubayy ibn Ka'b deemed taking found properties commendable, and that they not be left for beasts."
"5697","6074 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Sa'id al-Thawri informed me, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, that he said: I went out for Hajj and found a whip so I took it. So Zayd ibn Suhan said to me: Leave it; so I said: I shall not leave it for the beasts; I shall take it and benefit from it. So I met Ubayy ibn Ka'b and mentioned that to him; so he said to me: You did well in that; I had found a purse in it a hundred dinars in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I took it and mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so he said to me: Announce it for a year; if you find one who recognizes it, hand it to him, otherwise benefit from it."
"5698","6075 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, that he said: I heard Suwayd ibn Ghafalah say: I had gone out for Hajj and found a whip so I took it. So Zayd ibn Suhan said to me: Leave it from you, so I said: By Allah I shall not leave it for the beasts, and I shall take it and benefit from it. So I met Ubayy ibn Ka'b and mentioned that to him; so he said to me: You did well in taking it, for I had found a purse in it a hundred dinars in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I took it then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned it to him; so he said: Announce it for a complete year. He said: So I announced it for a year and found none who recognized it. He said: So I came with it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so he said: Go and announce it for a year, so I announced it for a year and found none who recognized it. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so he said: Announce it for a year, so I announced it for a year and found none who recognized it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Memorize its number, its vessel, its container, and its string; if its owner comes, otherwise enjoy it. Shu'bah said: Then Salamah ibn Kuhayl doubted in that, not knowing whether he said three years in the hadith or one year? Salamah ibn Kuhayl said: So this hadith pleased me; so I said that to Abu Sadiq, so Abu Sadiq said: And I heard that hadith also from Ubayy ibn Ka'b just as Suwayd ibn Ghafalah heard it from Ubayy ibn Ka'b exactly the same."
"5699","6076 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that he said: I had picked up a hundred dinars in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so I came with them to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, so he said to me: Announce them for a year; so I announced them for a year, then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so I said to him: I announced them for a year and found none who recognized them; so he said to me: Announce them for a year, so I announced them for a year and found none who recognized them, so I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so I said to him: I announced them for a year and found none who recognized them; so he said to me: Announce them for a year; so I announced them for a year and found none who recognized them, so he said to me: Know its number and its string, then enjoy it. And there has been narrated from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, in that also what has:"
"5700","6077 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah informed me, from al-Walid ibn Kathir, that he said: 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to me, from 'Amr and 'Asim, the two sons of Sufyan ibn 'Abdullah ibn Rabi'ah, that their father Sufyan ibn 'Abdullah had found a small chest and came with it to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab; so he said to him: Announce it for a year; if it is recognized then so, otherwise it is yours. He said: So he announced it for a year and it was not recognized. So he came with it to 'Umar in the next year, in the season, and informed him of that; so 'Umar said to him: It is yours. And he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had ordered us with that. So Sufyan refused to take it, so 'Umar ibn al-Khattab took it from him and placed it in the Muslim treasury."
"5701","6078 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn al-Husayn al-Luhbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from al-Dahhak ibn 'Uthman, from Abu al-Nadr, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about found property; so he said: Announce it for a year; if its seeker comes, deliver it to its owner, otherwise know its container and its string; if its seeker comes, deliver it to its seeker. Do you not see that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not rebuke Ubayy ibn Ka'b in his taking those dinars when he took them, and Ubayy ibn Ka'b deemed right his taking the whip to safeguard it for its owner, and not leave it for the beasts. And 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said in the hadith of Sufyan ibn 'Abdullah: It is your wealth; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had ordered us with that. So when Sufyan refused that, 'Umar placed it in the public treasury. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted taking found property and the stray, to safeguard them for their owner."
"5702","6079 - And the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also narrated in that what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Maslamah ibn Qa'nab al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that Thabit ibn al-Dahhak had found a camel; so 'Umar said to him: Announce it, so he announced that three times, then he came to 'Umar. So he said: It has distracted me from my work, so 'Umar said to him: Remove its halter, then release it where you found it."
"5703","6080 - Yunus narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to them, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, then he mentioned this hadith with its chain from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab likewise exactly the same. And he added in the hadith that Thabit ibn al-Dahhak, and he was of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - narrated to him that he had found a camel in the time of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"5704","6081 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn 'Iyad informed me, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar narrate from Thabit ibn al-Dahhak that he had found a camel, then he mentioned this hadith from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab likewise exactly the same. So this 'Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled in the stray with the ruling of found property. And likewise was narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar in that also, and it is as has:"
"5705","6082 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: al-'Awwam ibn Hawshab informed me, he said: al-'Ala' ibn Suhayl narrated to me that he heard 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar being asked about the stray, of a cup and a thing a man finds, so he said: Ward its good with its evil, and its evil with its good, and do not include it; for none includes the stray except one who is astray."
"5706","6083 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, both said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit: I heard a man asking 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar about the stray; so he said to him: Hand it to the ruler."
"5707","6084 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Nafi' and Ibn Sirin, that a man asked 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, saying: I have found a she-camel, so he said: Announce it, so he said: I announced it and it was not recognized, so he said: Hand it to the governor."
"5708","6085 - Salman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad al-Rusafi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, that he said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, when asked about the stray, say: Hand it to the ruler or to the commander."
"5709","6086 - And there has been narrated from 'A'ishah in that also what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir informed me, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yazid al-Rishk, from Mu'adhah al-'Adawiyyah, that a woman asked 'A'ishah, saying: I found a stray in the sanctuary, and I announced it and found none who recognized it, so 'A'ishah said to her: Benefit from it. And there has been narrated from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud in this the like of that also, and it is as has:"
"5710","6087 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani narrated to us, Abu Usamah informed me? Rather: Sharik informed me, from 'Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Abu Wa'il, that he said: 'Abdullah bought a servant for seven hundred dirhams, then sought his owner and did not find him, so he announced him for a year and did not find his owner; so he gathered the poor and began giving them, saying: O Allah, on behalf of his owner; if he refuses that, then that is from me, and the price is upon me. Then he said: Thus is done with strays."
"5711","6088 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-'Aliyah the wife of Abu Ishaq, that she said: I was with 'A'ishah when a woman came to her and said to her: O Mother of the Believers, I found a stray; how do you order me to deal with it? So she said: Announce it, fodder it, and milk it. She said: Then she returned and asked her; so 'A'ishah said: Do you want me to order you to sell it or remove it? That is not yours. So by what we mentioned the equalization between the ruling of strays and found property is established, and all this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"5712","6089 - And there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the found property and stray of Makkah what 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Alqamah narrated to us, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said - in describing Makkah: Its stray is not picked up except by one who announces it."
"5713","6090 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of that hadith exactly the same."
"5714","6091 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, then he mentioned this hadith with its chain from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise exactly the same. So al-Nadr ibn Shumayl used to say - in what reached me from him in that: The meaning of that is that a stray in the sanctuary should not be picked up except that a man hear a man seeking it and announcing it, so he lifts it to him to see it, then returns it to where he took it."
"5715","6092 - And this hadith has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with other than this wording also, and it is as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Awn informed me, he said: Abu Yusuf informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in describing Makkah: Its found property is not lifted except for its announcer."
"5716","6093 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal Abu Muhammad al-Anmati and Abu Salamah Musa ibn Isma'il al-Basri narrated to us, all together said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Alqamah, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said in describing Makkah: None lifts its found property except one who announces it; so this hadith forbids taking it except for announcement. So this hadith permitted taking the found property of the sanctuary to announce it; so it is possible that intended by that is that he announce then return it to its place. And it is possible that intended is that he announce as one announces found property found in other places and lands. So we found from 'A'ishah what we narrated from her in this chapter, that she was asked about the stray of the sanctuary, and that the woman who asked her about that had announced it and found none who recognized it, so she said to her: Benefit from it. So that indicates that the ruling of found property in the sanctuary is like its ruling outside the sanctuary."
"5717","6094 - And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the found property of the pilgrim also what Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Bukayr ibn 'Abdullah, from Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Uthman, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the found property of the pilgrim. So the meaning of this - in our view, and Allah knows best - is upon the found property which is not announced nor made known; because the found property of the sanctuary was only permitted for announcement. And it may be for the pilgrim and other than the pilgrim; whether the pilgrim's found property was outside the sanctuary or not, that it is likewise also, and Allah the Mighty and Majestic knows best."
"5718","6095 - Book of judiciary and testimonies. Chapter of judging between the dhimmis. Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr, from 'Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned a Jew and a Jewess when they sought judgment from him. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to the view that when the dhimmis commit something of Allah the Exalted's prescribed punishments, the Muslims do not judge upon them until they seek judgment from them and accept their judgment; then when they seek judgment from them, the imam is given choice: if he wishes he turns away from them and does not look into what is between them, and if he wishes he judges. And they argued in that also with Allah the Exalted's saying: If they come to you, judge between them or turn away from them. And others opposed them in that; they said: Upon the imam is to judge between them with the judgments of the Muslims; everything the imam must establish upon the Muslims of what they commit of prescribed punishments, he must establish upon the dhimmis, except what they deem lawful in their religion, like their drinking wine and what resembles it, and that their state therein differs, and the state of the Muslims: they are punished upon that, and the dhimmis are not punished upon it, except stoning in adultery, for it is not established in their view upon the dhimmis; because the causes by which ihsan is obligated in their view, one of them is Islam. As for other than that of obligatory punishments in violating sanctities, the dhimmis therein are like the people of Islam, and upon the imam is to establish it upon them, even if they do not seek judgment from him, just as upon him is to establish it upon the people of Islam, even if they do not seek judgment from him. And among the proof for them in the hadith of Ibn 'Umar which we mentioned is that he only reported therein that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned the Jews when they sought judgment from him. And he did not say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I only stoned them because they sought judgment from me. Had he said that, it would be known that judgment from him is only after they seek judgment from him, and that when they do not seek judgment from him he does not look into their affairs. But it did not come; it only came from him that he stoned them when they sought judgment from him. So he only reported of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) act and judgment when they sought judgment from him, and did not report of their judgment with him before they sought judgment from him: is establishing the hadd obligatory upon them therein or not? So it is voided that in this hadith is indication in that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor from Ibn 'Umar of his view. Then we looked into other than that of reports: do we find therein what indicates something of that?"
"5719","6096 - When Ahmad ibn Abi 'Imran: narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaythamah Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Mujalid ibn Sa'id, from 'Amir al-Sha'bi, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, that the Jews came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a man and woman of them who had committed adultery. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Bring four of you to testify. So by this it is established that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would look between them before the man and woman accused of adultery arbitrated him, because they were both both denying; had they been confessing, with their confession he would not need four to testify. And there was narrated from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that also."
"5720","6097 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from 'Abdullah ibn Murrah, from al-Bara', he said: A man was passed by the Messenger of Allah with his face blackened, beaten, being paraded. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is the matter with this? They said: He committed adultery. He said: What do you find in your Book? They said: His face is blackened, he is chastised, and paraded. So he said: I adjure you by Allah, what do you find his hadd in your Book? So they pointed to a man of them, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked him; so the man said: We find in the Torah stoning, but it became frequent among our nobles, so we disliked establishing the hadd upon our lowly and leaving our nobles, so we agreed upon something and placed this. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned him, and said: I am the most deserving to revive what they caused to die of Allah's command. So in this is what indicates that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had to judge between them even if they did not arbitrate him; because in this hadith they passed by him with him blackened, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned him. So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called them - disapproving what they did before they came to him, returning their affair to Allah's judgment which they had suspended and altered - by that it is established that he had to judge in what is between them, whether they arbitrated him or not. So this is what is in these reports of indications upon what we discussed. As for Allah the Mighty and Majestic's saying: If they come to you, judge between them or turn away from them, then those who went to affirming judgment say: It is abrogated."
"5721","6098 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Suddi, from 'Ikrimah: If they come to you, judge between them or turn away from them, he said: This verse abrogated it: And judge between them by what Allah has revealed, and do not follow their desires. And the others said: Its interpretation is (and judge between them by what Allah has revealed if you judge); so when its interpretation was disputed, and the reports had indicated what we mentioned, judgment upon them upon the Muslim imam was established, and leaving it was not his; because in his judging is salvation in their both sayings; because whoever says: upon him is to judge, says: he has left what upon him was to do. And whoever says: he may not judge, says: he has left what he might leave; so when he judges, both groups together testify to him of salvation, and when he does not judge they do not testify that to him. So the most deserving thing for us is to do what therein is salvation by agreement, not what therein is contrary to salvation by disagreement. And this which we mentioned of the obligatoriness of judgment upon them is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. So if a speaker says: But you do not stone the Jews when they commit adultery, so you have left some of what is in the hadith with which you argued. It is said to him: Judgment in adulterers was in the time of Musa, peace be upon him, stoning upon the married and unmarried. And likewise was the answer of the Jew whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked about the hadd of the adulterer in their book, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny that upon him; so upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was to follow that and act with it; because upon every prophet is to follow the law of the prophet before him until Allah brings a law abrogating his law; Allah the Exalted said: Those are they whom Allah guided, so follow their guidance; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned the two Jews upon that judgment, and no difference then in that between the married and unmarried. Then Allah the Mighty and Majestic brought for His prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a law, and it abrogated this law; so He said: As for those of your women who commit indecency, call four of you to witness against them; if they testify, confine them to houses until death takes them or Allah makes a way for them. And this was abrogating what was before it, and did not distinguish in that between the married and unmarried. Then Allah the Exalted abrogated that and made the hadd harm by the verse after it, and did not distinguish in that also between the married and others. Then He made a way for them; the virgin with the virgin: a hundred lashes and a year's banishment, and the previously-married: a hundred lashes and stoning. So then He distinguished between the hadd of the married and the hadd of the unmarried: lashing. Then people differed after in ihsan. So a group said: A man is not muhsan by his wife, nor a woman muhsanah by her husband, until they are free, Muslims, adult, and he had intercourse with her while adult in a valid marriage. And among those who said that: Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"5722","6099 - And others said: The People of the Book make one another muhsan, and the Muslim makes the Christian woman muhsanah, but the Christian woman does not make the Muslim muhsan; and Abu Yusuf had said this view in the dictation in what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to me from his father. So the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The previously-married with the previously-married: stoning, may be upon every previously-married, and may be upon a particular of the previously-married. So we looked into that and found them agreed that slaves are not included in that, and that the slave is not muhsan, previously-married or virgin, nor does he make his wife muhsanah, free or slave. And likewise the slave woman is not muhsanah by her husband, free or slave. So by what we mentioned it is established that the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The previously-married with the previously-married: stoning, only fell upon a particular of the previously-married, not upon every previously-married. So there does not enter into what they agreed it fell upon particularly except what they agreed it is therein included. And they agreed that the two free Muslim adult spouses who had intercourse are muhsan, and differed in others. So our knowledge encompassed that that entered into the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The previously-married with the previously-married: stoning. So we entered it therein, and our knowledge did not encompass other than that, so we excluded it. And it would come in analogy - when the slave woman does not make the free man muhsan, nor does the free man make her muhsanah, and she in her not making him muhsan is like him in his not making her muhsanah - that likewise the Christian woman; just as she does not make her Muslim husband muhsan, he also likewise does not make her muhsanah. And we have seen the slave woman also - when it was voided that she make the Muslim muhsan - voided that the disbeliever make muhsan by analogy, in view upon what we mentioned, and Allah the Exalted knows best."
"5723","6100 - Chapter of judging with an oath along with a witness. Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Hubab narrated to us, he said: Sayf ibn Sulayman al-Makki informed me, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged with an oath along with a witness."
"5724","6101 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed me, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5725","6102-6103 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, both said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman; and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. 'Abd al-'Aziz said: And Suhayl forgot it. He said: Rabi'ah narrated to me from me."
"5726","6104 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid - meaning al-Himmani - narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal and al-Darawardi narrated to us; and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. 'Abd al-'Aziz said: So I met Suhayl and asked him about this hadith and he did not recognize it."
"5727","6105 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn al-Hakam narrated to me, from Zuhayr ibn Muhammad, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Zayd ibn Thabit, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5728","6106 - Wuhban ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, he said: Abu Hammam narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Abd al-Majid al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5729","6107 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ja'far, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not mention Jabir."
"5730","6108 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5731","6109 - Bahr narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Muhammad, Malik ibn Anas and Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to judging with an oath along with a single witness in a particular of things: in wealth particularly, and they argued in that with these reports. And others opposed them in that; they said: It is not obligatory to judge in anything of things except with two men or a man and two women, and judgment is not with a witness and oath in anything of things. They said: As for what you narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what is mentioned therein that he judged with an oath along with a witness; weakness has entered it with which no proof stands. As for the hadith of Rabi'ah from Suhayl, al-Darawardi asked Suhayl about it and he did not recognize it; had that been of famous sunnahs and known matters, its knowledge would not have gone, and you weaken of hadiths what is stronger than this hadith with less than this. As for the hadith of 'Uthman ibn al-Hakam from Zuhayr ibn Muhammad from Suhayl from his father from Zayd ibn Thabit, it is rejected also; because Abu Salih is not known to have narration from Zayd. Had Suhayl had anything of that, he would not have denied to al-Darawardi what you mentioned from Rabi'ah, saying to him: My father did not narrate it to me from Abu Hurayrah, but narrated it to me from Zayd ibn Thabit; besides 'Uthman ibn al-Hakam is not one by whose narration the like of this is established. As for the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas, it is rejected; because Qays ibn Sa'd we do not know narrating from 'Amr ibn Dinar anything; so how do they argue with it in the like of this? As for the hadith of Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father from Jabir, 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated it as you mentioned. As for the memorizers Malik, Sufyan al-Thawri and their likes, they narrated it from Ja'far from his father from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and did not mention Jabir therein; and you do not argue with 'Abd al-Wahhab in what he contradicts al-Thawri and Malik therein. Then had its chain not been disputed, and these wordings narrated upon it were sound, they would bear the interpretation with which no proof stands for you with its like. That is that you only narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged with an oath along with a single witness. And it is not clarified in the hadith how that cause was, nor the one made to swear with whom he is? So it may be upon what you mentioned, and it may be intended by it the oath of the defendant. When the claimant claims and establishes upon his claim only a single witness, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the defendant swear for him; so that was narrated to let people know that the claimant is due the oath upon the defendant - not by another proof besides the claim - the oath is not due him except with it. As some group said: The claimant is not due the oath in what he claimed except that he establish proof that there was between him and the defendant mixing and dealing; if he establishes proof upon that, oath is made for him, otherwise not. So the one who narrated this hadith intended to negate this view and affirm the oath by the claim, even if with the claim there is nothing else; so this is a وجه. And it may be intended by it the oath of the claimant with his single witness; because his single witness was of those whose testimony alone is judged with, and he is Khuzaymah ibn Thabit; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had equated his testimony with the testimony of two men."
"5732","6110 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: 'Umarah ibn Khuzaymah al-Ansari informed me that his uncle narrated to him, and he is of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought a horse from a Bedouin and had him follow him to pay him the price of his horse. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastened walking, and the Bedouin lagged; so men began intercepting the Bedouin bargaining with him for the horse, not knowing that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had bought it, until some of them increased the Bedouin in bidding over the price of the horse which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had bought it for. So the Bedouin called the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: If you are buying this horse, buy it, otherwise I shall sell it. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said when he heard the Bedouin's call; he said: Did I not buy it from you? So the Bedouin said: No, by Allah, I did not sell you. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, I bought it from you. So people began surrounding the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Bedouin, while they disputed, and the Bedouin began saying: Bring a witness to testify for you that I sold you - from those Muslims who came; they said to the Bedouin: Woe to you, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say only truth. Until Khuzaymah came and listened to the dispute of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the dispute of the Bedouin, while he says: Bring a witness to testify for you that I sold you. So Khuzaymah said: I testify that you sold him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to Khuzaymah; he said: With what do you testify? He said: By believing you, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah made the testimony of Khuzaymah as the testimony of two men. So when that witness we mentioned may be Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, so the one testified for by his testimony alone becomes deserving of what was testified for him as others become deserving by two witnesses of what they testified for him; then the defendant claimed exit from that right to the claimant, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made him swear for him upon that; and this hadith was transmitted to know that the claimant when he establishes proof upon his claim, and the defendant claims exit from that right to him - that upon him is the oath with his proof. So these are aspects what came from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his judging with an oath along with a witness bears. So it is not for anyone to come to a report bearing these interpretations and incline it to one of them without proof indicating him upon that of Book or Sunnah or consensus, then claim that whoever opposes that opposes what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And how is he opposing what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he interpreted that upon a meaning what he said bears? Rather he only opposed his opponent's interpretation of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hadith, and did not oppose anything of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hadith."
"5733","6111 - And there has been narrated from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah ennoble his face, what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from 'Ali, he said: When there reaches you from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a hadith, think of it that which is most wholesome, and that which is most guiding, and that which is most lasting, and that which is best."
"5734","6112 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Abu al-Walid narrated to us, both said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Amr, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it, except that he did not say: And that which is best. So thus people should do, and make good the verification of their thoughts, and not say upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except what they knew; for they are forbidden from that and punished upon it. And how is it permissible for anyone to carry the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hadith upon what this opponent carried it, when we found Allah the Mighty and Majestic's Book repelling it, then the agreed-upon Sunnah repelling it also? As for Allah the Mighty and Majestic's Book, Allah the Exalted says: And call two witnesses of your men; if not two men, a man and two women, and He said: And have two just persons of you witness. And they before these two verses were revealed should not judge with the testimony of a thousand men, nor more nor less; because with their testimony truthfulness is not reached. So when Allah the Mighty and Majestic revealed what we mentioned, He cut thereby the excuse, and judged with what He commanded upon what He bound His creation, and did not judge with what is less than that because it did not enter into what they were bound with. As for the agreed-upon Sunnah, it is that judgment is not with the testimony of one bringing gain to himself, nor repelling loss from it. So judgment with an oath along with a single witness upon what this opponent carried the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hadith: therein is judgment for a claimant by his oath, so that is judgment for one bringing to himself by his oath. So this is an agreed-upon Sunnah repelling judgment with an oath along with a witness, with what also repelled it of what we mentioned of Allah the Exalted's Book. So the most deserving thing for us is to turn the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hadith to what agrees with Allah the Exalted's Book and the agreed-upon Sunnah, not to what contradicts them or one of them. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) textually what repels judging with an oath along with a witness upon what this opponent claimed for us."
"5735","6113 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah both together narrated to us, both said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Hamid, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from 'Alqamah ibn Wa'il, from Wa'il ibn Hujr, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when two men disputing over land came to him. So one of them said: This, O Messenger of Allah, seized my land upon me in the Jahiliyyah, and he is Imru' al-Qays ibn 'Abis al-Kindi, and his opponent Rabi'ah ibn 'Aydan. So he said to him: Your proof; so he said: I have no proof. He said: His oath. He said: Then he will go with it. He said: You have only that. So when he stood to swear, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever cuts off land wrongfully will meet Allah while He is angry with him."
"5736","6114 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from 'Alqamah ibn Wa'il, from his father, he said: A man of Hadramawt and a man of Kindah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so the Hadrami said: O Messenger of Allah, this has overpowered me over land that was mine. So the Kindi said: It is my land in my hand; I cultivate it; he has no right in it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Hadrami: Have you proof? He said: No. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I make him swear? He said: He has no oath. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have from him only that. So he went to make him swear; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If he swears over your wealth wrongfully to consume it, he will meet Allah while He is turned away from him."
"5737","6115 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Jandal ibn Waliq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it, except that he said: So the Hadrami said: O Messenger of Allah, this overpowered me over land that was mine. Abu Ja'far said: So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your oath or his oath; you have therein only that, it indicates that he deserves nothing without proof; so this negates judging with an oath along with a witness. And what is most deserving for us is to carry the وجه of what its interpretation differed of the first hadith upon what agrees with this, not upon what contradicts it. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If people were given by their claims, people would claim the blood of men and their wealth; but the oath is upon the defendant. So that indicates that the oath is never except upon the defendant, and we mentioned that with the chain in what preceded of this book. As for reasoning in this, it suffices us from mentioning most of the corruption of the view of those who went to judging with an oath along with a witness. So they made that in wealth particularly, apart from other things. So when it was established that judgment is not with oath and witness in other than wealth, the ruling of wealth in reasoning is likewise also. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"5738","6116 - Wuhban narrated to us, he said: Abu Hammam narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Zuhri, that Mu'awiyah was the first to judge with an oath along with a witness, and the matter was upon other than that, and Allah knows best."
"5739","6117 - Chapter: a man having testimony for a man, is it obligatory upon him to inform him of it? And does the judge accept him upon that or not? Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr narrated to us, he said: Jabir ibn Samurah narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed us at al-Jabiyah, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us as my standing among you today; so he said: Do good to my Companions, then those after them, then those after them, then lying will spread until a man testifies upon testimony without being asked it, and until a man swears upon an oath without being made to swear it."
"5740","6118 - 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: 'Arim ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Umayr narrated to us; and he mentioned with its chain the like of it, except that he said: Do good to my Companions, then those after them, then those after them, then those after them, then lying will spread."
"5741","6119 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: I heard Kahmas say: I heard 'Umar say. And he mentioned the like of the hadith of Abu Bakrah from Abu Ahmad. So a group went to the view that whoever testifies with testimony before being asked it is blameworthy, and they argued in that with these reports. And others opposed them in that, saying: Rather he is praiseworthy, rewarded upon what was from him of that. And among the proof for them in repelling what the people of the first view argued against them with is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then lying will spread until a man testifies upon testimony without being asked it, and until he swears upon an oath without being made to swear. So the meaning of that is that he testifies falsely or swears falsely, because he said: Until lying spreads, so it is such and such. So it is not permissible that that which is when lying spreads be except lying; otherwise there is no meaning to his mentioning lying spreading."
"5742","6120 - And the people of the first view also argued for their view with what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Suqah informed us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he addressed them at al-Jabiyah, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Honor my Companions, then those after them, then those after them, then those after them, then lying will spread until a man testifies before being called to testify."
"5743","6121 - 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Basri narrated to us, he said: 'Arim narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Abi Awfa, from 'Imran ibn Husayn, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of my nation is the generation I was sent among, then those after them, then those after them. He said: And Allah knows whether he mentioned the third or not? Then there will spread a people who testify and are not called to testify, vow and do not fulfill, betray and are not trusted, and oaths spread among them."
"5744","6122 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Thabit al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Zahdam ibn Mudarrib al-Jarmi, that he heard 'Imran ibn Husayn say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you is my generation, then he mentioned the like of it. They said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) blamed in this hadith one who testifies and is not called to testify. It was said to them: This is upon one who is not called to testify at the beginning of the matter, so he is in his testimony with the judge testifying with what he did not witness upon, nor know. So the meaning of this hadith returns to the meaning of the first hadith."
"5745","6123 - And they mentioned in that also what Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sulaym, from Mus'ab ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Umayyah, he said: Umm Salamah narrated to me that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There will come upon people a time in which the truthful is belied, the liar believed, the trustworthy deemed treacherous, the treacherous trusted, a man testifies though not called to testify, and a man swears though not made to swear."
"5746","6124-6125 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us. And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn 'Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, both together: from Abu Bishr, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of my nation is my generation, then those after them, then those after them, then I do not know whether he mentioned the third or not, then after them will succeed generations fond of fatness, and they testify and are not called to testify."
"5747","6126 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Sharahil narrated to me, from Bilal ibn Sa'd, from his father, he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, which of your nation is best? He said: I and my generation. He said: We said: Then what? He said: Then the second generation. He said: We said: Then what? He said: The third generation. He said: We said: Then what? He said: Then there will come a people who testify and are not called to testify, swear and are not made to swear, and are trusted but do not fulfill. Abu Ja'far said: So speech in its interpretation is the speech we mentioned in interpreting the reports in the section before this."
"5748","6127 - And they argued in that also with what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur and Sulayman, i.e. al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from 'Abidah, i.e. al-Salmani, from 'Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you is my generation, then those after them, then those after them; then there will succeed a people whose testimony precedes their oaths, and their oaths their testimony."
"5749","6128 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ashkib narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from 'Abidah, from 'Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5750","6129 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from 'Abdullah ibn Mawalah, he said: I was traveling with Buraydah al-Aslami, while he says: O Allah, join me to my generation of which I am, three times, while I was with him. So I said: And I. So he supplicated for me, then he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The best of this nation is the generation I was sent among, then those after them, then those after them, then those after them; then there will be a people whose testimonies precede their oaths, and their oaths their testimonies."
"5751","6130 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn 'Ali al-Ju'fi narrated to us, from Za'idah, from 'Asim, from Khaythamah, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The best of people is my generation, then those after them, then those after them; then there will succeed a people whose testimonies precede their oaths, and their oaths their testimonies."
"5752","6131 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Asim, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it, and added: Then those after them once more, then there will come a people. So among our proof against those who argued with these reports for the people of the first view is that this testimony was not intended as testimony upon rights, but intended thereby is testimony in oaths; and there has been narrated what indicates that from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i."
"5753","6132 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Shayban informed me, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from 'Abidah, from 'Abdullah, he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, which people are best? He said: My generation, then those after them, then those after them; then there will come a people whose testimony of one of them precedes his oath, and his oath his testimony. Ibrahim said: Our companions used to forbid us, while we were youths, from swearing by testimony and covenant. So this indicates from Ibrahim's saying that the testimony whose bearer the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) blamed is a man's saying: I testify by Allah such was not, upon the meaning of an oath; so he disliked that as oath-taking is disliked, because it is disliked for a man to do much of it, even if truthful. So he forbade testimony which is an oath as he forbade the oath except that he be made to swear by it, so then he is excused. And perhaps he intended by the testimony we mentioned swearing upon what was not; for his saying: (then lying will spread), so that testimony is false testimony."
"5754","6133 - And there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in preferring the witness beginning with testimony what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn 'Uthman, from Abu 'Amrah al-Ansari, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you of the best of witnesses? The one who comes with his testimony before being asked about it, or informs of his testimony before being asked it. Malik said: The one who informs of his testimony, which its beneficiary does not know, or comes with it to the imam and testifies with it before him; and he made him the best of witnesses. So it is most deserving for us to carry the first reports upon what we described of the interpretation of each report, so they do not contradict, differ, nor repel one another. So the first reports are upon the meanings we mentioned, and these other reports are upon preferring the beginner with testimony for its beneficiary or the informer of it to the imam. And the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that and came to the imam and testified initially, of them Abu Bakrah and those with him when they testified against al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, seeing that obligatory for themselves; and 'Umar did not rebuke them upon their beginning him with that, but heard their testimonies. Had they been blameworthy in that, he would have blamed them: Who asked you about this? Why did you not sit until asked? So when he heard from them and 'Umar did not deny that upon them, nor anyone present of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that indicates that their obligation is likewise, and that whoever does that initially, not upon asking, is praiseworthy."
"5755","6134 - So of what was narrated in that is what 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim and Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, both said: al-Sari ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Karim ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: A man came to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and testified against al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, so the color of 'Umar changed; then another came and testified, so the color of 'Umar changed; then another came and testified, so the color of 'Umar changed, until we recognized that in him, and he disapproved of that. And another came moving his hands, so he said: What have you, O stripped eagle? And Abu 'Uthman shouted a shout resembling 'Umar's shout, until I nearly fainted. He said: I saw a foul thing. He said: Praise be to Allah who did not let Satan gloat over the nation of Muhammad; so he ordered those persons flogged."
"5756","6135 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi informed me, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Four testified against al-Mughirah, so Ziyad ibn Abi Sufyan held back, so 'Umar ibn al-Khattab flogged the three, and had them repent; so two repented, and Abu Bakrah refused to repent, so he would accept the testimony of the two when they repented, and Abu Bakrah's testimony was not accepted because he refused to repent, and he was like the emaciated one from worship."
"5757","6136 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn 'Abdullah ibn Jumay' narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Tufayl narrated to me, he said: A group came with a woman until they alighted and dispersed for their needs, so a man stayed behind with a woman, and they returned with him between her legs; so three of them testified that they saw him thrusting as the collyrium stick thrusts in the kohl bottle. And the fourth said: I guard my hearing and sight; I did not see him thrusting in it; I saw his testicles striking her buttocks, and her legs like donkey's ears. And over Makkah that day was Nafi' ibn al-Harith al-Khuza'i, and he wrote to 'Umar. So 'Umar wrote: If a fourth testifies like what the three testified, bring them forward and flog them both, and if they were married stone them both; and if they testify only with what you wrote to me, flog the three, and free the man. He said: So he flogged the three and freed the man and woman. So these Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) testified some initially, and some accepted it, and most attended that and did not deny. So that indicates their agreement all upon this meaning, and it is established that the meanings of the first reports are upon what we mentioned of their meanings we described in their places. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"5758","6137 - Chapter: a judge judging with a thing, while in reality it is contrary to the apparent. Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed me, from al-Zuhri, he said: 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, and her mother Umm Salamah, informed him that her mother Umm Salamah said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard clamor of dispute at his door, so he went out to them; he said: I am only a human, and a disputant comes, and perhaps some of you are more eloquent than others, so I judge for him by that, thinking him truthful; so whoever I judge for with a Muslim's right, it is only a piece of the Fire, so let him take it or leave it."
"5759","6138 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Salih, from Ibn Shihab; and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"5760","6139 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from Zaynab, from Umm Salamah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You dispute to me, and I am only a human, and perhaps some of you are more fluent with his proof, so I judge for him upon what I hear from him; so whoever I judge for with something of his brother's right, I only cut for him a piece of the Fire, so let him not take it."
"5761","6140 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Amr informed us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5762","6141 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd - he heard it from 'Abdullah ibn Nafi', the freedman of Umm Salamah - from Umm Salamah, she said: Two men of the Ansar came disputing to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over inheritances between them that had become effaced, with no proof between them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am only a human, and a disputant comes, and perhaps some of you are more eloquent than others, so I judge for him by that, thinking him truthful; so whoever I judge for with a Muslim's right, it is only a piece of the Fire, so let him take it or leave it. So the two men wept, and each said: My right is for my brother. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Since you did this, go and divide, seek the truth, then cast lots, then let each of you absolve his companion."
"5763","6142 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd informed me, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"5764","6143 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: Usamah narrated to me, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to the view that every judgment a judge judges of conferring wealth, granting ownership for wealth, affirming marriage, its dissolution by divorce, or what resembles it, all that is upon the ruling of the inward, and that inwardly it is as outwardly; that is obligated upon what the judge judged. And if that inwardly is contrary to what the two witnesses testified, and contrary to what he judged by their testimony upon the apparent judgment, the judge's judgment does not necessitate anything of conferring, prohibiting, or permitting; and they argued in that with this hadith. And of those who said that: Abu Yusuf. And others opposed them in that, saying: What was of that of conferring wealth is upon the ruling of the inward, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever I judge for with something of his brother's right, let him not take it, for I only cut for him a piece of the Fire. And what was of that of judgment by divorce or marriage with witnesses whose outward is uprightness and inward wounding, so the judge judged by their testimony upon their outward which Allah bound to judge with the testimony of their like with it, that forbids inwardly as it forbids outwardly. And proof upon this is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the two mutual imprecators."
"5765","6144 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed me, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) separated between the two brothers of Banu al-'Ajlan, and said to them: Your reckoning is upon Allah; Allah knows that one of you is lying; there is no way for you to her. He said: O Messenger of Allah, my dower which I dowered her? He said: You have no wealth upon her; if you dowered upon her, it is for what you deemed lawful of her private part; and if you lied upon her, it is farther from you than it."
"5766","6145 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he heard Sahl ibn Sa'd say: I witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) separate between the two mutual imprecators, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, I lied upon her if I keep her."
"5767","6146 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, that Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi informed him that 'Uwaymir al-'Ajlani came to 'Asim ibn 'Adi al-Ansari and said to him: What do you think, O 'Asim, if a man finds with his wife a man, does he kill him and you kill him, or how does he do? Ask for me about that, O 'Asim, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So when 'Asim returned to his family, 'Uwaymir came to him and said: O 'Asim, what did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say to you? So 'Asim said: O 'Uwaymir, you did not bring me good; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked the question you asked him. So 'Uwaymir said: I shall not stop until I ask him about it. So 'Uwaymir came until he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) amid people, and said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you think of a man who finds with his wife a man, does he kill him and you kill him, or how does he do? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has revealed regarding you and your wife; go and bring her. Sahl said: So they imprecated, while I was with people with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So when they finished, 'Uwaymir said: I lied upon her, O Messenger of Allah, if I keep her, so he divorced her thrice before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him. Ibn Shihab said: So it was the sunnah of the two mutual imprecators."
"5768","6147 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: al-Majishun narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, from 'Asim, he said: 'Uwaymir came to me, then he mentioned the like of it. So we knew that had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) known the liar of them specifically, he would not have separated between them, nor imprecated; had he known the woman truthful, he would have hadd the husband for his slandering her. And had he known the husband truthful, he would have hadd the woman for the adultery from her. So when the truthful of them was hidden from the judge, another judgment became obligatory, so he forbade the private part to the husband inwardly and outwardly, and did not return that to the ruling of the inward. So when they testified in the two mutual imprecators, it is established that likewise all separations, and judgment in what has no conferring of wealth, is upon the ruling of the apparent, not upon the ruling of the inward; and the judge's judgment brings about therein forbidding and permitting outwardly and inwardly together; and it is contrary to wealth which is judged upon the apparent ruling, while inwardly upon contrary to that. So the first reports are in judgment with wealth, and the other reports are in judgment with other than wealth, of affirming contracts and their dissolution, so the meanings of the reports and judgments agree and do not contradict. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged in two sellers when they differ over the price, while the commodity stands, that they both swear and return. So the slave girl returns to the seller, her private part lawful to him and unlawful to the buyer. Had the liar of them been known specifically, he would have judged with what the truthful says, and not judged with annulling a sale, nor with the obligatoriness of the sold slave girl's private part's sanctity upon the buyer. So when that was upon what we described, likewise is every judgment with forbidding, permitting, contracting marriage or its dissolution, upon what the judge judged therein outwardly, not upon its ruling inwardly; and this is the view of Abu Hanifah and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"5769","6148 - Chapter: a free man upon whom debt is due, with no wealth, what is his ruling? Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid al-Zanji narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Baylamani, he said: I was in Egypt, when a man said to me: Shall I not guide you to a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So he took me to a man, so I said: Of whom are you, may Allah have mercy on you? He said: I am Surraq. I said: May Allah have mercy on you, you should not be named with this name, while you are a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named me Surraq, so I shall never leave that. I said: Why did he name you Surraq? He said: I met a man of the desert folk with two camels of his selling them, so I bought them from him, and said to him: Come with me until I give you; so I entered my house, then went out from behind me, and fulfilled with the price of the two camels my need, and hid until I thought the Bedouin had left. So I went out with the Bedouin staying, so he seized me and brought me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I informed him the news. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What carried you upon what you did? I said: I fulfilled with their price my need, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then pay him. He said: I said: I have nothing. He said: You are Surraq; go with him, O Bedouin, and sell him until you obtain your right. He said: So people began bidding for me, turning to him; saying: What do you want? They say: We want to buy him from you and free him. He said: By Allah, none of you is more needy of him than I; go, I have freed you."
"5770","6149 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Dinar narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, he said: I met a man at Alexandria called Surraq, so I said: What is this name? So he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named me it; I came to Madinah and informed them that wealth would come to me, so they sold to me and I consumed their wealth; so they brought me to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so he said: You are Surraq, so he sold me for four camels. So his creditors said to him: What does he do with him? He said: I free him. They said: We are not more averse to reward than you, so they freed me. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith is the sale of the free man in debt; and that was in early Islam: one upon whom debt was, was sold for his debt when he had no wealth to pay for himself, until Allah the Mighty and Majestic abrogated that; He said: If there is one in hardship, respite until ease. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged with that in one who bought fruits and was stricken with them, so his debt became much. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give charity, so charity was given upon him, but that did not reach payment of his debt. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take what you find, and you have only that. And we mentioned that with its chain in what preceded of this book of ours. So in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to his creditors: (You have only that) is proof that they have no right in his sale; had it not been that, he would have sold him for them as he sold Surraq in his debt for his creditors; and this is the view of all people of knowledge, may Allah have mercy on them."
"5771","6150 - Chapter: does the father own his son's wealth or not? Rabi' al-Jizi and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, both said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have wealth and dependents, and my father has wealth and dependents, and he wants to take my wealth to his wealth. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You and your wealth belong to your father."
"5772","6151 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Husayn al-Mu'allim narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: A man said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I have wealth, and I have a father who wants to sweep away my wealth. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You and your wealth belong to your father; your children are of the best of your earnings, so eat of your children's earnings. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to the view that what the son earns of wealth belongs to his father, and they argued in that with these reports. And others opposed them in that, saying: What the son earns of anything belongs to him particularly, apart from his father. And they said: The Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying this is not upon conferring to the father the son's earnings, but upon that the son should not oppose the father in anything of that, and make his command therein effective as his command in what he owns. Do you not see him say: You and your wealth belong to your father, yet the son was not owned by his father by the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascribing him; likewise he is not owner of his wealth by the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascribing it to him."
"5773","6152 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No wealth ever benefited me as the wealth of Abu Bakr benefited me. So Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I and my wealth are only for you, O Messenger of Allah. So Abu Bakr did not intend by that that his wealth is possession of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apart from him, but he intended that his command is effective therein and in himself. So likewise His saying: You and your wealth belong to your father is upon this meaning also, and Allah knows best. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The wealth of Muslims is unlawful as their blood is unlawful, and He did not except in that father nor other."
"5774","6153-6154 - So of what was narrated from him in that is what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us. And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Murrah ibn Sharahil, he said: A man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to me - and I think he said: in this expedition of mine - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us, saying: Do you know what day this is? They said: Yes, the Day of Sacrifice. He said: You spoke truth, the Day of the Greater Hajj. He said: Do you know what month this is? They said: Yes, Dhu al-Hijjah. He said: You spoke truth, Allah's deaf month. Do you know what land this is? They said: Yes, the Sacred Mash'ar. He said: You spoke truth. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your blood and your wealth - and I think he said: and your honors - are unlawful upon you, as the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours."
"5775","6155 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab al-Bakrawi, he is Ibn Khalifah, narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his sermon on the Day of Sacrifice in the Farewell Hajj: Your wealth, honors and blood are unlawful among you, in the like of this day of yours, in the like of this land of yours; let the witness convey to the absent."
"5776","6156 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Salih narrate from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri or from Abu Hurayrah, and I think it Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the Farewell Hajj: The greatest of days in sanctity is this day, the greatest of months in sanctity is this month, the greatest of lands in sanctity is this land; and your blood and wealth are unlawful upon you, as the sanctity of this day, this month, and this land. Did I convey? They said: Yes. He said: O Allah, bear witness."
"5777","6157 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed them in the Farewell Hajj, saying: Your blood and wealth are unlawful upon you until you meet your Lord, as the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours."
"5778","6158 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Duhaym ibn al-Yatim narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn al-Ghaz al-Jurashi narrated to us, he said: Nafi' informed me, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, then he mentioned the like of it."
"5779","6159 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Kulthum ibn Jabr narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Ghadiyah al-Juhani, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, then he mentioned the like of it."
"5780","6160 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn 'Azib ibn Shabib ibn Gharqadah Abu Gharqad narrated to us, from Shabib ibn Gharqadah, from Sulayman, from 'Amr ibn al-Ahwas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed in the Farewell Hajj, and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the sanctity of wealth as the sanctity of bodies. So just as the bodies of sons are not lawful to fathers except with due rights, likewise their wealth is not lawful to them except with due rights. So if a speaker says: We want to find what you mentioned regarding the father textually from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 6161 -"
"5781","6161 - I said: Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub informed me, from 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas al-Qitbani, from 'Isa ibn Hilal al-Sadafi, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: I was commanded with the Day of Sacrifice as a festival which Allah made for this nation. So the man said: What do you think if I find only my son's granted milch animal, shall I sacrifice with it? He said: No, but you take from your hair and nails, trim your mustache, and shave your pubes; that is the completion of your sacrifice with Allah. Abu Ja'far said: So when this man said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I sacrifice with my son's granted animal? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No. And he had ordered him to sacrifice from his wealth and urged him upon it - that indicates that the ruling of his son's wealth is contrary to his wealth. Besides the most deserving thing for us is to carry these reports upon this meaning; because Allah the Mighty and Majestic's Book indicates that; Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: Allah enjoins you regarding your children: to the male the like of two females' share. Then He said: And to his parents, to each one of them the sixth of what he leaves. So Allah the Mighty and Majestic gave inheritance to other than the child with the father from the son's wealth; so it is impossible that wealth belongs to the father in the son's life, then some of it becomes for other than the father. Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: After a bequest he bequeaths or debt. So Allah the Mighty and Majestic made inheritances for the father and others after paying a debt if upon the deceased, and after executing his bequests from a third of his wealth. And they agreed that the father does not pay from his wealth his son's debt, nor execute his father's bequests from his wealth; so in that is what indicated what we mentioned. And the Muslims agreed that when the son owns a slave girl, it is lawful for him to have intercourse with her, and she is of those Allah the Mighty and Majestic permitted him intercourse with by His Exalted saying: And those who guard their private parts, except upon their wives or what their right hands possess. So had his wealth belonged to his father, intercourse with slave girls he earned would be unlawful to him, as the unlawfulness of intercourse with his father's slave girls upon him. So that also indicates the negation of the father's ownership of the son's wealth, and that the son's ownership therein is established apart from his father. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"5782","6162 - Chapter: a child claimed by two men, what is the ruling in it? Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Mujazziz al-Mudliji entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and saw Usamah and Zayd, with a blanket covering their heads, so he said: These feet are some from some; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me joyful."
"5783","6163 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me joyful, his face's lines shining, saying: Did you not see that Mujazziz looked just now at Zayd ibn Harithah and Usamah ibn Zayd and said: Some of these feet are from some. Abu Ja'far said: So a group argued with this hadith, claiming that therein is what they deemed that physiognomists' word is judged with, and lineages established with it. They said: Had it not been that, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have denied Mujazziz and said to him: What makes you know? So when he was silent and did not deny him, it indicates that word leads to a reality with which judgment is obligatory. And others opposed them in that; they said: It is not permissible to judge with a physiognomist's word in lineage nor other. And among the proof for them against the people of the first view is that the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joy with Mujazziz al-Mudliji's word they mentioned in 'A'ishah's hadith has therein no proof of what they imagined of the obligatoriness of judgment with the physiognomists' word; because Usamah's lineage had been established from Zayd before that. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not need in that anyone's word; had it not been that, Usamah would not have been ascribed to Zayd before. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only marveled at Mujazziz's correctness, as one marvels at a man's conjecture hitting the reality of the thing he conjectured, and judgment is not obligatory with that. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left denying him, because he did not undertake with that word establishing what had not been established before; so this is what this hadith bears. And there has been narrated regarding physiognomists from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, what indicates other than this."
"5784","6164 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him that marriage was in the Jahiliyyah upon four modes. Of it that a number of men gather upon a woman, not refusing whoever comes to her, and they are prostitutes, and they would erect banners upon their doors, so every one entering upon her has intercourse with her; then when she conceives and delivers her load, physiognomists are gathered for them; whomever they attach him to, he was his father, and he is called his son, not refusing that. So when Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the truth, He demolished that marriage in which that judgment was, and affirmed people upon the marriage in which no physiognomist's word is needed, and made the child for his father who claims him, so his lineage is established with that, and the prior judgment judged with the physiognomists' word was abrogated. And the prostitutes' children born in the Jahiliyyah, whoever claims one of them in Islam, he joins him."
"5785","6165-6167 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id. And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas informed me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id; Malik said in his hadith: from Sulayman ibn Yasar, and Anas said: Sulayman ibn Yasar informed me, that 'Umar used to attach the Jahiliyyah folk with them: whoever claims them in Islam. So that indicates that they were not attached to them with the physiognomists' word, so their word is as proof testifying upon that. Had their word been applied in Islam as applied in the Jahiliyyah, 'A'ishah would not have said: That is of what was demolished, when knowledge would be obligatory with it that the boy is of those men who had intercourse with a slave woman; so in abrogating that is proof that their word: no judgment with lineage establishment is obligatory with it. And the people of the first view also argued with their view with what Yunus narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id informed me, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that two men came to 'Umar, each claiming a woman's child. So he called for them a man of Banu Ka'b, a physiognomist, and he looked at them; so he said to 'Umar: They both shared in him, so 'Umar struck him with the stick; then he called the woman and said: Inform me your news. She said: This was for one of the two men coming to her while she was among her family's camels, not leaving her until she thought pregnancy continued with her, then he left her, and she shed blood upon him; then this other succeeded him, meaning the other, not leaving her until pregnancy continued with her; none knows from whom he is. So the Ka'bi exclaimed takbir, so 'Umar said to the boy: Befriend whichever you wish."
"5786","6168 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Malik who narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sulayman, the like of it."
"5787","6169 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from Yahya ibn Hatib, from his father, he said: Two men came to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, disputing over a boy of Jahiliyyah birth, this saying: He is my son, and this saying: He is my son. So 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, called for them a physiognomist of Banu al-Mustaliq and asked him about the boy; so the Mustaliqi looked at him, then said to 'Umar: By Him who honored you, they both shared in him together. So 'Umar stood to him and struck him with the stick until he lay down, then he said: By Allah, نظر took you to other than a path. Then he called the boy's mother and asked her; she said: This is for one of the two men; people had overpowered me until I bore him children, then he fell upon me as he used to do, so I conceived, as I see, and blood flow struck me, until it occurred to me there is nothing in my belly; then this other fell upon me; by Allah I do not know from which of them he is? So 'Umar said to the boy: Follow whichever you wish; so he followed one of them. 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib said: As if I look at him following one of them, so he went with him. And 'Umar said: May Allah fight the brother of Banu al-Mustaliq. They said: So in this hadith 'Umar judged with physiognomy, so it agrees what we interpreted in the hadith of Mujazziz al-Mudliji. So among the proof against them for the others is that in this hadith is what indicates the voidness of what they said, in that therein the physiognomist said: He is of them both together. So 'Umar did not make him likewise, but said to him: Befriend whichever you wish, upon what is obligatory in a boy claimed by two men; if one acknowledges, he was his father; so when 'Umar returned that to the claimed boy's judgment when two men claim him, with no physiognomist present before the imam, not to the physiognomist's word, that indicates that with the physiognomists' word no lineage establishment of anyone is obligatory. And there has been narrated from 'Umar also of sound aspects that he made him between the two men together."
"5788","6170 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Tawbah al-'Anbari, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn 'Umar, that two men shared in a woman's purity, so she bore, so 'Umar called the physiognomists, and they said: He took resemblance from them both together, so he made him between them."
"5789","6171 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from 'Umar, the like of it. He said: So Sa'id said to me: For whom do you see his inheritance? He said: It is for the last of them in death."
"5790","6172 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: 'Awf ibn Abi Jamilah narrated to me, from Abu al-Muhallab, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab judged in a man claimed by two men, each claiming he is his son, and that in the Jahiliyyah. So 'Umar called the claimed boy's mother and said: I remind you by Him who guided you to Islam, for which of them is he? She said: No, by Him who guided me to Islam, I do not know for which of them he is? This came to me first of the night, and this came to me last of the night; I do not know for which of them he is? He said: So 'Umar called four of the physiognomists, and called for sand and scattered it, ordering the two claiming men, so each trod with a foot, and ordering the claimed, so he trod with a foot; then he showed the physiognomists. He said: Look; when you come, do not speak until I ask you. He said: So the physiognomists looked and said: We have affirmed. Then he separated between them, then asked them man by man. He said: So they disputed, i.e. they contended, all testifying that this is of these two. He said: So 'Umar said: How strange what these say; I knew that the bitch is impregnated by numerous dogs, and I did not feel that women do that before this. I do not reject what they see; go, they are both your fathers."
"5791","6173 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya informed me, from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that two men shared in a woman's purity, so she bore them a boy, so they rose to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him; so he called for them three of the physiognomists, called for dust, and the two men and the boy trod in it. Then he said to one of them: Look, so he looked, faced, turned sideways and back, then said: Shall I confide or proclaim? So 'Umar said: Rather confide. He said: He took resemblance from them both together; I do not know for which of them he is? So he seated him. Then he said to the other also: Look, so he looked, faced, turned sideways and back, then said: Shall I confide or proclaim? He said: Rather confide. He said: He took resemblance from them both together; I do not know for which of them he is? And he seated him. Then he ordered the third, so he looked, faced, turned sideways and back, then said: Shall I confide or proclaim? He said: He took resemblance from them both together; I do not know for which of them he is? So 'Umar said: We know reports by their word thrice, and 'Umar was a physiognomist, so he made him for them both; they inherit him and he inherits them. So Sa'id said to me: Do you know of his 'asabah? I said: No. He said: The remainder of them both. Abu Ja'far said: So the judgment in these reports we mentioned does not vacate one of two aspects: either it is by claim, because the two men claimed the boy while he is in their hands, so he attached him to them by their claim; or it is done so. But those who judge with the physiognomists' word do not judge with their word when they say: He is the son of these two. So when their word was likewise, it is established upon their view that 'Umar's judging the child to the two men was without the physiognomists' word. And in the hadith of Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib is what indicates that, in that he said: So the physiognomists said: We do not know for which of them he is? So 'Umar made him between them. And the physiognomists did not say: He is their son; so that indicates that 'Umar established his lineage from the two men by their claim, and for their possession over him, not by the physiognomists' word. So if a speaker says: When that is as you mentioned, what was 'Umar's need for the physiognomists until he called them? It is said to him: That bears in our view, and Allah knows best, that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, fell in his heart that a pregnancy is not from two men, so attaching the child to one he knows did not beget him is impossible; so he called the physiognomists to know from them whether a child carried is from two men's fluids or not? And that was clarified by what we mentioned in the hadith of Abu al-Muhallab. So when the physiognomists informed him that that may be, and it is not impossible, he returned to the claim that was from the two men, so he judged with it, making the child the son of them both together; he inherits them and they inherit him; so that is judgment by claim, not by the physiognomists' word."
"5792","6174 - And there has been narrated from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, in that also what Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak, from a freedman of Banu Makhzumah, he said: Two men fell upon a slave girl in one purity, so the slave girl conceived, and it was not known from which of them he is. So they came to 'Umar disputing over the child; so 'Umar said: I do not know how to judge in this. So they came to 'Ali, so he said: He is between you both; he inherits you and you inherit him, and he is for the survivor of you. So this is judgment with the child to both claimants together, making him the son of them both, and he did not need in that the physiognomists' word; and with this we take. And it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"5793","6175 - Chapter: a man buying a commodity in its possession, then dying with its price upon him as debt. Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man going bankrupt, and a man finds his wealth with him intact, he is more deserving of it than others."
"5794","6176-6177 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us. And Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id; and from Husayn ibn Nasr, from Yazid ibn Harun, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to the view that when a man buys a slave for a price, takes possession of the slave and does not pay its price, then the buyer goes bankrupt with debt upon him, while the slave stands in his hand intact, that its seller is more deserving of it than others of the buyer's creditors; and they argued in that with this hadith. And others opposed them in that; they said: Rather the slave's seller and the rest of the creditors therein are equal, because his ownership has ceased from the slave and gone from his liability; he is only in demanding a debtor of the demanded debtors, demanding him with a debt in his liability, with no security in his hands; so he and they in all their wealth are equal. And among their proof against the people of the first view in the corruption of what they held, arguing for their view from the hadith of Abu Hurayrah which we mentioned, is that what is in that hadith: and a man finds his wealth intact; and one's wealth intact only falls upon the usurped, loans, trusts, and what resembles that; so that is his wealth intact, so he is more deserving of it than the rest of the creditors. And in that this hadith came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith would only be proof for the people of the first view had it been: and a man finds other than his wealth which had been his, so he sold it to him in whose hand he found it, and did not take its price from him, so he is more deserving of it than the rest of the creditors. And this which would be proof for them, had the hadith's wording been likewise. But since it is upon what we narrated in the hadith, there is no proof for them in that; and it is upon trusts, usurpations, loans and pledges: the seekers' wealth at the time of demand for them; and that as came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Samurah."
"5795","6178 - For Bahr? Rather: Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Hajjaj, from Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn 'Uqbah, from his father, from Samurah ibn Jundub, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has goods stolen or goods lost, and finds them intact in a man's hands, he is more deserving of them, and the buyer returns upon the seller with the price. Abu Ja'far said: So the people of the first view said: Had the hadith been upon what you mentioned of the interpretation you described, there would have been no need for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning that, because the common know this, let alone the special; so speech with that is superfluous, and it is not of his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) attribute to speak superfluously, nor to speak with what has no benefit. So among the proof for the others against them in that is that that is not superfluous, but correct speech, with benefit therein, in that he informed them that when a man goes bankrupt, all in his hand must be divided among his creditors; so one's ownership established for some in his hand, that he is more deserving of that, and that what was in his hand he owned and was deceived therein, so no judgment is due him therein since deceived; so he taught them with this hadith, taught them with the hadith of Samurah, and negated that the deceived whose judgment is obscure to the common deserves with that deception anything; so this is a sound وجه for this hadith. And the people of the first view said: And this hadith is narrated from other than this وجه, with wordings other than the first hadith's wordings. So they mentioned what:"
"5796","6179 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged with the commodity a man buys, then goes bankrupt while it is with him intact, its owner not paid anything of its price, that he is equal with the creditors. Abu Bakr said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged that whoever dies with a man's commodity with him intact, unpaid anything of its price, the commodity's owner is equal with the creditors."
"5797","6180 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man buying goods, then the buyer goes bankrupt, with the seller unpaid anything of its price, and he finds it intact, he is more deserving of it; but if the buyer dies, the goods' owner is equal with the creditors. They said: So by this hadith it became clear that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only intended in this first hadith the sellers, none else. So among the proof for the others against them is that this hadith is disconnected, no proof stands with its like. So if they say: We only accepted it, though disconnected, because it clarified what was obscure in the connected hadith. It is said to them: You should have - when the hadith of Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman was disturbed, al-Zuhri narrating it from him as we mentioned last, and 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrating it from him upon what we described first - returned to another's hadith, namely Bashir ibn Nahik, making it the origin of Abu Hurayrah's hadith, and dropping what contradicts it. And when you do that, the first proof returns upon you; and if you do not do that, your opponent may also say: This hadith which al-Zuhri narrated from Abu Bakr, differentiating therein between the ruling of bankruptcy and death, is other than the first hadith; so the first hadith with him is applied from where he interpreted it, and this second hadith is a disconnected irregular hadith, no proof stands with its like, so abandoning its use is obligatory. So this which we mentioned is the وجه of speech in the reports narrated in this chapter. As for its وجه by reasoning, we saw when a man sells to a man something, he may retain it until he pays the price. And if the buyer dies with debt upon him, the seller is equal with the creditors. So the seller, whenever retaining what he sold until the buyer dies, is more deserving of it than the buyer's creditors. And whenever he delivers it to the buyer and he takes possession from him, then dies, he and the rest of the creditors therein are equal. So what necessitates for him exclusivity with its price, apart from the creditors - is its remaining in his hand. So when what we described was likewise, likewise is the buyer's bankruptcy: when the slave is in the seller's hand, he is more deserving of it than the buyer's creditors. And if he had taken it from his hand to the buyer's hand, he and the rest of the creditors therein are equal; so this is a sound proof. And another proof: we saw when the buyer does not take possession, with all the price remaining for the seller, or he pays some price, with a portion remaining for him - that he is more deserving of the slave until he obtains what remains for him of the price. So by its remaining in his hand he was more deserving of it, whether all the price or some price is his; no difference between anything of that, making its ruling one ruling. So when that was likewise, and they agreed that when the buyer takes possession of the slave and pays the seller a portion of its price, then the buyer goes bankrupt, the seller is not with that remaining portion his more deserving of the slave than the rest of the creditors, but he and they therein are equal. And likewise when all its price remains for him until bankruptcy, he is not with that more deserving of the slave than the rest of the creditors; he and they therein are equal. So its ruling is equal whether all the price remains for him upon the buyer, or some price, until the buyer's bankruptcy, as both remaining for him upon him were equal, until the death which they agreed therein upon what we mentioned. So by reasoning what we mentioned of that is established, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"5798","6181-6182 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim. And Sulayman narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ash'ath, freedman of Al Humran, from al-Hasan, he said: He is equal with the creditors, and Allah knows best."
"5799","6183 - Chapter: testimony of the Bedouin. Is it accepted against the villager? Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Nafi' ibn Yazid and Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Ata', from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The testimony of the Bedouin against the villager is not accepted. So a group went to the view that the testimony of desert folk is not accepted against town folk, and they argued in that with this hadith. And others opposed them in that, saying: As for whoever of desert folk responds when called, with causes of uprightness as in upright town folk, his testimony is accepted, and he is as town folk. And whoever of them does not respond when called, his testimony is not accepted."
"5800","6184 - And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in all that what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Umm Sunbulah al-Aslamiyyah came, with a skin of milk gifting it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so she placed it with me, with a cup of hers. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and said: Welcome to Umm Sunbulah. She said: By my father and mother, I gifted you a skin of milk. He said: May Allah bless you; pour for me in this cup. So she poured for him in the cup; when he took it, I said: You said: I do not accept a gift from a Bedouin. He said: The Bedouins of Aslam, O 'A'ishah; they are not Bedouins, but they are our desert folk, and we are their town folk; when we call them they respond, and when we call they respond to us; then he drank."
"5801","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"5802","Al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Abdillah ibn Niyar, from Urwah, from A'ishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similarly, and he added at its end: So they are not Bedouins. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me that whoever among the people of the desert responds when called is like the people of settled towns, and that the nomadic Bedouins whose gifts are not accepted are different from these; they are those who do not respond when called. Whoever is like that, their testimony is not accepted, and they are those whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah which we mentioned, as we see, and Allah knows best."
"5803","Book of hunting, slaughtered animals and sacrifices. Chapter on the defects that make sacrificial animals and Hadis impermissible when present. Abu Musa Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith, Ibn Lahi'ah and al-Layth ibn Sa'd informed me that Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to them, from Ubayd ibn Fayruz, freedman of Banu Shayban, from al-Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, that he asked him about what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked among sacrificial animals, or what he forbade. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us, and my hand is shorter than his hand, and said: Four are not sufficient in sacrificial animals: the one-eyed whose blindness is evident, the lame whose lameness is evident, the sick whose sickness is evident, and the emaciated that has no marrow. Al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I would see the sheep left aside and I would walk to it, and if it blinked I would take it and sacrifice it. I said to him: I dislike any deficiency in the teeth, or deficiency in the ear, or deficiency in the horn. He said: Whatever you dislike, leave it, but do not forbid it to anyone."
"5804","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Ubayd ibn Fayruz, from al-Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: What is to be avoided among sacrificial animals? He gestured with his hand and said: Four. Al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, would gesture with his hand and say: My hand is shorter than the hand of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the lame whose defect is evident, the one-eyed whose blindness is evident, the sick whose sickness is evident, and the emaciated that has no marrow."
"5805","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid and Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us. And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: I heard Ubayd ibn Fayruz say: I asked al-Bara, and he mentioned the same."
"5806","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he said: And the emaciated that has no marrow, and he did not say: And the broken-legged. Abu Ja'far said: A group adopted this hadith and said: No sheep, camel or cow suffices if it has one of these four defects, neither as Hadi nor as sacrifice. They said: Whatever is besides these four, such as cutting of the tail or ear and the like, does not prevent the sheep, cow or camel from being offered as Hadi or sacrificed. And they also argued for that with what is mentioned below."
"5807","Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah and Sharik narrated to us, from Jabir, from Muhammad ibn Qarazah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I bought a ram to sacrifice, and a wolf attacked it and cut off its tail, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked, and he said: Sacrifice it. Others disagreed with them and said: It is not permissible to sacrifice a sheep, cow or camel that has a defect among these four defects, nor is it permissible along with that to sacrifice one with a cut ear, nor to offer it as Hadi. And they also argued for that with what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in other than this hadith."
"5808","Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Khaythamah narrated to me, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Shurayh ibn al-Nu'man, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Do not sacrifice a muqabalah, nor a mudabarah, nor a kharqa, nor a sharqa, nor a one-eyed animal."
"5809","Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Shurayh ibn al-Nu'man - Abu Ishaq said: and he was a truthful man - from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5810","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Jurayy ibn Kulayb say: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade animals with a broken horn and ear. Qatadah said: I said to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib: What is a broken ear? He said: When half or more of it is cut off."
"5811","Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Shurayh ibn al-Nu'man al-Hamdani, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sacrificing a muqabalah, mudabarah, sharqa, kharqa, or jad'a."
"5812","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed me, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Hujayyah ibn Adi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to inspect the eye and the ear."
"5813","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us. And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, both together: from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Hujayyah ibn Adi, he said: A man came to Ali and asked him about one with a broken horn. He said: It does not harm you. He said: A lame one? He said: When it reaches the place of sacrifice. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to inspect the eye and the ear. Abu Ja'far said: In these reports is the prohibition of sacrificing a muqabalah or mudabarah, and that is in the ear: what is from the front of the ear is muqabalah, and what is from its lower part is mudabarah. And Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib explained the broken ear forbidden for sacrifice; he said: It is one with half its ear cut off. Thus what he forbade of that in the ear is established, and we may not abandon it, because the hadith of al-Bara which we mentioned is either before this hadith of Ali, making this hadith of Ali an addition to it, or after it, making it abrogating it. Since the abrogation of the hadith of Ali is not known after its authenticity is known to us, we hold it firm along with the hadith of al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, and require acting upon both together. If one says: But you do not dislike a broken horn, while in the hadith of Jurayy ibn Kulayb from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), there is prohibition of it. It is said to him: We left that only because Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saw no harm in it, in what we reported from him in the hadith of Hujayyah ibn Adi, so we knew thereby that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, would not have said after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to what he heard from him, except after the abrogation of that was established with him. As for the hadith of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri which we reported through Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi, it is a defective hadith in its chain and text, as Shu'bah has explained."
"5814","Abd al-Ghani ibn Rifa'ah ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Jabir, from Muhammad ibn Qarazah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said - and we did not hear it from him - that he bought a ram to sacrifice, and its tail or part of its tail was eaten, so he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that and he said: Sacrifice it. The chain of this hadith is defective for what we mentioned, and its text is defective, because he said: Its tail or part of its tail was cut. If the part is what was cut, it may be less than a quarter, and that does not prevent sacrificing it according to anyone. Even if the hadith were as Ibrahim ibn Muhammad reported, that its tail was cut, that could also be upon part of it, for it is said: He cut its tail, when he cut part of it, as it is said: He cut his finger, when he cut part of it. The correction of these reports prevents sacrificing the four in the hadith of al-Bara, or a muqabalah and mudabarah, which is one with most of its ear slit from its front or back. When that does not suffice in sacrifices, one with a cut ear is even more undeserving to suffice. Likewise by analogy in our view, every limb cut off a sheep, like its udder or its tail, prevents sacrificing it when cut off entirely; if part of it is cut, our companions, may Allah have mercy on them, differ. As for Abu Hanifah, may Allah's mercy be upon him, it is reported from him: If what is cut is a quarter of that limb or more, what that was cut from is not valid; if less than a quarter, he may sacrifice it. Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both, said: If what is cut is half or more, what that was cut from is not to be sacrificed; if less than half, there is no harm in sacrificing it. Except that Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, mentioned that he mentioned this view to Abu Hanifah, and he said to him: My view is like your view. Thus the retraction of Abu Hanifah, may Allah's mercy be upon him, from his earlier view to what Abu Yusuf told him is established. What they said agrees with what we reported from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib in this chapter explaining the broken animal forbidden for sacrifice, that it is one with half its ear cut off. And everything of this that is diminished may not be a sacrifice, nor may it be a Hadi."
"5815","Chapter on one who slaughters on the day of sacrifice before the Imam slaughters. Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Sunayd ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed on the day of sacrifice in Madinah. Some men went ahead and slaughtered, thinking the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had slaughtered, so he ordered whoever had slaughtered before him to repeat with another slaughter, and not to slaughter until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slaughters. Abu Ja'far said: A group adopted this and said: No one may slaughter until the Imam slaughters; if he slaughters before that, after the prayer or before it, that does not suffice him. They argued with this hadith and interpreted the word of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: O you who believe, do not advance before Allah and His Messenger. Others disagreed and said: Whoever slaughters after the Imam's prayer, that suffices him; whoever slaughters before the prayer, that does not suffice him. They said: It is reported from Ibn al-Zubayr that this verse was revealed regarding another meaning. And they mentioned what follows."
"5816","Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Yusuf informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, that Ibn Abi Mulaykah informed him that Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr informed him: A delegation of Banu Tamim came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Messenger of Allah, appoint al-Qa'qa ibn Ma'bad ibn Zurarah. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Appoint al-Aqra ibn Habis. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You only meant to oppose me. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I did not mean to oppose you. They argued until their voices were raised, so Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: O you who believe, do not advance before Allah and His Messenger. And part of their proof for their view was that the hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, was reported with a different wording."
"5817","Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man slaughtered before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, a young jadh\a goat. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It will not suffice anyone after you, and he forbade them to slaughter before he prays. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith the prohibition of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was aimed at forbidding slaughter before the prayer, not before his own slaughter, and he could not forbid them slaughter before he prays except intending thereby to inform them of the permissibility of slaughter for them after he prays; otherwise mentioning the prayer would have no meaning. And there is also reported in that from other than Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what agrees with this."
"5818","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, both said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Zubayd al-Yami, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi narrating from al-Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us on the day of sacrifice to al-Baqi', and began by praying two rak'ahs, then turned to us with his face and said: The first of our rites on this day is to begin with prayer, then return and slaughter; whoever does that has conformed to our Sunnah, and whoever slaughters before that, it is only meat he hastened to his family, not part of the rite at all. My maternal uncle stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have slaughtered, and I have a jadh'ah better than a musinnah. He said: Slaughter it, but it will not suffice - or will not complete - for anyone after you."
"5819","Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Zubayd, Mansur, Dawud, Ibn Awn and Mujalid informed me, from al-Sha'bi. And this is the hadith of Zubayd, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi here narrating from al-Bara at a pillar in the mosque; had I been near it I would have told you its place, then he mentioned the same."
"5820","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mutarrif ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to us, from Zubayd, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he said: Slaughter it, but no jadh'ah will purify after. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The first of our rites on this day is to pray, then return and slaughter; whoever does that has conformed to our Sunnah. He informed that the rite on the day of sacrifice is prayer, then slaughter after it. That proves that what permits slaughter is the prayer, not the slaughter of the Imam after it, and that the ruling of slaughter after prayer differs from slaughter before it. And the like of this is also reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by other than al-Bara."
"5821","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of sacrifice; he passed by people who had slaughtered before he prayed, and said: Whoever slaughtered before the prayer, let him repeat; when we have prayed, whoever wishes may slaughter, and whoever wishes may not slaughter."
"5822","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Jundub ibn Abdillah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever slaughtered before praying, let him repeat another in its place, and whoever has not slaughtered, let him slaughter."
"5823","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, he heard Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I witnessed the sacrifice with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he learned that people had slaughtered before the prayer, and said: Whoever slaughtered, let him repeat, and whoever has not, let him slaughter in the name of Allah."
"5824","Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi informed us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas informed us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Jundub ibn Sufyan, he said: I witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after he had led the people in the Eid prayer, and there were sheep already slaughtered, so he said: Whoever slaughtered before the prayer, that is a sheep of meat; whoever has not slaughtered, let him slaughter in the name of Allah."
"5825","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad - Hammad said: and I only know it from Anas - and Hisham from Muhammad, from Anas: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, then preached, and ordered whoever had slaughtered before the prayer to repeat the slaughter. Abu Ja'far said: What we mentioned proves that the first time for slaughter on the day of sacrifice is after the prayer, not after the slaughter of the Imam. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of reports. As for what analogy indicates, we see the agreed-upon principle that if the Imam did not slaughter at all, that would neither cancel slaughter for the people nor prevent them from slaughtering that year. And there is reported from Hudhayfah ibn Asid Abu Sarihah what..."
"5826","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ashhal ibn Hatim narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from al-Sha'bi, from Abu Sarihah that Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, would not sacrifice. Abu Ja'far said: Do you think no one sacrificed in those years because their Imam did not sacrifice? Do you not see that if an Imam were occupied on the day of sacrifice fighting an enemy or otherwise, and that kept him from slaughtering, others who wish to sacrifice may sacrifice? If one says no one may sacrifice that year, he departs from the view of the Imams. If he says the people may sacrifice when the sun declines and the prayer time passes, that proves that what permits slaughter at the time of the Eid prayer is the prayer, not the slaughter of the Imam; when the Imam prays, slaughter becomes permissible for whoever wishes. Do you not see that if the Imam slaughtered before praying, that would not suffice him, just as the rest of the people? The Imam and others are alike in slaughter before prayer not sufficing them. By analogy the Imam and the rest of the people are also alike in slaughter after prayer: just as the Imam's slaughter after prayer suffices him, so the slaughter of the rest of the people after prayer suffices them. This is the analogy here, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"5827","Chapter on the camel: for how many does it suffice in sacrifices and Hadis. Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan ibn al-Hakam, both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out in the year of al-Hudaybiyah intending to visit the House, and drove the Hadi with him; the Hadi was seventy camels, the people were seven hundred men, and each camel was for ten. Abu Ja'far said: A group held that a camel suffices for ten in Hadis and sacrifices, arguing with this hadith. Others disagreed and said: A camel suffices only for seven. They said: There is reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about slaughtering camels on the day of al-Hudaybiyah what contradicts this. And they mentioned in that what follows."
"5828","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, that Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to them that they slaughtered on the day of al-Hudaybiyah one camel for seven, and one cow for seven."
"5829","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"5830","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar and Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: We slaughtered with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one camel for seven persons. It was said to Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him: And the cow? He said: It is like it. Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, was present in the year of al-Hudaybiyah; he said: And that day we slaughtered seventy camels."
"5831","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Imran narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slaughtered seventy camels on the day of al-Hudaybiyah, and ordered us that seven of us share in one camel."
"5832","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sulayman ibn Qays, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We slaughtered with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy camels, each camel for seven."
"5833","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: I heard Aban ibn Yazid narrating from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: The camel is for seven. So Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, informs from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we mentioned, and he was with him then. And there is reported from Ali and Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, from their saying what agrees with this about the camel being for seven."
"5834","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, from Amir, from Ali and Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, both said: The camel is for seven, and the cow is for seven. And the like is also reported from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, relating it from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with them."
"5835","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would share seven in one camel of camels, and seven in one camel of cattle. This is the school of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with them, about the camel, agreeing with what is reported from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, not what is reported from al-Miswar and Marwan, so it is worthier. When they differed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what we mentioned, we returned to what is reported from him in this chapter besides what he slaughtered on the day of al-Hudaybiyah."
"5836","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: A she-camel is due upon me and it has gone away from me. He said: Buy seven sheep. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith equated it with seven sheep each of which suffices for one man, and did not equate it with ten sheep? That proves the correctness of what Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported in that, not what al-Miswar reported. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for its aspect by way of analogy, we see they unanimously agreed that a cow does not suffice in sacrifice for more than seven, though it is among camels by their agreement. By analogy a she-camel is like it and does not suffice for more than seven. If one says: A she-camel, though a camel like a cow is a camel, is higher than a cow in fatness and stature. It is said to him: Even so, that gives you no proof against us. Do you not see that a medium cow suffices for seven, likewise what is below it and what is above it? Likewise a she-camel suffices for seven, whether noble or otherwise. Fatness and stature do not distinguish some cattle from others, nor some camels from others, in what suffices in Hadi and sacrifices. Rather all of it has one ruling sufficing for one number. Since that is so, and camels and cattle are all camels, their ruling is one, and some do not suffice for more than what the rest suffice for, even if some exceed others in fatness and stature. Since a cow does not suffice for more than seven, a she-camel is likewise by analogy not sufficing for more than seven. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"5837","Chapter on the sheep: for how many does it suffice to sacrifice it? Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle narrated to us. And Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, both said: Haywah narrated to us, from Abu Sakhr al-Madani, from Yazid ibn Abdillah ibn Qusayt, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a horned ram that treads in blackness, looks in blackness and kneels in blackness, and it was brought for him to sacrifice. Then he said: O A'ishah, bring the knife, then he said: Sharpen it with a stone, and she did. Then he took it and took the ram and laid it down, then slaughtered it and said: In the name of Allah, O Allah accept from Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, and from the nation of Muhammad, then he sacrificed it."
"5838","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, or from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he sacrificed, would buy two large fat white-black horned castrated rams; he would slaughter one of them for his nation, whoever among them bore witness to monotheism and bore witness to him of conveying, and the other for Muhammad and the family of Muhammad."
"5839","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Amr, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Abu Rafi', that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he sacrificed, would buy two large white-black rams; when he had preached to the people and prayed, one of them was brought while he stood in his prayer place, and he slaughtered it with his hand, then said: O Allah, this is for my entire nation, whoever bore witness to You of monotheism and bore witness to me of conveying. Then the other was brought and he slaughtered it, then said: O Allah, this is for Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, then he gathered them both and he and his family ate from them. He said: We remained years with no man of Banu Hashim sacrificing, Allah having sufficed the burden and resolve through the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5840","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, both said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir ibn Abdillah informed me, he said: My father narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought two white-black large horned castrated rams; he laid down one of them and said: In the name of Allah, Allah is Greatest, O Allah for Muhammad and his nation, whoever bore witness to You of monotheism and bore witness to me of conveying."
"5841","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi informed us, he said: Ibn Ishaq informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Ayyash, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed two rams on the day of Eid. When he faced them he said: I have turned my face to the One who created the heavens and the earth, to the end of the verse: O Allah, from You and to You, for Muhammad and his nation, then he named Allah, magnified Him and slaughtered."
"5842","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman and Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Salim informed me, from Amr, freedman of al-Muttalib, from al-Muttalib ibn Abdillah, and from a man of Banu Salamah, that both narrated to him that Jabir ibn Abdillah informed them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed for the people on the day of sacrifice. When he finished his sermon and prayer, he called for a ram and slaughtered it himself, saying: In the name of Allah, Allah is Greatest, O Allah from me and from whoever of my nation did not sacrifice."
"5843","Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Ibrahim al-Tarjumaani narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Rubayh ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id al-Khudri, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed a horned ram, then said: O Allah, this is from me and from whoever of my nation did not sacrifice. Abu Ja'far said: A group held that there is no harm in sacrificing one sheep for a group, however many, and the people of this view split into two parties: One party said: It suffices only if those sacrificed for are of one household. Another party said: It suffices whether those sacrificed for are of one household or of several households, because the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed the ram he sacrificed for his entire nation, who are of several households; if that is established for those after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it suffices for whomever the slaughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sufficed for. Thus the view of those who said it may be sacrificed for the household and others is established. Then the discussion was between these and the party opposing them all, who say: A sheep suffices for no more than one, holding that what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did, which the first two parties used as proof, is abrogated or specific. Among what proves that is that when a ram sufficed for more than one, with no limit of time or number, the cow and camel would be even more so, sufficing for more than one with no limit; yet we reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what proves otherwise, in the previous chapter, of his Companions slaughtering with him a camel for seven and a cow for seven, and that was with his Companions upon his instruction to them, that one cow or camel does not suffice for more than what it was slaughtered for that day, and reports to that effect are successive from them."
"5844","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: From Hajjaj, from Zuhayr ibn Habib, from al-Mughirah ibn Hadhf, from Ali, and from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Hujayyah ibn Adi, Abdillah ibn Tammam and Malik ibn Huwayrith - as Salamah ibn Kuhayl thought - that a man bought a cow as a sacrifice and it gave birth, so he asked Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: May I exchange another in its place? He said: No, but slaughter it and its calf on the day of sacrifice for seven."
"5845","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Rib'i, he said: The Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with them, would say: The cow is for seven."
"5846","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Husayn. And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Khalid ibn Salamah, from Abu Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The cow is for seven."
"5847","Rabi al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abdillah ibn Qusayt, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from people among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. When the cow was made for seven, and that was established, and we were not given to exceed it to more, the sheep was even more deserving not to suffice for more than the cow does. When it is established that a sheep does not suffice for more than seven, the view of whoever says it suffices for all sacrificed for, without limit or number, is negated, and its opposite is established: that a sheep suffices only for one. One said: We made the sheep suffice for more than the cow and camel suffice for, because the sheep is better than them."
"5848","It was said to him: Why do you say that? What is your proof? There is reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sacrifice a camel when found, and would not slaughter a cow or sheep while able to, then when he did not find a camel he would slaughter a cow and sheep, and a ram when he did not find a camel. Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed in this hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sacrifice a camel when he found it, proving he would leave other sacrificial animals of cattle and sheep while able, and sacrifice a sheep when he could not afford a camel; that proves the camel was with him better than the sheep. And we see in Hajj Hadis the camel given a merit not given to sheep: the camel may be shared by a group as Hadi for their Qiran and Tamattu', while the sheep is not so."
"5849","Among what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting partnership in Hadi when it is a camel: Rabi al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered one hundred camels as Hadi and associated Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in a third of it."
"5850","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drove seventy camels and shared them among them. When partnership is permissible in a camel as Hadi, and not permissible in a sheep, that proves a sheep equals only a part of a camel. And we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the previous chapter that a man said to him: A she-camel is due upon me and it has gone from me, and he ordered him to make in its place seven sheep; that also proves what we mentioned. And there is reported from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, also what agrees with this meaning."
"5851","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, he said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, was asked about what is easy of Hadi, and said: A camel and a cow, or a share in blood."
"5852","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, say, and he mentioned the same. Abdillah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed that a part of a camel equals a sheep in what is easy of Hadi. And there is also reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what proves the merit of a camel over a cow, and of a cow over a sheep."
"5853","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Abdillah al-Agharr, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Friday comes, at every gate of the mosque are angels writing the first then the first; when the Imam sits, they fold the scrolls and sit listening to the remembrance. The example of the early comer is like one who offers a camel, then like one who offers a cow, then like one who offers a ram, then like one who offers a chicken, then like one who offers an egg."
"5854","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, both said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The example of the early comer to prayer is like one who offers a camel, then the one after him like one who offers a cow, then the one after him like one who offers a ram, then the one after him like one who offers a chicken, then the one after him like one who offers an egg."
"5855","Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the like."
"5856","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5857","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, he said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned the same. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the early comer in the best time like one offering a camel, and the early comer in the next time like one offering a cow, and the early comer in the third like one offering a ram, that proves the best of what is offered is the camel, then the cow, then the ram. When the camel is the greatest of what is offered, it is the greatest of what is sacrificed. When it is negated that a sheep suffices for more than seven, it is established that it suffices only for specific persons. Since by their agreement it does not suffice in sacrifice for more than seven, a sheep is even less deserving to suffice for that; and they unanimously agreed it suffices for one, differing about more, so nothing enters what is established as specific except what they unanimously agreed enters it. Thus what we mentioned proves one sheep may not be sacrificed for two, nor for more, and this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"5858","Chapter on one who obligates a sacrifice in the ten days or resolves to sacrifice: may he cut his hair or nails? Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Thabit al-Bazzaz narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever among you sees the crescent of Dhu al-Hijjah and intends to sacrifice, let him not take from his hair and nails until he sacrifices."
"5859","Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Amr ibn Muslim, that he said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib informed me that Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), mentioned the same. Al-Layth said: This has come, but most people are upon other than it. Abu Ja'far said: A group adopted this hadith, followed it and made it a basis. Others disagreed and said: There is no harm in cutting nails and hair in the ten days, for one who resolved to sacrifice and one who did not. They argued with what we mentioned in the book of Hajj from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: I used to twist the garlands of the Hadi of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would send them, then remain among us in a state of permissibility, avoiding nothing that a pilgrim avoids, until the people return. In that is proof of the permissibility of what the first hadith prohibited. And the transmission of the hadith of A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, is better, for it came successively transmitted; the hadith of Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, did not come so, but the chain of Malik's hadith was criticized: it was said it is stopped at Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her."
"5860","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Malik informed us, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her - and he did not raise it - she said: Whoever sees the crescent of Dhu al-Hijjah and intends to sacrifice, let him not take from his hair nor his nails until he sacrifices."
"5861","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, the same - and he did not raise it. This is the basis of the hadith from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of reports. As for analogy, we see Ihram prohibits things previously all lawful, including: intercourse, kissing, cutting nails, shaving hair, and killing game; all these become forbidden by Ihram, with differing rulings. Intercourse: whoever does it in Ihram nullifies his Ihram; other acts do not nullify Ihram. Intercourse is thus the gravest of what Ihram forbids. Then we see one upon whom the ten days enter while intending to sacrifice is not thereby prevented from intercourse; since that does not prevent him from intercourse, the gravest of what Ihram forbids, it is even more deserving not to prevent what is below it. This is the analogy in this chapter also, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all. And that is also reported from a group of the predecessors."
"5862","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me. And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abdillah ibn Qusayt, that Ata ibn Yasar, Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, and Abu Bakr ibn Sulayman saw no harm in a man taking from his hair and trimming his nails in the ten of Dhu al-Hijjah. And some of our companions also argued in that with what follows."
"5863","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me, from Uthman ibn Ubaydillah ibn Abi Rafi', from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz, from Muhammad ibn Rabi'ah, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saw me with a long mustache at Dhu al-Hulayfah while I was upon my she-camel intending Hajj, and ordered me to cut from my hair, and I did. There is no proof with us in this, because one intending Hajj does not intend to sacrifice, so there is no proof in this against the people of the first view, for they only forbid that to one who intends to sacrifice. Another proof repels this hadith from being a proof against them: it does not mention whether that was in the ten of Dhu al-Hijjah or before it."
"5864","Chapter on slaughtering with tooth and nail. Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir and Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, both said: Shu'bah narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, both together: from Simak ibn Harb, from Murri ibn Qatari, a man of Banu Tha'labah, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I send my dog and it catches game, and I have nothing to slaughter it with except a flint and a stick. He said: Make the blood flow with whatever you wish, and mention the name of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. Abu Ja'far said: A group held that what is slaughtered with a removed or unremoved tooth and nail is permissible, arguing with this hadith. Others disagreed: they disliked what is slaughtered with them when unremoved, and permitted what is slaughtered with them when removed."
"5865","They argued in that with what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh and Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, both said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from his grandfather Rafi' ibn Khadij, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, we will meet the enemy tomorrow and we have no knives. He said: Whatever makes the blood flow and Allah's name is mentioned over it, eat it; not the tooth and nail. And I shall inform you: As for the nail, it is the knives of Abyssinia; as for the tooth, it is bone."
"5866","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to me, from his father, from Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, from his grandfather Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): We hope - or fear - to meet the enemy, and we have no knives; may we slaughter with reeds? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever makes the blood flow and Allah's name is mentioned over it, eat, except the tooth and nail. In this hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) excluded tooth and nail from what he permitted slaughter with. That could be upon removed ones or upon both removed and unremoved. If upon removed ones, then unremoved ones are even more so. If upon unremoved ones, there is no proof of the ruling of removed ones. Since knowledge encompasses the prohibition falling upon unremoved ones, but does not encompass its falling upon removed ones, and the hadith of Adi which we mentioned came unrestricted, we excluded from it what knowledge encompasses the hadith of Rafi' excluding from it, and left what knowledge does not encompass the hadith of Rafi' excluding from it, upon the unrestrictedness of the hadith of Adi ibn Hatim, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5867","There is reported from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in this what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, from Abu Raja al-Utaridi, he said: We set out as pilgrims, and a man of the company hunted a rabbit and slaughtered it with his nail, roasted it and they ate it, but I did not eat with them. When we came to Madinah, I asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both; he said: Perhaps you ate with them? I said: No. He said: You did right; he only killed it by strangling."
"5868","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Salam ibn Zarir narrated to us, from Abu Raja, the same. Do you not see that Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, explained in his hadith the meaning by which what is slaughtered with a nail is forbidden: it is strangling, because what is slaughtered with it is slaughtered with a palm, not otherwise, so it is strangled? That proves what is forbidden of slaughter with a nail is the nail fixed in the palm, not the removed nail. Likewise what is forbidden along with it of slaughter with a tooth is upon the tooth fixed in the mouth, for that is biting; as for the removed tooth, no. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"5869","Chapter on eating sacrificial meats after three days. Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Ubayd, freedman of Abd al-Rahman, that he heard Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, say on the day of sacrifice: O people, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade you to eat your rites after three, so do not eat them after it."
"5870","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Ubayd, freedman of Ibn Azhar, narrated to me, he said: I prayed with Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, the Eid while Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, was besieged; he prayed then preached and said: Do not eat of your sacrificial meats after three days, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered that."
"5871","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya al-Kalbi narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Eat of it three - meaning sacrificial meats."
"5872","Rabi al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth informed us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he would say: Let none of you eat of his sacrificial meat beyond three days. A group adopted this and forbade sacrificial meats after three days, arguing with these reports. Others disagreed and saw no harm in eating and storing them."
"5873","They argued in that with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ma'n ibn Isa narrated to us: from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Thawban, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slaughtered his sacrifice, then said: O Thawban, prepare the meat of this sacrifice. I kept feeding him from it until he arrived in Madinah."
"5874","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Yazid, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We would indeed eat it after twenty - meaning sacrificial meats."
"5875","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abi Namir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id al-Khudri, from his father and his uncle Qatadah, may Allah be pleased with them, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Eat sacrificial meats and store them. It may be that one of these two meanings we mentioned is a proof for one of the two views, abrogating the other meaning, so we examined that."
"5876","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Zayd narrated to me, he said: al-Nabighah ibn Mukhariq ibn Sulaym narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me that Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I had forbidden you to store sacrificial meats beyond three days, so store them as you wish."
"5877","Rabi al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, both said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Rabi'ah ibn al-Nabighah, from his father, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5878","Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Ayyub ibn Hani, from Masruq ibn al-Ajda', from Abdillah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5879","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5880","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us. And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, both said: Ma'ruf ibn Wasil narrated to us, he said: Muharib ibn Dithar narrated to me, then he mentioned the same with its chain."
"5881","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5882","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me that Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban informed him, that al-Wasi' ibn Habban informed him, that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5883","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he heard him narrating from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, that they would eat sacrifices in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for three days, not exceeding it, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) afterwards permitted them to eat and take provision."
"5884","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, similarly."
"5885","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Zubayd, that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that he came to his family and found with them a bowl of tharid and meat of sacrificial meat, and refused to eat it. Then Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, his brother, came and narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of Hajj said: I had forbidden you not to eat sacrificial meats beyond three days, and now I permit it to you, so eat of it as you wish."
"5886","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdillah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Malih, from Nubayshah al-Khayr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I forbade you sacrificial meats beyond three days so that it would suffice you, for Allah has brought ease, so eat and store; these days are days of eating and drinking and remembrance of Allah, the Exalted."
"5887","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and Malik informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating sacrificial meats after three, then permitted it and said: Eat, take provision and store. Amr said: Abu al-Zubayr said: Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: So we took provision of it to Madinah."
"5888","Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: Idris ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Mudar, he said: Khalid ibn Yazid informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We sacrificed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mina and took provision of it to Madinah."
"5889","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, from Zaynab bint Ka'b, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade storing sacrificial meats beyond three, and ordered us to eat of it and give charity of it, and not to eat it after three; we remained upon that as Allah willed, then it occurred to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to order us to eat it, give charity of it, and that whoever loves may store it."
"5890","Rabi al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Ya'qub, from Yazid ibn Abi Yazid, Yazid al-Ansari, from his wife, that she asked A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about sacrificial meats. She said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, came from a journey, and we served him of it, but he said: I shall not eat until I ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he asked him and he said: Eat from one Dhu al-Hijjah to the next Dhu al-Hijjah."
"5891","Bahr narrated to us, from Shu'ayb, from his father, from al-Harith ibn Ya'qub, from Yazid ibn Abi Yazid, freedman of the Ansar, then he mentioned the same with its chain. Abu Ja'far said: In these reports is what proves the abrogation of what we reported at the beginning of this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of forbidding sacrificial meats beyond three days. If it is said: You reported from Ali in this section, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he permitted sacrificial meats after he had forbidden them. Then you reported from him in the previous section that he preached to the people while Uthman was besieged, saying: Do not eat of your sacrificial meats after three days, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to order that. That proves the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that after he had permitted it, so that what you reported from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, agrees and does not contradict. It is said to him: There is no proof in this of what you mentioned, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may have forbidden sacrificial meats beyond three days due to hardship the people were in, then that hardship was lifted and he permitted them that, then that recurred at the time Ali preached to the people, so he ordered them with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had ordered them in a like case."
"5892","The proof of what we mentioned is that Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abis narrated to us, from his father, he said: I entered upon A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: O Mother of the Believers, did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid eating sacrificial meats beyond three days? She said: He did that only in a year when people hungered, wanting the rich to feed the poor. She said: We would raise the trotter for fifteen nights. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith proves that prohibition was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only due to the circumstance mentioned in this hadith. When that circumstance was lifted, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted them what he had prohibited, as we mentioned in the first reports in the previous section. Therefore what Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, did in the time of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, ordering the people after his knowledge of the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he had forbidden them, was with us - and Allah knows best - due to straitened circumstances like those in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he forbade them sacrificial meats beyond three days. So Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered them in their days with the like of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had ordered the people in the like of it. And there is reported from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade that due to a arriving group that came upon them."
"5893","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed us, from Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: People of the desert came hastening, and I attended to sacrifice, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Store a third, and give charity of the rest. She said: Afterwards I said: O Messenger of Allah, people used to benefit from their sacrifices, carrying of it fat and making of it waterskins. He said: What is that? I said: You forbade keeping sacrificial meats after three. He said: I only forbade you due to the arriving party that came, so eat, give charity and take provision."
"5894","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, and he mentioned the same with its chain. A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not forbidden it, but intended to widen provision for the arriving party that had come upon them. The meaning of this hadith thus returns to the meaning of the hadith of Abis from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her. And this hadith is reported from Abis from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, with a different wording."
"5895","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abis ibn Rabi'ah, he said: I came to A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: O Mother of the Believers, did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid sacrificial meats beyond three? She said: No, but only few of them had sacrificed, so he did that so that whoever among them sacrificed would feed whoever did not sacrifice; I saw us hiding the trotter, then eating it after three. It may be that arriving party was numerous, and the people sacrificing with them few, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them with what he ordered of charity due to that. The meaning of this also returns to the meaning of what preceded. And there is also reported from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that that saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not as a binding command, but as encouragement to charity."
"5896","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Abdillah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said regarding sacrificial meats: We used to salt of it, and the people brought it to Madinah, so he said: Do not eat except three days - not as a binding command, but intending they feed of it. The prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of sacrificial meats beyond three days is thus of one of two aspects: either it was encouragement from him to charity and good; if upon encouragement to charity, not prohibition, that proves no harm in storing sacrificial meats and eating them after three. If it was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as prohibition, then after that came what abrogated it and required permissibility. Thus what we mentioned proves the permissibility of storing sacrificial meats and eating them within three and after, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"5897","Among what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding every fanged beast of prey: Rabi al-Mu'adhdhin and Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Majid ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every fanged beast of prey, and every taloned bird."
"5898","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every fanged beast of prey, and every taloned bird."
"5899","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, and he mentioned the same with its chain, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade."
"5900","Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mu'min al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"5901","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Hakam, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5902","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Salim informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith al-Makhzumi, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating every fanged beast of prey."
"5903","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5904","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Biraki narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. The proof is established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding eating every fanged beast of prey, and reports to that effect are successive from him. It is not permissible to exclude the hyena from that, if it is a fanged beast of prey, except with what establishes proof obliging us to exclude it. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"5905","Chapter on the game of Madinah. Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim al-Taymi narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed us upon a pulpit of brick, with a sword upon which was hung a paper, and said: By Allah, we have no book to read except the Book of Allah, and what is in this paper. Then he spread it, and in it was: Madinah is sacred, from Ayr to Thawr."
"5906","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muhammad, from Amir ibn Sa'd, that Sa'd rode to his palace at al-Aqiq and found a boy cutting a tree or gathering firewood. Abu Ja'far, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I think in it: So he took his spoil. When he returned, the boy's family came and spoke to him to return what he took from their boy. He said: Allah forbid that I return something the Messenger of Allah gave me as spoil, and refused to return it to them."
"5907","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from his father, from Ya'la ibn Hakim, from Sulayman ibn Abi Abdillah, he said: I witnessed Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, when people came to him about their slave whose spoil Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas had taken; he saw him hunting in the sanctuary of Madinah which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made sacred, and took his spoil. They spoke to him to return his spoil, but he refused and said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) marked the boundaries of the sanctuary, he made Madinah sacred and said: Whomever you find hunting in any of these boundaries, whoever finds him, his spoil is his. I shall not return to you a sustenance the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed me; but if you wish, I shall pay you the price of his spoil, I shall do it."
"5908","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah informed us, from Uthman ibn Hakim, he said: Amir ibn Sa'd informed me, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made sacred what is between the two lava tracts of Madinah, that its thorn trees be cut or its game killed."
"5909","Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, he said: Abu Thabit Imran ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Abdillah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Munba'ith, from Salih ibn Ibrahim, from his father, he said: I hunted a bird at al-Qunbulah and went out with it in my hand - my father Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, met me and said: What is this? I said: A bird I hunted at al-Qunbulah. He twisted my ear severely, then released it from my hand - then he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the game of what is between its two lava tracts."
"5910","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Yunus ibn Yusuf, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, that he found boys who had driven a fox into a corner, and he drove them away. Malik said: I only know that he said: In the sanctuary of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is this done?"
"5911","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Shaybani narrated to us, from Yusayr ibn Amr, from Sahl ibn Hunayf, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - or he pointed with his hand to Madinah - say: It is a secure sanctuary."
"5912","Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar al-Ramadi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Shurahbil, he said: Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us while we were setting traps of ours in Madinah, and threw them away, saying: Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade its game?"
"5913","Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abdillah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibrahim, peace be upon him, made Makkah sacred and supplicated for them, and I have made Madinah sacred and supplicated for them with the like of what Ibrahim supplicated for the people of Makkah: that they be blessed in their sa' and mudd."
"5914","Ali narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya informed me, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"5915","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibrahim, peace be upon him, made the House of Allah sacred and secure, and I have made Madinah sacred, what is between its two lava tracts; its thorn trees shall not be cut, nor its game hunted."
"5916","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us. And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, from Zaynab bint Ka'b, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade what is between the two lava tracts of Madinah: that its trees be lopped or beaten for leaves."
"5917","Husayn ibn Nasr and Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, both said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us, he said: Utbah ibn Muslim, freedman of Banu Taym, informed me, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made sacred what is between the two lava tracts of Madinah."
"5918","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Utbah ibn Humayd, that Marwan ibn al-Hakam preached and mentioned Makkah, its sanctity and its people, but did not mention Madinah, its sanctity and its people. Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and said: Why do I hear you mention Makkah, its sanctity and its people, but not mention Madinah, its sanctity and its people? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made sacred what is between the two lava tracts of Madinah, and that with us is in the Khawlani hide; if you wish, I shall have you read it. Marwan said: I have heard."
"5919","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, both said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Abdillah ibn Amr ibn Uthman, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention Makkah, then say: Ibrahim, peace be upon him, made Makkah sacred, and I have made sacred what is between its two lava tracts, meaning Madinah."
"5920","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Amr, freedman of al-Muttalib, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked upon Uhud and said: This is a mountain that loves us and we love it. O Allah, Ibrahim made Makkah sacred, and I make sacred what is between its two lava tracts."
"5921","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Amr, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similarly."
"5922","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5923","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Asim, he said: I asked Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) make Madinah sacred? He said: Yes, it is sacred from such-and-such to such-and-such."
"5924","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5925","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Asim, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made Madinah sacred, what is between such-and-such to such-and-such, that its trees not be lopped."
"5926","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Asim al-Ahwal, he said: I heard Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, say: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, adding: Whoever innovates in it an innovation, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels and all people."
"5927","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he would say: If I saw gazelles pasturing in Madinah, I would not frighten them, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: What is between its two lava tracts is sacred."
"5928","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Zayd, from al-Walid ibn Rabah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibrahim made Makkah sacred, and I make Madinah sacred with the like of what he made sacred. He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that its trees be lopped or beaten for leaves, or its birds taken. Abu Ja'far said: A group held the game of Madinah and its trees forbidden, making it in that like Makkah in the sanctity of its game and trees. They said: Whoever does any of that in the sanctuary of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his spoil becomes lawful to whoever finds him doing it, arguing with these reports. Others disagreed and said: As for your mention of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding the game of Madinah and its trees, he did so not making it like the sanctity of the game of Makkah nor its trees, but intending thereby to preserve the adornment of Madinah, so they would find it pleasant and love it. And we see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade demolishing the fortresses of Madinah, saying: They are the adornment of Madinah."
"5929","Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from al-Umari, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the fortresses of Madinah to be demolished."
"5930","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: al-Umari narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"5931","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi informed us, he said: Abdillah ibn Nafi' narrated to me, from his father, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not demolish the fortresses, for they are the adornment of Madinah."
"5932","Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them demolishing the fortresses of Madinah because they are an adornment for it? They said: Likewise what he forbade them, of cutting its trees and killing its game, is only because that is an adornment for Madinah, wanting to leave for them its adornment so they love it and their dwelling in it is pleasant, not because it is in that like Makkah in the sanctity of its game and vegetation, and the obligation of recompense upon whoever violates the sanctity of any of that. Then we looked: do we find from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that another proof guiding us to what we mentioned?"
"5933","Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: We read to Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i, from al-Thaqafi, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Abu Talhah had a son from Umm Sulaym called Abu Umayr; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would joke with him when he entered, and he had a small bird (nughayr). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and saw Abu Umayr sad, and said: What is the matter with Abu Umayr? It was said: O Messenger of Allah, his nughayr died. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Umayr, what happened to the nughayr?"
"5934","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Abu Talhah had a son called Abu Umayr; he had a nughayr, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he entered would say: O Abu Umayr, what happened to the nughayr?"
"5935","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would mix with us, even saying to a small brother of mine: O Abu Umayr, what happened to the nughayr?"
"5936","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Umarah ibn Zadhan narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I had a brother; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would face him and say: O Abu Umayr, what happened to the nughayr? Abu Ja'far said: This was in Madinah; had the ruling of its game been like the ruling of the game of Makkah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have allowed him to keep the nughayr nor play with it, just as that is not allowed in Makkah. One said: It may be that this was at Qanat, and that place is other than the sanctuary place, so you have no proof in this hadith. So we looked: do we find in other than this hadith what proves something of the ruling of the game of Madinah?"
"5937","Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, both said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from Mujahid, he said: A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The household of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a wild animal; when he went out, it would play and run, come and go, but when it sensed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had entered, it would lie down and not move, lest it harm him. This was in Madinah in a place included in what was made sacred of it, yet they would shelter wild animals in it, take them, and close doors upon them. This also proves that the ruling of Madinah in that differs from the ruling of Makkah. And there is..."
"5938","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Qutaylah al-Madani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talhah al-Taymi narrated to us, from Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from his father, from Abu Salamah, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', that he would hunt and bring the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his game, then was late, then came to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What kept you? He said: O Messenger of Allah, game moved away from us, so we hunt between Tit and Qanat. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had you hunted at al-Aqiq, I would have escorted you when you go and met you when you come, for I love al-Aqiq."
"5939","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talhah al-Taymi narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"5940","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith ibn Khalid al-Taymi narrated to me, then he mentioned the same with its chain. In this hadith is what proves the permissibility of the game of Madinah: do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) directed Salamah, while he was in it, to the place of game, and that is not lawful in Makkah? Do you not see that if a man, while in Makkah, directed a man to game of its game, he would be sinful? Since Madinah in that is not like Makkah, the ruling of its game is established differing from the ruling of the game of Makkah; in this hadith is also the permissibility of the game of al-Aqiq. And we reported from Sa'd in the first section from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that what we reported, and in this is what contradicts it. As for what is in the hadith of Sa'd permitting the spoil of one who hunts the game of Madinah, that with us - and Allah knows best - was at the time when punishments due for sins were in wealth. Among that is what is reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Zakat, that he said: Whoever pays it willingly, his is its reward; whoever does not, we shall take it from him and half his wealth. And what is reported from him about one who steals fruit from its sheaths, that upon him is double compensation, and many likes of that which we mentioned in their place in this book of ours. Then that was abrogated at the time of the abrogation of usury: taken things were returned to their likes, if they had likes, or to their value if they had no like, and punishments for violating the sanctuary were made in bodies, not wealth. This is the aspect of what is reported about the game of Madinah. As for its ruling by way of analogy, we see Makkah sacred, and its game and trees likewise - this none among Muslims disputes. Then we see whoever intends to enter Makkah may not enter it except in Ihram; entering the sanctuary is not lawful for one in permissibility, and the sanctity of its game and trees was like its sanctity in itself. Then we see Madinah: all unanimously agreed there is no harm in entering it for a man in permissibility; since it is not itself sacred, the ruling of its game and trees is like its ruling in itself. Just as the game of Makkah became forbidden due to its sanctity, and Madinah is not itself sacred, its game and trees are not sacred. Thus the view of whoever held the game of Madinah and its trees like the game and trees of other lands besides Makkah is established. This is also the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"5941","Chapter on eating lizards. Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj ibn Sulayman al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ata narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hasanah, he said: We alighted in a land of many lizards, and hunger struck us, so we cooked of them; the pots were boiling with them when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and said: What is this? We said: Lizards we caught. He said: A nation of the Children of Israel was transformed into beasts on earth, and I fear these are they, so overturn them."
"5942","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Wahb al-Juhani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Hasanah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, then he mentioned the same. Abu Ja'far said: A group held lizard meats forbidden, for they were not sure they are not transformed, arguing with this hadith. Others disagreed and saw no harm in them; among their proof was that Husayn reported this hadith from Zayd ibn Wahb with a meaning contrary to what al-A'mash reported it with."
"5943","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Husayn, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Thabit ibn Zayd al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); people caught lizards, roasted and ate them. I caught a lizard, roasted it, then brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he took a palm stalk and began counting his fingers with it, saying: A nation of the Children of Israel was transformed into beasts on earth, and I do not know, perhaps it is they? I said: People roasted them and ate them. He neither ate nor forbade."
"5944","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Husayn, and he mentioned the same with its chain, except that he said: Thabit ibn Wadi'ah. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is contrary to what is in the first hadith, for in this the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not forbid them eating it, though he feared in this hadith it may be transformed, as he feared in the first hadith. Yet it may be that he left forbidding because they were in hunger, as in the hadith of al-A'mash, permitting that to them due to necessity. Then we returned to what is in that besides these two hadiths."
"5945","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid and Affan narrated to us, both said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Husayn, a man of Banu Fazarah, he said: Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, informed me that an Arab Bedouin came to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he preached, interrupted his sermon and said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about the lizard? He said: A nation of the Children of Israel was transformed, and I do not know which beasts were transformed."
"5946","6339 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Shu'bah, he said: al-Hakam narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from al-Bara ibn Azib, from Thabit ibn Wadi'ah al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that a lizard was brought to him and he said: A nation that was transformed."
"5947","6340 - Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Zayd ibn Wahb, from al-Bara ibn Azib, from Thabit ibn Wadi'ah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a lizard. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: A nation was lost, and Allah knows best."
"5948","6341 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Adiyy ibn Thabit, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Thabit ibn Wadi'ah, that a man from Banu Fazarah brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) some lizards he had caught, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began turning them over and looking at a lizard among them. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A nation that was transformed, and we do not know what became of it, and I do not know, perhaps this is from it."
"5949","6342 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Bishr narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'afa ibn Imran narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused to eat it, meaning the lizard, and said: I do not know, perhaps it is from the early generations which were transformed. Abu Ja'far said: So in these reports is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left eating it, fearing that it might be from what was transformed. So it is possible that he prohibited it along with that, and it is possible that he left it refraining from eating it, and did not prohibit it, so we examined that matter."
"5950","6343 - And behold, Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Aqil Bashir ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nadrah narrated to us, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that a Bedouin asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I am in a garden full of lizards, and it is the food of my family, and he remained silent. So we said to him: Ask him again, and he asked him again, and he remained silent, then we said to him: Ask him again, and he asked him again, so he said: Allah was angry with a tribe from the Children of Israel and transformed them into beasts crawling upon the earth, and I do not think them to be but these, and I neither eat it nor prohibit it. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not prohibit lizards, despite his fear that they might be from the transformed. Then we examined: was there narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what negates that lizards are transformed?"
"5951","6344 - And behold, Abu Bakrah had narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from al-Mughirah ibn Abdillah al-Yashkuri, from al-Ma'rur ibn Suwayd, from Abdillah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about monkeys and pigs: are they from what was transformed? So he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, never destroyed a people, or never transformed a people, and then gave them progeny or offspring."
"5952","6345 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, and added: And monkeys and pigs existed before that."
"5953","6346 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adiyy informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Mis'ar, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from al-Mughirah al-Yashkuri, from al-Ma'rur, from Abdillah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah never destroyed a people and then gave them progeny or offspring."
"5954","6347 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, from Layth, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from al-Ma'rur ibn Suwayd, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified in this hadith that the transformed have no progeny nor offspring, so we knew by that that had the lizard been from what was transformed, it would not have remained, so it is thereby negated that the lizard is objectionable, whether on the grounds that it was transformed or on the grounds of what might possibly have been transformation. Then we examined what was narrated therein contrary to what we mentioned: do we find in any of that what indicates to us the permissibility of eating it, or prohibition of that?"
"5955","6348 - And behold, Husayn ibn Nasr and Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Iyas had narrated to us, they said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Musa informed us, from Husayn ibn Waqid, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said one day: Would that we had a cake of brown wheat, mixed with ghee and milk. Then a man from his companions stood and prepared it, then brought it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In what was its ghee? He said: In a skin for lizard fat. He said to him: Take it away. Then one may say: In this hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, is what indicates the dislike of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for eating lizard meat. It is said to him: It is possible that this was upon the dislike which Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his hadith which we have narrated from him, not upon his prohibiting it to the people. And there was also narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, what indicates that."
"5956","6349 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a lizard, and he neither ate it nor prohibited it."
"5957","6350 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to me, from Abdillah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: A man called out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: What do you say about the lizard? So he said: I neither eat it nor prohibit it."
"5958","6351 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the lizard, and he mentioned the like of it."
"5959","6352 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Amir al-Bajali narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Mighwal narrated to us, he said: I heard Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the lizard, so he said: I do not eat, nor do I forbid."
"5960","6353 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Warqa narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5961","6354 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5962","6355 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from Abdillah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So this Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, informs from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he did not prohibit eating the lizard. And there was narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: It is lawful."
"5963","6356 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Abd al-Samad narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Tawbah al-Anbari, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi saying: I saw so-and-so when he narrates from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I sat with Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and I did not hear him narrate from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except that he said: Some people from among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were eating a lizard, when a woman from among the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called out to them: It is a lizard. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Eat it, it is not from my food; and in the hadith of Wahb: For it is lawful. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that it is lawful, and that he left it because it was not from his food. And there was also narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not prohibit it."
"5964","6357 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, he said: I asked Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, about the lizard. So he said: It was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: I do not eat it. And Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not prohibit it, and Allah benefits more than one person by it, and it is the food of most shepherds, and had I it I would eat it. And some people disliked eating the lizard, among them Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all. And Muhammad ibn al-Hasan argued for them in that with what [follows]."
"5965","6358 - Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, (H). 6359 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, (H). 6360 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Hammad, and he is Ibn Abi Sulayman, narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was gifted a lizard and did not eat it. Then a beggar stood over them, and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, wanted to give it to him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Would you give what you yourself do not eat? Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on him, said: So that indicates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked for himself and for others eating the lizard, he said: So by that we take. It is said to him: There is no proof in this for what you mentioned. It is possible that he disliked for her to feed the beggar with it, because she only did that because she found it distasteful, and had she not found it distasteful she would not have fed it to him, and what one feeds the beggar is only for Allah, Exalted. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted that what is used to draw near to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, be only from the best of food, just as he forbade giving bad unripe dates and bad dates in charity. So from what was narrated from him in that is what [follows]."
"5966","6361 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded charity, then a man came with a bunch of this palm - Sufyan said: meaning unripe dates - and no one would bring anything except that it was attributed to the one who brought it, so it was revealed: {And do not aim at the bad thereof to spend} . And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade ju'rur and the lawn of hubayq to be taken in charity; al-Zuhri said: Two kinds of the dates of Madinah."
"5967","6362 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade ju'rur and the lawn of hubayq."
"5968","6363 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Abu Malik, from al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: They used to come in charity with the worst of their dates and the worst of their food, so it was revealed: {O you who believe, spend from the good things which you have earned, and from what We have brought forth for you from the earth, and do not aim at the bad thereof to spend, while you would not take it except with closed eyes} . He said: Had it been yours and they gave it to you, you would not take it except while seeing that it had diminished you of your right."
"5969","6364 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Himran narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Salih, from Abu Murrah, from Awf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: While we were in the mosque, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us, and in his hand was a stick, and bunches hung in the mosque, in them a cluster of dry bad dates, so he said: Had the owner of this cluster wished, he would have given charity better than it; the owner of this charity will eat dry bad dates on the Day of Resurrection. Then he turned to the people and said: By Allah, they will surely leave it made easy for forty years for wild grazers, meaning: the palms of Madinah."
"5970","6365 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Abi Arib narrated to me, from Kathir ibn Murrah al-Hadrami, from Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So this is the meaning for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked for Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, giving the lizard in charity, not because its eating is unlawful."
"5971","6366 - And there was also narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), regarding the permissibility of eating it, what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus and Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that he informed them, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, entered with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the house of Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, and a roasted lizard was brought, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stretched his hand toward it. Then some of the women who were in the house of Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Inform the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he wants to eat of it. So they said: It is a lizard, and he lifted his hand, so I said: Is it unlawful? So he said: No, but it was not in the land of my people, so I find myself disliking it. So I pulled it to me and ate it, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was looking at me, and he did not forbid me."
"5972","6367 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from al-Shaybani, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, he said: We were invited to a wedding in Madinah, and food was brought near to us and we ate it, then thirteen lizards were brought near to us, so among us was one who ate, and among us one who left. So in the morning I came to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and informed him of that, then some of those with him said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I neither eat it, nor prohibit it, nor command it, nor forbid it. So Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not sent as one permitting or prohibiting [of himself]. Meat was brought near to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he extended his hand to eat. Then Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: O Messenger of Allah, it is lizard meat, so he withheld his hand, then said: This is meat I have never eaten at all. Then al-Fadl ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, ate, and his wife was with them. And Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I do not eat food from which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not eat."
"5973","6368 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Habib al-Mu'allim narrated to us, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a large dish in which were lizards, so he said: Eat, for I find it distasteful."
"5974","6369 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: My maternal aunt, Umm Hufayd, gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dried yogurt, ghee, and lizards, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate from the dried yogurt and the ghee, and did not eat from the lizards, and it was eaten upon the table of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and had it been unlawful it would not have been eaten upon his table, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So it is established by the authentication of these reports that there is no harm in eating the lizard, and it is the position with us, and Allah knows best the correct."
"5975","11 - Chapter: Eating the flesh of domestic donkeys. 6370 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar ibn Kidam narrated to us, from Ubayd ibn Hasan, from Ibn Ma'qil, from two men from Muzaynah, one of them from the other, Abdillah ibn Umar ibn Luwaym, and the other Ghalib ibn al-Abjar. Mis'ar said: I think Ghalib is the one who asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: O Messenger of Allah, nothing remains of my wealth from which I can feed my family except donkeys of mine. He said: So feed your family from the fat of your wealth, for I only deemed filthy for you the roaming beasts of the village."
"5976","6371 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ubayd ibn Hasan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ma'qil, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Bishr, from men of Muzaynah from among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the outer region, from Abjar, or Ibn Abjar, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, nothing remains of my wealth with which I can feed my family except donkeys of mine. He said to me: So feed your family from the fat of your wealth, for I only disliked for you the roaming beasts of the village."
"5977","6372 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: I heard Ubayd ibn al-Hasan, from Abdillah ibn Ma'qil, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Bishr, that some people from among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from Muzaynah, narrated from the chief of Muzaynah, al-Abjar, or Ibn al-Abjar, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"5978","6373 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Except that he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ma'qil, and said: From men of Muzaynah of the outer region, and he did not say from among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: That Abjar, or Ibn Abjar. Abu Ja'far said: So some people went to this, and permitted eating the flesh of domestic donkeys, and argued in that with this hadith. Others disagreed with them in that, and disliked eating the flesh of domestic donkeys, and said: It is possible that the donkeys which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted eating in this hadith were wild, and that the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for I only disliked for you the roamer of the village, was upon the domestic ones. And Sharik narrated this hadith of Ghalib contrary to what Mis'ar and Shu'bah narrated."
"5979","6374 - Ibn Abi Dawud, Yahya ibn Uthman, and Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, they said: Yusuf ibn Adiyy narrated to us, (H), and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated to us, (H). 6375 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, each of them adding over the other, they said: Sharik narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir, from Ubayd ibn al-Hasan, from Ghalib ibn Abjar, he said: It was said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A year of drought has struck us, and the fat of our wealth is in donkeys, so he said: Eat from the fat of your wealth. So he informed that what he had permitted them of that was in a year of drought. So if that was upon what we carried the hadith of Mis'ar and Shu'bah, then it is upon what we carried it of that. And if that was upon domestic donkeys, then it was only in a state of necessity, and carrion becomes lawful in a state of necessity. So there is no proof in this hadith for the ruling of the flesh of domestic donkeys in other than a state of necessity. And reports have come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in successive abundance, in his forbidding eating the flesh of domestic donkeys. So from what was narrated from him in that is what had [been narrated as follows]."
"5980","6376 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus, Usamah, and Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Hasan and Abdillah, the two sons of Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib, from their father, that he heard Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with them, saying to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating the flesh of tame donkeys and temporary marriage with women on the day of Khaybar."
"5981","6377 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Salim informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith al-Makhzumi, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade on the day of Khaybar eating the flesh of tame donkeys."
"5982","6378 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade on the day of Khaybar eating the flesh of domestic donkeys."
"5983","6379 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"5984","6380 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Duhaym narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Abu Hanifah, he is al-Nu'man, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5985","6381 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Amr ibn Damrah al-Fazari, from Abdillah ibn Abi Sulayt, from his father, Abu Sulayt, and he was a Badri, he said: The prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from eating the flesh of donkeys reached us while we were at Khaybar, and the pots were boiling with it, so we overturned them upon their faces."
"5986","6382 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade on the day of Khaybar eating the flesh of domestic donkeys, and permitted the flesh of horses."
"5987","6383 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, (H). 6384 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed us horse flesh, and forbade us donkey flesh."
"5988","6385 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us that Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki informed him that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah saying: We ate during Khaybar horses and wild donkeys, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the domestic donkey."
"5989","6386 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Khalid al-Ahmar informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Jabir, the like of it."
"5990","6387 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah informed us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara, he heard it from him, he said: We obtained donkeys on the day of Khaybar and cooked them, then the caller of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called out to overturn the pots."
"5991","6388 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Adiyy ibn Thabit, from al-Bara and Ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5992","6389 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from Adiyy ibn Thabit, he said: I heard al-Bara and Abdillah ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it, and he did not mention Khaybar."
"5993","6390 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Ibn Abi Awfa, the like of it."
"5994","6391 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, from Ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"5995","6392 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Amr informed us, he said: I said to Jabir ibn Zayd: They claim that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the flesh of domestic donkeys. So he said: Al-Hakam ibn Amr al-Ghifari used to say that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but the learned one rejected that, meaning Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he recited: {Say: I do not find in what was revealed to me anything forbidden to an eater who eats it} the verse."
"5996","6393 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade on the day of Khaybar the flesh of tame donkeys."
"5997","6394 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to me, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"5998","6395 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Khaybar, they obtained donkeys and cooked from them, then the caller of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called out: Verily Allah and His Messenger forbid you from it, for it is impure, so overturn the pots."
"5999","6396 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham, from Muhammad, from Anas, and Ayyub from Muhammad, Hammad said - and I think it from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought news on the day of Khaybar, and it was said to him: The donkeys have been eaten, and he remained silent, then he was brought news and it was said to him: The donkeys have perished, so he commanded Abu Talhah to call out, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6000","6397 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Hisham informed us, from Muhammad, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
